/* What residents know about real-world history and people, for whispers: each entry is a list of names, then sentences (summary, when, details, legacy). Built by nightmare/tools/res_know/build.py. */
window.NV_RES_KNOW={"kb":[{"n":["ancient egypt","egyptians","pharaohs"],"s":["Ancient Egypt was one of the world’s first great civilisations, built along the River Nile in north-east Africa.","It lasted roughly three thousand years, from the unification of Upper and Lower Egypt around 3100 BC until Rome took it over in 30 BC.","Its rulers, the pharaohs, were seen as god-kings who stood between the gods and the people.","The Nile’s yearly flood laid down rich black soil, which let Egyptian farmers grow far more food than they needed.","Egyptians wrote in hieroglyphs, a script of pictures and sound-signs carved on temples and painted on papyrus.","They mummified their dead because they believed the body had to survive for the soul to live on in the afterlife.","History is usually split into the Old, Middle and New Kingdoms, with unstable “intermediate periods” in between.","Famous rulers include Khufu, Hatshepsut, Akhenaten, Tutankhamun, Ramesses II and, at the very end, Cleopatra VII.","Egyptians made paper-like sheets from the papyrus reed.","They worshipped many gods, like Ra, Osiris, Isis and Anubis.","They practised surprisingly advanced medicine, including setting broken bones.","Cats were sacred and sometimes mummified.","The Rosetta Stone, found in 1799, let scholars decipher hieroglyphs.","Jean-François Champollion cracked hieroglyphs in 1822.","Women in Egypt could own property and run businesses.","Egyptians invented an early form of toothpaste.","Pharaohs’ tombs were often robbed in ancient times.","The Great Sphinx is about 73 metres long.","Egyptians used a 365-day year divided into three seasons tied to the Nile.","Egypt gave the world monumental architecture, an early 365-day calendar, advances in medicine and some of the oldest writing on Earth."]},{"n":["pyramids","great pyramid","pyramid of giza","pyramids of giza","giza"],"s":["The Great Pyramid of Giza is the largest of the three pyramids on the Giza plateau, near modern Cairo.","It was built for the pharaoh Khufu around 2560 BC, during Egypt’s Old Kingdom.","It was originally about 146 metres tall and stayed the tallest human-made structure in the world for nearly four thousand years.","It is made of roughly 2.3 million stone blocks, many weighing several tonnes.","Most historians now think it was built by paid, organised work gangs, not slaves, who lived in a town nearby.","Its sides are lined up almost exactly with north, south, east and west.","It is the only one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World still standing.","The Sphinx, with a lion’s body and human head, guards the Giza pyramids.","The pyramids were once covered in shining white limestone.","The pyramids remain the most famous symbol of ancient Egypt and a lasting puzzle about how early societies organised huge projects."]},{"n":["tutankhamun","king tut","tutankhamen"],"s":["Tutankhamun was a young pharaoh of Egypt’s 18th Dynasty, famous today mostly because his tomb was found almost intact.","He ruled from about 1332 to 1323 BC and died at around 19 years old.","He came to the throne as a child, after the religious upheaval of Akhenaten, and his reign restored worship of the old gods.","The British archaeologist Howard Carter discovered his tomb in the Valley of the Kings in November 1922.","The tomb held more than five thousand objects, including his solid gold burial mask.","Stories of a “curse” spread after the expedition’s sponsor, Lord Carnarvon, died in 1923, but there is no evidence for one.","Studies of his mummy suggest he had a club foot and suffered from malaria.","His golden mask weighs about 10 kilograms.","His tomb was small for a pharaoh, perhaps because he died suddenly.","His tomb gave the modern world its clearest picture of royal burial in ancient Egypt and sparked a worldwide craze for Egyptology."]},{"n":["cleopatra","cleopatra vii"],"s":["Cleopatra VII was the last active ruler of Ptolemaic Egypt, a Greek-speaking dynasty founded after Alexander the Great.","She lived from 69 to 30 BC and ruled Egypt from 51 BC until her death.","She was highly educated and was said to speak many languages, and she was the first of her dynasty to learn Egyptian.","She formed alliances, and relationships, with two of Rome’s most powerful men: Julius Caesar and then Mark Antony.","She had a son with Caesar, Caesarion, and three children with Antony.","Antony and Cleopatra were defeated by Octavian at the Battle of Actium in 31 BC.","She died in 30 BC, traditionally by the bite of an asp, and Egypt became a Roman province.","She famously had herself smuggled to Caesar, according to Plutarch, rolled in bedding or a carpet.","She ruled at first alongside her brothers, as was the custom.","She lived closer in time to the Moon landing than to the building of the Great Pyramid.","Ancient writers praised her charm and intelligence more than her looks.","Her son Caesarion was killed on Octavian’s orders.","She commissioned buildings and coins showing herself as the goddess Isis.","Her life has been retold endlessly, from Shakespeare’s Antony and Cleopatra to Hollywood, often more as legend than history."]},{"n":["ramesses ii","ramses ii","ramesses the great","ramses"],"s":["Ramesses II, often called Ramesses the Great, was one of the most powerful and celebrated pharaohs of ancient Egypt.","He ruled for about 66 years, from around 1279 to 1213 BC, during the New Kingdom.","He fought the Hittites at the Battle of Kadesh around 1274 BC, which he presented at home as a great victory.","Later, Egypt and the Hittites signed one of the earliest known peace treaties.","He built enormously, including the temples at Abu Simbel and the Ramesseum.","He is thought to have had more than a hundred children.","Later pharaohs called him “the Great Ancestor”.","His mummy was issued an Egyptian passport when flown to Paris for treatment in 1976.","His monuments made him a byword for royal power, and he is often linked, without firm evidence, to the pharaoh of the Exodus story."]},{"n":["mesopotamia","sumer","sumerians"],"s":["Mesopotamia is the region between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers, mostly in modern Iraq, often called the cradle of civilisation.","Its first cities, like Uruk and Ur, grew up in the south, in Sumer, from around 4000 to 3000 BC.","The Sumerians developed cuneiform, one of the world’s first writing systems, pressing wedge-shaped marks into clay tablets.","They built stepped temple towers called ziggurats.","Mesopotamians used a base-60 number system, which is why we still have 60 minutes in an hour and 360 degrees in a circle.","Later empires there included the Akkadians, Babylonians and Assyrians.","The Epic of Gilgamesh, one of the oldest surviving works of literature, comes from Mesopotamia.","Sargon of Akkad founded perhaps the first empire, around 2300 BC.","Enheduanna, a priestess of Ur, is the first named author in history.","Writing, the wheel, early law codes and city life all have deep roots in Mesopotamia."]},{"n":["hammurabi","code of hammurabi"],"s":["Hammurabi was a king of Babylon best known for his code of laws.","He ruled from about 1792 to 1750 BC.","He expanded Babylon from a small city-state into an empire covering most of Mesopotamia.","His code has 282 laws, carved on a tall black stone pillar now kept in the Louvre in Paris.","It covers trade, property, family life, wages and crime.","It is famous for the principle of “an eye for an eye”, though punishments depended on a person’s social class.","The stele shows Hammurabi receiving the laws from Shamash, the god of justice.","The stele was found in Susa, Iran, in 1901.","It is one of the earliest and most complete written legal codes, and a landmark in the idea that laws should be public."]},{"n":["epic of gilgamesh","gilgamesh"],"s":["The Epic of Gilgamesh is an ancient Mesopotamian poem and one of the oldest surviving great works of literature.","Its earliest versions date from around 2100 BC, with the best-known version from about 1200 BC.","It follows Gilgamesh, a proud king of Uruk, and his friend Enkidu.","After Enkidu dies, Gilgamesh goes on a quest to escape death.","It includes a flood story strikingly similar to that of Noah.","Much of it survives on clay tablets found in the library of the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal at Nineveh.","It remains a powerful story about friendship, grief and accepting that we are mortal."]},{"n":["ancient greece","greeks","ancient greeks","classical greece"],"s":["Ancient Greece was a civilisation of independent city-states around the Aegean Sea, whose ideas shaped much of the Western world.","Its classical age is usually dated to the 5th and 4th centuries BC.","The most famous city-states were Athens, a centre of democracy and philosophy, and Sparta, a militarised society.","The Greeks gave us the Olympic Games, theatre, history writing and much of Western philosophy.","They fought off two Persian invasions, at battles such as Marathon, Thermopylae and Salamis.","Athens and Sparta later fought each other in the long Peloponnesian War.","Greek culture spread across the Mediterranean and Middle East after Alexander the Great’s conquests.","Greek city-states founded colonies around the Mediterranean and Black Sea.","Herodotus, called the “father of history”, wrote about the Persian Wars.","Greek drama produced tragedians like Aeschylus, Sophocles and Euripides.","Greek temples followed the Doric, Ionic and Corinthian styles.","The Greek alphabet was adapted from the Phoenician one.","The Greek world fought amongst itself as much as with outsiders.","Greek doctors followed Hippocrates’ idea that diseases have natural causes.","Mathematicians like Euclid systematised geometry.","Democracy, geometry, drama, medicine and philosophy all owe a great deal to the ancient Greeks."]},{"n":["athens","athenian democracy","democracy in athens"],"s":["Athens was the leading city-state of classical Greece and the birthplace of democracy.","Its democracy began with the reforms of Cleisthenes around 508 BC.","Adult male citizens could vote directly in the Assembly, though women, slaves and foreigners could not.","Many officials were chosen by lot rather than by election.","Under Pericles in the 5th century BC, Athens built the Parthenon on the Acropolis.","It was home to Socrates, Plato, Aristotle and the great playwrights.","Athens lost the Peloponnesian War to Sparta in 404 BC.","Ostracism exiled citizens for ten years after a vote using pottery shards.","The Athenian experiment in rule by citizens is still the starting point for discussions about democracy."]},{"n":["sparta","spartans"],"s":["Sparta was a powerful city-state in southern Greece, famous for its military discipline.","It was at its height between the 6th and 4th centuries BC.","Spartan boys entered a harsh state training system called the agoge at around age seven.","Sparta depended on the labour of helots, a large enslaved population.","Spartan women had more freedom than women in most Greek cities, including owning property.","Three hundred Spartans under King Leonidas made a famous last stand at Thermopylae in 480 BC.","Sparta defeated Athens in the Peloponnesian War, but declined after losing to Thebes at Leuctra in 371 BC.","Spartans were famous for short, blunt speech, which gives us the word “laconic”.","The word “spartan”, meaning plain and tough, comes from their way of life."]},{"n":["thermopylae","battle of thermopylae","300 spartans","leonidas"],"s":["The Battle of Thermopylae was a famous last stand by a small Greek force against the invading Persian army.","It was fought in 480 BC at a narrow coastal pass in central Greece.","King Leonidas of Sparta led about seven thousand Greeks, including his three hundred Spartans.","The Persian army of Xerxes I was vastly larger.","The Greeks held the pass for three days until a local man showed the Persians a path around it.","Leonidas sent most of the army away and stayed to fight with his Spartans and a few allies, who were killed.","Weeks later the Greek navy beat the Persians at Salamis.","When told Persian arrows would blot out the sun, a Spartan reportedly replied, “Then we shall fight in the shade.”","Thermopylae became a lasting symbol of courage against overwhelming odds."]},{"n":["alexander the great","alexander of macedon","alexander iii of macedon"],"s":["Alexander the Great was a king of Macedon who built one of the largest empires of the ancient world.","He lived from 356 to 323 BC and became king at 20, after his father Philip II was murdered.","He was tutored as a boy by the philosopher Aristotle.","He defeated the Persian Empire in a series of battles, including Issus and Gaugamela.","His empire stretched from Greece and Egypt to what is now Pakistan.","He founded many cities, the most famous being Alexandria in Egypt.","He never lost a battle, but his troops refused to go further when they reached India.","He died in Babylon at 32, possibly of illness, and his generals split the empire between them.","His horse was called Bucephalus.","He cut the Gordian Knot rather than untie it.","He married Roxana, a Bactrian noblewoman.","He visited the oracle at Siwa, which hailed him as son of Zeus-Ammon.","His body was taken to Egypt, and the location of his tomb is lost.","He named a city after his horse, Bucephala.","His conquests spread Greek language and culture across the Middle East, beginning the Hellenistic age."]},{"n":["socrates"],"s":["Socrates was an Athenian philosopher, one of the founders of Western philosophy.","He lived from about 470 to 399 BC.","He wrote nothing himself; we know him mostly through his student Plato.","He questioned people relentlessly to expose weak reasoning, a style now called the Socratic method.","He is famous for saying he was wise only because he knew that he knew nothing.","In 399 BC Athens put him on trial for “corrupting the youth” and not honouring the city’s gods.","He was sentenced to death and drank poison hemlock rather than flee.","He served as a soldier in the Peloponnesian War.","He often met people in the agora, Athens’ marketplace.","His wife was Xanthippe.","His idea that “the unexamined life is not worth living” still sits at the heart of philosophy."]},{"n":["plato"],"s":["Plato was an Athenian philosopher, a student of Socrates and the teacher of Aristotle.","He lived from about 428 to 348 BC.","He wrote dialogues, usually starring Socrates, that discuss justice, love, knowledge and the soul.","His best-known work, The Republic, imagines an ideal state ruled by philosopher-kings.","His “allegory of the cave” compares most people to prisoners mistaking shadows for reality.","He founded the Academy in Athens, often called the first university in the Western world.","His theory of Forms says that perfect, unchanging ideas lie behind the imperfect things we see.","His real name may have been Aristocles; “Plato” may have been a nickname meaning “broad”.","He travelled to Sicily to advise rulers, with little success.","The philosopher Alfred North Whitehead said all of Western philosophy is a series of footnotes to Plato."]},{"n":["aristotle"],"s":["Aristotle was a Greek philosopher and scientist, a student of Plato and tutor to Alexander the Great.","He lived from 384 to 322 BC.","He wrote on an astonishing range of subjects: logic, ethics, politics, biology, physics, poetry and more.","He founded his own school in Athens, the Lyceum.","He developed formal logic, including the syllogism.","In biology he carefully observed and classified hundreds of animals.","His ethics focus on virtue and living a flourishing life, which he called eudaimonia.","He collected a library and research materials at the Lyceum.","He believed heavier objects fall faster, an idea Galileo later disproved.","His ideas dominated European and Islamic thought for nearly two thousand years."]},{"n":["pythagoras","pythagorean theorem"],"s":["Pythagoras was a Greek philosopher and mathematician, best known for the theorem named after him.","He lived from about 570 to 495 BC and founded a community in southern Italy.","The Pythagorean theorem says that in a right-angled triangle, a² + b² = c², where c is the longest side.","The relationship was actually known earlier in Babylon and India, but his school is credited with proving it.","His followers believed numbers were the key to understanding the universe.","They also linked mathematics to music, discovering that harmonious notes follow simple number ratios.","His community had strict rules, including, famously, not eating beans.","He reportedly believed in reincarnation.","The theorem is still one of the first proofs students learn, and it underpins geometry, navigation and engineering."]},{"n":["archimedes"],"s":["Archimedes was a Greek mathematician, engineer and inventor from Syracuse in Sicily.","He lived from about 287 to 212 BC.","According to legend, he shouted “Eureka!” in his bath when he realised how to measure volume by displacement.","He worked out the principle of buoyancy, now called Archimedes’ principle.","He estimated the value of pi with remarkable accuracy.","He explained the lever, reportedly saying, “Give me a place to stand and I will move the Earth.”","He designed war machines to defend Syracuse against Rome and was killed when the city fell.","He is said to have been killed while drawing circles, telling a soldier, “Do not disturb my circles.”","He is often ranked among the greatest mathematicians of all time."]},{"n":["homer","iliad","odyssey","the iliad","the odyssey"],"s":["Homer is the legendary Greek poet credited with the Iliad and the Odyssey, the oldest works of Western literature.","The poems are thought to date from the 8th or 7th century BC.","The Iliad tells of the anger of Achilles during the Trojan War.","The Odyssey follows Odysseus on his ten-year journey home from Troy.","They were composed for oral performance and passed down by singers before being written down.","Scholars still debate whether Homer was one person, several people or a tradition.","The Iliad has over 15,000 lines.","Phrases like “Achilles’ heel”, “Trojan horse” and “odyssey” all come from these stories."]},{"n":["trojan war","troy","trojan horse"],"s":["The Trojan War is the legendary war between the Greeks and the city of Troy, told in Homer’s Iliad and later poems.","If it happened, it would date to around the 12th or 13th century BC.","In legend it began when the Trojan prince Paris took Helen, wife of King Menelaus of Sparta.","The Greeks besieged Troy for ten years.","They finally won by hiding soldiers inside a giant wooden horse, which the Trojans pulled into the city.","Archaeologists have found the remains of a real city, Hisarlik in modern Turkey, that matches Troy.","The Iliad covers only a few weeks of the ten-year war.","Whether or not it happened as told, it became one of the founding stories of Western culture."]},{"n":["olympic games","olympics","ancient olympics"],"s":["The Olympic Games began in ancient Greece as a religious festival in honour of Zeus.","The traditional date of the first Games is 776 BC, at Olympia.","They were held every four years, a period the Greeks called an Olympiad.","Events included running, wrestling, boxing, chariot racing and the pentathlon.","Winners received an olive wreath, though their home cities often rewarded them richly.","The ancient Games ended around AD 393.","The Frenchman Pierre de Coubertin revived them, and the first modern Olympics were held in Athens in 1896.","Married women were not allowed to watch the ancient Games.","The marathon was added to the modern Games in 1896.","Today the Olympics are the world’s largest international sporting event."]},{"n":["ancient rome","roman empire","romans","rome"],"s":["Ancient Rome grew from a small city in Italy into an empire that ruled the whole Mediterranean world.","Rome was traditionally founded in 753 BC; the Western Empire fell in AD 476, and the Eastern Empire lasted until 1453.","It was first a kingdom, then a republic from 509 BC, then an empire after Augustus took power in 27 BC.","At its height, around AD 117, it stretched from Britain to Mesopotamia.","Romans were brilliant engineers, building roads, aqueducts, bridges and concrete domes.","Roman law became the foundation of many modern legal systems.","Latin, the Roman language, gave rise to Italian, French, Spanish, Portuguese and Romanian.","Christianity became the empire’s official religion in the late 4th century.","Romans built about 400,000 kilometres of roads.","Their concrete, made with volcanic ash, has lasted two thousand years.","The city of Rome may have had a million people at its height.","Gladiator fights were hugely popular entertainment.","Rome used aqueducts to bring fresh water into cities.","The Pantheon, rebuilt around AD 125, still has the world’s largest unreinforced concrete dome.","Emperor Trajan took the empire to its largest size.","Hadrian built a wall across northern Britain from AD 122.","The empire was split into East and West in 395.","Its army was organised into legions of around 5,000 soldiers.","Wealthy Romans lived in villas with central heating called hypocausts.","Public baths were social hubs.","Latin phrases like “carpe diem” and “et cetera” are still used.","Rome’s population depended on grain shipped from Egypt.","Rome’s laws, languages, roads, calendar and ideas of citizenship still shape Europe and beyond."]},{"n":["roman republic"],"s":["The Roman Republic was the period when Rome was governed by elected officials rather than kings or emperors.","It lasted from about 509 BC to 27 BC.","Power was shared between two consuls elected each year, the Senate and popular assemblies.","Society was divided between patricians, the old aristocracy, and plebeians, the common citizens.","The Republic conquered Italy and defeated Carthage in the Punic Wars.","In its last century it was torn apart by civil wars between ambitious generals.","It ended when Octavian became Augustus, the first emperor.","Roman citizens could appeal to the people against magistrates.","Its ideas, like checks and balances and the Senate, inspired the founders of the United States."]},{"n":["julius caesar","caesar"],"s":["Julius Caesar was a Roman general and statesman whose rise to power ended the Roman Republic.","He lived from 100 to 44 BC.","He conquered Gaul, roughly modern France and Belgium, between 58 and 50 BC.","In 49 BC he crossed the Rubicon river with his army, starting a civil war; “crossing the Rubicon” now means passing a point of no return.","He defeated his rival Pompey and became dictator.","He reformed the calendar into the Julian calendar, the ancestor of the one we use today.","He had a relationship with Cleopatra and a son, Caesarion.","He was stabbed to death in the Senate on the Ides of March, 15 March 44 BC, by senators including Brutus.","Pirates once kidnapped him; he joked he would crucify them, and later did.","He formed the First Triumvirate with Pompey and Crassus in 60 BC.","He wrote his own account of the Gallic Wars.","He was declared “dictator for life” in 44 BC, alarming senators.","His supposed last words, “Et tu, Brute?”, come from Shakespeare.","The month of July is named after him.","His murder led to more civil war, not the return of the Republic.","He was elected chief priest of Rome, pontifex maximus, in 63 BC.","He wrote, “I came, I saw, I conquered” (veni, vidi, vici) after a quick victory in 47 BC.","His name became a title: “Kaiser” and “Tsar” both come from Caesar."]},{"n":["augustus","octavian","caesar augustus"],"s":["Augustus, born Octavian, was the first Roman emperor.","He lived from 63 BC to AD 14 and ruled as emperor from 27 BC.","He was Julius Caesar’s great-nephew and adopted son.","He defeated Mark Antony and Cleopatra at the Battle of Actium in 31 BC.","He kept the outward forms of the Republic while holding real power himself.","His reign began the Pax Romana, about two centuries of relative peace.","He famously said he found Rome a city of brick and left it a city of marble.","The month of August is named after him.","He was only 18 when Caesar was assassinated.","He reorganised the army into a professional force.","He created the system of rule by emperors that lasted for centuries."]},{"n":["punic wars","carthage","hannibal","hannibal barca"],"s":["The Punic Wars were three wars between Rome and Carthage, a powerful trading city in North Africa.","They were fought between 264 and 146 BC.","The first war was fought largely at sea over Sicily, and Rome won.","In the second war, the Carthaginian general Hannibal marched an army with war elephants over the Alps into Italy.","Hannibal crushed the Romans at Cannae in 216 BC, one of history’s most famous battles.","Rome recovered, and Scipio Africanus defeated Hannibal at Zama in 202 BC.","In the third war Rome destroyed Carthage completely in 146 BC.","Hannibal swore as a boy never to be a friend of Rome.","Victory made Rome the dominant power of the Mediterranean, and Hannibal is still studied as a master of strategy."]},{"n":["colosseum","the colosseum","flavian amphitheatre"],"s":["The Colosseum is a huge ancient amphitheatre in the centre of Rome.","It was begun under Emperor Vespasian around AD 72 and finished under Titus in AD 80.","It could hold an estimated 50,000 to 80,000 spectators.","It hosted gladiator fights, animal hunts, executions and public spectacles.","Beneath the arena floor was a network of tunnels and lifts called the hypogeum.","Earthquakes and stone robbers damaged it over the centuries.","It could be covered with a giant awning called the velarium.","It is one of the most recognisable buildings on Earth and a symbol of Rome itself."]},{"n":["pompeii","vesuvius","mount vesuvius"],"s":["Pompeii was a Roman town near Naples that was buried by the eruption of Mount Vesuvius.","The eruption happened in AD 79.","The town was covered in metres of ash and pumice, preserving it remarkably well.","Excavations began in the 18th century.","Archaeologists poured plaster into hollows left by bodies in the ash, creating haunting casts of victims.","The writer Pliny the Younger left an eyewitness account; his uncle, Pliny the Elder, died trying to rescue people.","Nearby Herculaneum was buried too.","Graffiti on Pompeii’s walls includes jokes, love notes and election slogans.","Pompeii offers an unmatched snapshot of everyday Roman life."]},{"n":["nero"],"s":["Nero was a Roman emperor remembered as one of the worst.","He ruled from AD 54 to 68.","He became emperor at 16, after his mother Agrippina is said to have poisoned his predecessor Claudius.","He had his own mother killed in AD 59.","A great fire burned much of Rome in AD 64; the story that he “fiddled while Rome burned” is a legend.","He blamed Christians for the fire and persecuted them.","Facing revolt, he took his own life in AD 68.","He fancied himself a great performer and competed in Greek games.","His name became a byword for cruelty and excess."]},{"n":["marcus aurelius","meditations"],"s":["Marcus Aurelius was a Roman emperor and Stoic philosopher.","He ruled from AD 161 to 180.","He was the last of the “Five Good Emperors”.","He spent much of his reign fighting on the empire’s northern frontier.","He wrote the Meditations, private notes to himself on how to live well.","His Stoic advice focuses on self-control, duty and accepting what we cannot change.","His reign also saw a devastating plague, the Antonine Plague.","He wrote his Meditations in Greek, not Latin.","The Meditations are still widely read as a guide to calm and resilience."]},{"n":["constantine","constantine the great"],"s":["Constantine the Great was the Roman emperor who legalised Christianity.","He ruled from AD 306 to 337.","Before the Battle of the Milvian Bridge in 312, he is said to have seen a sign of the cross in the sky.","In 313 the Edict of Milan granted religious tolerance across the empire.","He called the Council of Nicaea in 325 to settle Christian disputes.","He founded a new capital, Constantinople, on the site of Byzantium in 330.","He was baptised only on his deathbed.","His choices shaped the future of Christianity and of the Eastern Roman Empire."]},{"n":["fall of rome","fall of the roman empire","fall of the western roman empire"],"s":["The fall of the Western Roman Empire was its collapse into separate kingdoms.","It is usually dated to AD 476, when the last emperor in the West, Romulus Augustulus, was deposed.","The causes included civil wars, economic trouble, overstretched borders and pressure from migrating peoples.","Germanic groups such as the Goths and Vandals settled within the empire.","Rome itself was sacked by the Visigoths in 410 and by the Vandals in 455.","The Eastern Empire, centred on Constantinople, survived for nearly a thousand more years.","Edward Gibbon’s The History of the Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire is a famous study of it.","Historians still argue about why Rome fell, and the question shapes how we think about the rise and fall of powers."]},{"n":["byzantine empire","byzantium","eastern roman empire","constantinople"],"s":["The Byzantine Empire was the continuation of the Roman Empire in the east, centred on Constantinople.","It lasted from the fall of the West in 476 until Constantinople fell to the Ottomans in 1453.","Its people called themselves Romans, though they spoke mostly Greek.","Emperor Justinian I reconquered parts of the West and compiled Roman law.","The great church of Hagia Sophia was built under Justinian in 537.","It was a centre of Orthodox Christianity.","It preserved much classical Greek learning that later reached Western Europe.","It used “Greek fire”, a liquid weapon that burned on water.","Its emperors were crowned in Hagia Sophia.","Byzantium bridged the ancient and medieval worlds and shaped Eastern Europe and Russia."]},{"n":["ancient china","china history","chinese history"],"s":["Chinese civilisation is one of the oldest continuous cultures in the world.","Its first well-documented dynasty, the Shang, ruled from around 1600 to 1046 BC.","China was ruled by a long series of dynasties, including the Zhou, Qin, Han, Tang, Song, Ming and Qing.","The Qin dynasty first unified China in 221 BC.","Confucianism, Daoism and later Buddhism deeply shaped Chinese thought.","China gave the world paper, printing, gunpowder and the compass.","The imperial system ended in 1912, when the last emperor, Puyi, abdicated.","The Han dynasty, from 206 BC to AD 220, gives its name to China’s largest ethnic group.","The civil service exam system lasted from the Sui dynasty until 1905.","Chinese astronomers recorded a supernova in 1054.","Chinese silk was so prized in Rome that the Senate tried to ban it.","Chinese writing, philosophy and inventions influenced all of East Asia and beyond."]},{"n":["qin shi huang","first emperor of china","terracotta army"],"s":["Qin Shi Huang was the first emperor of a unified China.","He lived from 259 to 210 BC and declared himself emperor in 221 BC.","He standardised writing, coins, weights, measures and even cart axle widths.","He connected earlier walls into an early version of the Great Wall.","He ordered books burned and scholars persecuted to silence opposition.","He was buried with the Terracotta Army: about eight thousand life-sized clay soldiers.","The army was discovered by farmers digging a well in 1974.","He sought an elixir of immortality and may have died from mercury pills.","His unification created the idea of China as a single state that has lasted ever since."]},{"n":["great wall","great wall of china"],"s":["The Great Wall of China is a series of walls and fortifications built across northern China.","Walls were built from the 7th century BC onwards; most of what survives today dates from the Ming dynasty, 1368 to 1644.","It was built to protect against raids by nomadic peoples from the north.","Including all its branches, it measures more than 21,000 kilometres.","It is made of stone, brick, rammed earth and wood.","Contrary to popular myth, it cannot be seen from space with the naked eye.","Workers who died building it were sometimes buried in it.","Sticky rice was mixed into some of its mortar.","It is one of the most famous structures on Earth and a symbol of China."]},{"n":["confucius","confucianism"],"s":["Confucius was a Chinese teacher and philosopher whose ideas shaped East Asian society for more than two thousand years.","He lived from 551 to 479 BC, in the state of Lu.","He taught that a good society rests on moral leaders, proper relationships and respect for family.","Filial piety, respect for parents and ancestors, was central to his teaching.","His sayings were collected by his students in the Analects.","A version of the “golden rule” appears in his teaching: do not do to others what you would not want done to you.","Confucianism became the official philosophy of the Han dynasty and the basis of China’s civil service exams.","He travelled for years seeking a ruler who would adopt his ideas.","He had about 3,000 students, according to tradition.","His influence on education, government and family life across East Asia is immense."]},{"n":["sun tzu","art of war","the art of war"],"s":["Sun Tzu was a Chinese general and strategist, traditionally credited with The Art of War.","He is said to have lived in the 6th century BC, though some scholars doubt the details.","The Art of War is a short book of military strategy in thirteen chapters.","It stresses deception, flexibility and knowing both yourself and your enemy.","Its most famous idea is that the best victory is won without fighting at all.","It has been studied by generals, business leaders and athletes.","It was first translated into a European language, French, in 1772.","It is still one of the most influential books on strategy ever written."]},{"n":["silk road"],"s":["The Silk Road was a network of trade routes linking China with Central Asia, the Middle East and Europe.","It flourished from around the 2nd century BC, under the Han dynasty, to the 15th century.","Silk, spices, tea, horses, glass and precious metals travelled along it.","Ideas travelled too, including Buddhism, Islam, Christianity, technology and art.","Few traders went the whole way; goods passed through many hands.","Cities like Samarkand and Kashgar grew rich on the trade.","Diseases also spread along it, possibly including the Black Death.","Paper and gunpowder travelled west along it.","The Chinese kept silk-making secret for centuries until silkworm eggs were smuggled out.","It was one of history’s great connectors of cultures."]},{"n":["ancient india","indus valley","indus valley civilisation","harappa","mohenjo daro"],"s":["The Indus Valley Civilisation was one of the world’s earliest urban cultures, in what is now Pakistan and north-west India.","It flourished from about 2600 to 1900 BC.","Its cities, like Harappa and Mohenjo-daro, had planned street grids and advanced drainage systems.","People used standard weights and fired bricks of consistent sizes.","They had a script that has still not been deciphered.","They traded with Mesopotamia.","The civilisation declined, possibly because of climate change and shifting rivers.","It shows that sophisticated city planning existed more than four thousand years ago."]},{"n":["ashoka","asoka"],"s":["Ashoka was an emperor of the Maurya dynasty who ruled most of the Indian subcontinent.","He reigned from about 268 to 232 BC.","After his bloody conquest of Kalinga, he was horrified by the suffering and turned to Buddhism.","He promoted non-violence, tolerance and care for people and animals.","He had his edicts carved on rocks and pillars across his empire.","He sent Buddhist missionaries abroad, helping spread the religion.","His lion capital is now India’s national emblem.","His dharma wheel appears on India’s flag.","He is remembered as one of history’s most remarkable rulers for choosing compassion after conquest."]},{"n":["buddha","siddhartha gautama","gautama buddha","buddhism"],"s":["Siddhartha Gautama, known as the Buddha, was a spiritual teacher whose teachings founded Buddhism.","He probably lived in the 5th century BC in what is now Nepal and northern India.","Tradition says he was a prince who left his comfortable life after seeing old age, sickness and death.","After years of searching he reached enlightenment while meditating under a tree.","He taught the Four Noble Truths: life involves suffering, suffering has a cause, it can end, and there is a path to end it.","That path is the Noble Eightfold Path, a middle way between luxury and harsh self-denial.","Buddhism spread across Asia and today has hundreds of millions of followers.","His teachings were passed down orally before being written.","The Bodhi Tree site at Bodh Gaya is a major pilgrimage destination.","His ideas about mindfulness, compassion and the mind are widely studied today."]},{"n":["persian empire","achaemenid empire","cyrus the great","persia"],"s":["The Achaemenid, or First Persian, Empire was the largest empire the world had yet seen.","It lasted from about 550 to 330 BC.","It was founded by Cyrus the Great, who conquered Media, Lydia and Babylon.","Cyrus allowed exiled Jews in Babylon to return home, and is remembered for tolerance.","Darius I organised the empire into provinces called satrapies and built the Royal Road.","Its kings invaded Greece twice and were defeated.","Alexander the Great conquered it in the 330s BC.","Its Royal Road let messengers cross the empire in about a week.","Persia’s model of a large, diverse, well-run empire influenced many later states."]},{"n":["middle ages","medieval","medieval period","dark ages"],"s":["The Middle Ages is the period of European history between the fall of the Western Roman Empire and the Renaissance.","It is usually dated from about AD 500 to 1500.","It is often split into the Early, High and Late Middle Ages.","Society was organised around feudalism, with lords, vassals and peasants bound by obligations.","The Catholic Church was the most powerful institution in Western Europe.","Castles, cathedrals, monasteries and universities all flourished.","The period saw the Crusades, the Black Death and the Hundred Years’ War.","Historians now avoid calling it the “Dark Ages”, since it saw real advances in learning, art and technology.","Most people were peasants who rarely travelled far from home.","The longbow and the crossbow changed medieval warfare.","Parliaments, universities and many European nations took shape in the Middle Ages."]},{"n":["feudalism","feudal system"],"s":["Feudalism was the system of land and loyalty that organised medieval European society.","It was most common from about the 9th to the 15th century.","A king granted land, called a fief, to nobles in return for military service.","Those nobles granted land to knights, who swore loyalty in return.","Peasants, many of them serfs, worked the land and could not leave it without permission.","Everyone owed something to someone above them.","It declined as towns grew, money replaced service and the Black Death made labour scarce.","In Japan a similar system linked shoguns, daimyo and samurai.","Ideas about land ownership, nobility and contracts of loyalty grew out of it."]},{"n":["charlemagne","charles the great"],"s":["Charlemagne was king of the Franks and the first Holy Roman Emperor.","He lived from about 742 to 814 and was crowned emperor by the Pope on Christmas Day, 800.","He united much of Western Europe, including modern France, Germany and northern Italy.","He encouraged learning, and his court produced the clear handwriting called Carolingian minuscule.","This “Carolingian Renaissance” preserved many classical texts.","Ironically, he struggled to write himself.","His empire was divided among his grandsons in 843.","He kept a wax tablet under his pillow to practise writing.","His capital was Aachen.","He is often called the “Father of Europe”."]},{"n":["vikings","viking","norse"],"s":["The Vikings were seafaring Norse people from Scandinavia.","The Viking Age lasted from about 793, with the raid on Lindisfarne, to around 1066.","They raided, traded and settled across Europe, from Britain and Ireland to Russia and the Mediterranean.","Their longships were fast and shallow enough to sail up rivers.","They settled Iceland, Greenland and parts of Britain and Normandy.","Leif Erikson reached North America around the year 1000, centuries before Columbus.","Real Vikings did not wear horned helmets; that image comes from 19th-century opera.","Viking society included jarls (nobles), karls (free people) and thralls (slaves).","They settled Dublin and York.","They traded as far as Baghdad.","Viking women could own property and divorce.","Most Vikings were farmers who raided only occasionally.","They navigated using the sun, stars and perhaps “sunstones”.","Days of the week like Wednesday and Thursday come from the Norse gods Odin and Thor."]},{"n":["battle of hastings","norman conquest","william the conqueror","1066"],"s":["The Battle of Hastings was fought between the Normans and the English and changed English history.","It took place on 14 October 1066.","Duke William of Normandy claimed the English throne after Edward the Confessor died.","King Harold II had just defeated a Norwegian invasion at Stamford Bridge before marching south.","Harold was killed, traditionally by an arrow in the eye, and the English army broke.","William was crowned King of England on Christmas Day 1066.","The Bayeux Tapestry tells the story in embroidered pictures.","The Normans ordered the Domesday Book, a vast survey of England, in 1086.","Harold’s army fought on foot behind a shield wall.","Norman rule brought French words into English and reshaped its nobility, law and architecture."]},{"n":["magna carta"],"s":["Magna Carta is a charter of rights agreed by King John of England.","It was sealed at Runnymede on 15 June 1215.","Rebel barons forced John to accept limits on his power.","It said that free men could not be punished except by lawful judgement.","The Pope annulled it within months, but it was reissued several times.","Only a few of its clauses are still law in England today.","Four original copies of the 1215 charter survive.","It became a symbol of the idea that even rulers must obey the law, inspiring later constitutions."]},{"n":["crusades","the crusades","crusade"],"s":["The Crusades were a series of religious wars fought mainly between Christians and Muslims over the Holy Land.","The main Crusades took place between 1095 and 1291.","Pope Urban II called the First Crusade in 1095.","The crusaders captured Jerusalem in 1099.","The Muslim leader Saladin retook Jerusalem in 1187.","Richard the Lionheart led the Third Crusade but did not take Jerusalem.","The Fourth Crusade notoriously sacked Christian Constantinople in 1204.","The last crusader stronghold, Acre, fell in 1291.","Military orders like the Knights Templar were founded to protect pilgrims.","The Children’s Crusade of 1212 is mostly legend mixed with real events.","They increased trade and contact between Europe and the Middle East, and left a long legacy of mistrust."]},{"n":["saladin"],"s":["Saladin was a Muslim sultan of Egypt and Syria who retook Jerusalem from the crusaders.","He lived from 1137 to 1193.","He was of Kurdish origin and founded the Ayyubid dynasty.","He defeated the crusaders at the Battle of Hattin in 1187.","He captured Jerusalem the same year, sparing most of its inhabitants.","He fought Richard the Lionheart in the Third Crusade, ending with a truce.","He was famed, even among his enemies, for chivalry and generosity.","He died with little personal wealth, having given most of it away.","He is remembered across the Muslim world and beyond as a model of a just ruler."]},{"n":["richard the lionheart","richard i"],"s":["Richard I, known as the Lionheart, was King of England and a leader of the Third Crusade.","He lived from 1157 to 1199 and ruled from 1189.","He spent only about six months of his reign in England.","He won battles at Acre and Arsuf but did not recapture Jerusalem.","On his way home he was captured and held for a huge ransom.","He died from a crossbow wound while besieging a castle in France.","He spoke French rather than English.","He became a legendary figure of chivalry, appearing in the Robin Hood stories."]},{"n":["black death","bubonic plague","the plague"],"s":["The Black Death was a devastating pandemic of bubonic plague.","It swept through Europe, Asia and North Africa from 1346 to 1353.","It was caused by the bacterium Yersinia pestis, spread by fleas on rats and from person to person.","It killed an estimated 30 to 60 per cent of Europe’s population.","Symptoms included swollen lymph nodes called buboes, fever and blackened skin.","With so many dead, surviving workers could demand better wages.","Some communities blamed and attacked Jewish people.","Plague kept returning to Europe for centuries.","It reached Europe through Crimea and Italian trading ships in 1347.","Doctors wore beaked masks filled with herbs, though mostly in later plagues.","Some groups, called flagellants, whipped themselves to seek forgiveness.","The word “quarantine” comes from the Italian for forty days.","It returned to London in the Great Plague of 1665.","It was one of the deadliest events in human history and reshaped European society."]},{"n":["hundred years war","hundred years’ war"],"s":["The Hundred Years’ War was a series of conflicts between England and France.","It lasted from 1337 to 1453, which is actually 116 years.","It began over English kings’ claims to the French throne.","English longbowmen won famous victories at Crécy, Poitiers and Agincourt.","Joan of Arc helped turn the tide for France in 1429.","France eventually drove the English out of almost all its territory.","It saw the first use of cannons in European battles.","It helped build national identities in both England and France."]},{"n":["joan of arc"],"s":["Joan of Arc was a French peasant girl who led French armies during the Hundred Years’ War.","She lived from about 1412 to 1431.","She said she heard the voices of saints telling her to help the French heir, Charles.","At 17 she helped lift the English siege of Orléans in 1429.","Charles was crowned king at Reims soon after.","She was captured, handed to the English and tried for heresy.","She was burned at the stake in Rouen at 19.","She was declared innocent in 1456 and made a saint in 1920.","She could not read or write.","Her trial records survive and show sharp answers to her judges.","She is a national heroine of France and a symbol of courage."]},{"n":["agincourt","battle of agincourt"],"s":["The Battle of Agincourt was a famous English victory in the Hundred Years’ War.","It was fought on 25 October 1415 in northern France.","Henry V’s small, exhausted army faced a much larger French force.","English longbowmen shot down French knights struggling through mud.","Many French nobles were killed or captured.","The English army may have been outnumbered three or four to one.","Shakespeare made it legendary in Henry V, with the “band of brothers” speech."]},{"n":["genghis khan","chinggis khan","mongol empire","mongols"],"s":["Genghis Khan founded the Mongol Empire, the largest connected land empire in history.","He lived from about 1162 to 1227 and was proclaimed Great Khan in 1206.","His birth name was Temüjin.","He united the feuding tribes of the Mongolian steppe.","His armies of mounted archers conquered northern China, Central Asia and Persia.","He promoted soldiers on merit and protected trade along the Silk Road.","His conquests were extremely violent, and millions died.","After him the empire reached from Korea to Eastern Europe.","His father was poisoned when he was a boy, leaving his family outcast.","He created a law code called the Yassa.","He created a postal relay system, the Yam.","Some genetic studies suggest millions of people today descend from him.","His burial place is unknown.","The Mongols used sophisticated signals and messengers to coordinate armies across huge distances.","Their messengers could cover over 300 kilometres a day by changing horses.","The Mongols connected East and West, and Genghis remains a national hero in Mongolia."]},{"n":["kublai khan"],"s":["Kublai Khan was a grandson of Genghis Khan and the founder of the Yuan dynasty in China.","He lived from 1215 to 1294.","He completed the Mongol conquest of China in 1279.","He made Khanbaliq, modern Beijing, his capital.","Marco Polo described his court in glowing terms.","His invasions of Japan in 1274 and 1281 failed, partly due to storms the Japanese called kamikaze, “divine winds”.","He welcomed foreign merchants and religions to his court.","He was the first non-Han emperor to rule all of China."]},{"n":["marco polo"],"s":["Marco Polo was a Venetian merchant who travelled to China and wrote about it.","He lived from 1254 to 1324.","He set out with his father and uncle in 1271.","He spent about seventeen years in China, serving Kublai Khan.","His book, The Travels of Marco Polo, was dictated in a prison in Genoa.","It amazed Europeans with descriptions of paper money, coal and huge cities.","Some historians question parts of his account.","His book was a medieval bestseller.","His book inspired later explorers, including Christopher Columbus."]},{"n":["islamic golden age","golden age of islam","house of wisdom"],"s":["The Islamic Golden Age was a period of great scientific and cultural flourishing in the Muslim world.","It is usually dated from the 8th to the 13th century.","Baghdad’s House of Wisdom gathered scholars to translate and build on Greek, Persian and Indian works.","Al-Khwarizmi helped found algebra; the word algorithm comes from his name.","Ibn al-Haytham made breakthroughs in optics and the scientific method.","Ibn Sina wrote The Canon of Medicine, used in Europe for centuries.","Hindu-Arabic numerals, including zero, spread to Europe through this world.","The Mongol sack of Baghdad in 1258 is often seen as its end.","Al-Jazari built water clocks and early robots.","It preserved and advanced knowledge that later fuelled Europe’s Renaissance."]},{"n":["muhammad","prophet muhammad","islam"],"s":["Muhammad is regarded by Muslims as the final prophet of God and is the founder of Islam.","He was born in Mecca around AD 570 and died in Medina in 632.","Muslims believe he received revelations from God, which were collected as the Quran.","In 622 he and his followers moved from Mecca to Medina, the Hijra, which begins the Islamic calendar.","He returned to Mecca in 630.","By his death much of Arabia had united under Islam.","Islam spread rapidly afterwards across the Middle East, North Africa and beyond.","The Kaaba in Mecca is Islam’s holiest site.","Islam today is the world’s second-largest religion, with around two billion followers."]},{"n":["ottoman empire","ottomans","ottoman"],"s":["The Ottoman Empire was a powerful Turkish state that ruled much of south-east Europe, the Middle East and North Africa.","It lasted from about 1299 to 1922.","It was founded by Osman I in Anatolia.","Sultan Mehmed II conquered Constantinople in 1453, renaming it Istanbul over time.","It reached its peak under Suleiman the Magnificent in the 16th century.","It besieged Vienna in 1529 and 1683.","It slowly declined and was called “the sick man of Europe”.","It fought on the losing side in the First World War and was dissolved; modern Turkey was founded in 1923.","Its elite soldiers, the Janissaries, were recruited as boys from Christian families.","Its capital, Istanbul, was one of the largest cities in the world.","It was home to many religions and peoples.","Coffee houses spread through the empire in the 16th century.","Its legacy shapes the politics, food and cultures of many countries today."]},{"n":["fall of constantinople","mehmed ii","mehmed the conqueror"],"s":["The Fall of Constantinople was the capture of the Byzantine capital by the Ottoman Turks.","It happened on 29 May 1453.","Sultan Mehmed II besieged the city for nearly two months.","He used enormous cannons to batter the ancient walls.","The last Byzantine emperor, Constantine XI, died fighting.","Hagia Sophia was converted into a mosque.","Mehmed had ships dragged overland to get into the city’s harbour.","It ended the Roman Empire for good and is often used to mark the end of the Middle Ages."]},{"n":["aztecs","aztec empire","tenochtitlan"],"s":["The Aztecs were a people who built a powerful empire in central Mexico.","Their empire flourished from the 14th century until Spain conquered it in 1521.","Their capital, Tenochtitlan, was built on an island in Lake Texcoco, where Mexico City stands today.","It may have had over 200,000 people, larger than most European cities then.","They grew crops on floating gardens called chinampas.","Their religion included human sacrifice to their gods.","Hernán Cortés and his Indigenous allies conquered them, helped by smallpox.","Their main god was Huitzilopochtli, god of the sun and war.","They used cacao beans as money.","The last Aztec emperor before the conquest was Moctezuma II.","Tenochtitlan had aqueducts, markets and canals.","Mexico’s flag still shows the eagle on a cactus from the Aztec founding legend."]},{"n":["maya","mayans","maya civilisation","mayan"],"s":["The Maya were a Mesoamerican civilisation in what is now southern Mexico and Central America.","Their classic period was from about AD 250 to 900, though Maya people still live there today.","They built cities with huge stepped pyramids, such as Tikal, Palenque and Chichén Itzá.","They developed the most complete writing system in the pre-Columbian Americas.","They were superb astronomers and created complex calendars.","They used the concept of zero.","Many southern cities were abandoned around the 9th century, possibly due to drought and war.","They played a ballgame using a heavy rubber ball, often with religious meaning.","Millions of Maya people today keep their languages and traditions alive."]},{"n":["incas","inca empire","machu picchu","inca"],"s":["The Inca Empire was the largest empire in the Americas before Europeans arrived.","It flourished from the early 15th century until the Spanish conquest in 1532.","It stretched along the Andes, from Ecuador to Chile.","Its capital was Cusco, in Peru.","It had a road network of around 40,000 kilometres.","The Incas kept records with knotted strings called quipu.","Machu Picchu, a mountain estate, was built around 1450.","Francisco Pizarro captured the emperor Atahualpa in 1532.","The Inca ruler was called the Sapa Inca.","They had no written language as we know it.","Their stonework fits so tightly a knife blade cannot pass between the stones.","Quechua, the Inca language, is still spoken by millions."]},{"n":["mali empire","mansa musa"],"s":["The Mali Empire was a rich West African empire, and Mansa Musa was its most famous ruler.","Mansa Musa ruled from around 1312 to 1337.","Mali controlled major gold and salt trade routes.","On his pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324, Mansa Musa spent so much gold in Cairo that its value dropped.","He is often called one of the richest people who ever lived.","He built mosques and made Timbuktu a centre of learning.","He returned from Mecca with scholars and architects.","Timbuktu’s libraries held tens of thousands of manuscripts.","He put West Africa on European maps, literally: he appears on the 1375 Catalan Atlas."]},{"n":["samurai"],"s":["The samurai were the warrior class of feudal Japan.","They rose in the 12th century and lasted until they were abolished in the 1870s.","They served lords called daimyo.","Their code of conduct became known as bushido, “the way of the warrior”.","They fought with bows and, famously, curved swords called katana.","Japan was ruled by military leaders called shoguns from 1192 to 1868.","The Meiji Restoration ended samurai privileges.","Some women, called onna-bugeisha, also trained and fought.","Samurai ideals of loyalty and discipline still shape Japanese culture and popular imagination."]},{"n":["renaissance","the renaissance"],"s":["The Renaissance was a cultural movement that revived interest in classical Greek and Roman learning.","It began in Italy in the 14th century and spread across Europe until about the 17th century.","Its name means “rebirth” in French.","It started in wealthy Italian cities such as Florence, supported by families like the Medici.","Humanism, a focus on human potential and classical texts, was at its heart.","Artists used perspective, anatomy and light to make realistic work.","Leonardo da Vinci, Michelangelo and Raphael are its most famous artists.","The printing press helped spread its ideas quickly.","The architect Brunelleschi built the dome of Florence Cathedral, finished in 1436.","Northern Renaissance artists included Albrecht Dürer and Jan van Eyck.","Erasmus was a leading humanist scholar.","The fall of Constantinople sent Greek scholars and texts west.","The word was popularised by 19th-century historians.","It laid the groundwork for modern science, art and thought."]},{"n":["leonardo da vinci","da vinci","leonardo","mona lisa","the last supper"],"s":["Leonardo da Vinci was an Italian painter, inventor and scientist, the ultimate “Renaissance man”.","He lived from 1452 to 1519.","He painted the Mona Lisa and The Last Supper.","The Mona Lisa hangs in the Louvre in Paris and is probably the most famous painting in the world.","His notebooks are full of designs for flying machines, tanks and diving suits.","He dissected bodies to draw remarkably accurate anatomy.","He wrote his notes in mirror writing, right to left.","He left many projects unfinished.","He was born in Vinci, near Florence, the son of a notary.","He trained in the workshop of the artist Verrocchio.","Only about 15 to 20 paintings are firmly attributed to him.","He drew the famous Vitruvian Man around 1490.","He worked for the Duke of Milan as a military engineer.","He spent his last years in France as a guest of King Francis I.","The Mona Lisa was stolen from the Louvre in 1911 and recovered in 1913.","He was left-handed.","He designed a giant crossbow and a robot knight.","He is the model of curiosity across art and science."]},{"n":["michelangelo","sistine chapel","statue of david"],"s":["Michelangelo was an Italian sculptor, painter and architect of the Renaissance.","He lived from 1475 to 1564.","He carved the statue of David from a single block of marble between 1501 and 1504.","He painted the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel in Rome between 1508 and 1512, mostly standing, not lying down.","He later painted The Last Judgement on the chapel’s altar wall.","He designed the dome of St Peter’s Basilica.","He considered himself a sculptor first.","He worked on St Peter’s for free in his old age.","He signed only one work, the Pietà in St Peter’s.","He is often called the greatest artist of all time."]},{"n":["medici","medici family","lorenzo de medici"],"s":["The Medici were a banking family who ruled Florence and became major patrons of the Renaissance.","They were powerful from the 15th to the 18th century.","Cosimo de’ Medici founded their political power in the 1430s.","Lorenzo “the Magnificent” supported artists like Botticelli and the young Michelangelo.","The family produced four popes and two queens of France.","Their bank was one of the most powerful in Europe.","Their coat of arms showed red balls, possibly pills or coins.","Without Medici money, much Renaissance art might never have existed."]},{"n":["printing press","gutenberg","johannes gutenberg"],"s":["Johannes Gutenberg was a German goldsmith who introduced movable-type printing to Europe.","He developed his press around 1440 in Mainz.","He combined metal movable type, oil-based ink and a press adapted from wine presses.","His most famous work is the Gutenberg Bible, printed around 1455.","Movable type had earlier been invented in China and Korea.","By 1500, millions of books had been printed in Europe.","Printing made books cheaper and spread literacy.","About 180 Gutenberg Bibles were printed; around 49 survive.","It powered the Reformation, the Scientific Revolution and the spread of ideas."]},{"n":["reformation","protestant reformation","martin luther","95 theses","ninety-five theses"],"s":["The Reformation was a 16th-century movement that split Western Christianity.","It is usually dated from 1517, when Martin Luther published his Ninety-five Theses.","Luther, a German monk, attacked the sale of indulgences, which promised to reduce punishment for sins.","He argued that people are saved by faith alone and that the Bible is the highest authority.","He translated the Bible into German.","Other reformers included John Calvin and Huldrych Zwingli.","In England, Henry VIII broke from Rome to create the Church of England.","The Catholic Church responded with the Counter-Reformation.","Luther was excommunicated in 1521.","Printing spread Luther’s pamphlets across Germany within weeks.","It created Protestant Christianity and reshaped European politics, often through war."]},{"n":["henry viii"],"s":["Henry VIII was King of England, famous for his six marriages and his break with the Catholic Church.","He lived from 1491 to 1547 and reigned from 1509.","He wanted to annul his marriage to Catherine of Aragon because she had no surviving son.","When the Pope refused, he made himself head of the Church of England in 1534.","His wives’ fates are remembered as “divorced, beheaded, died, divorced, beheaded, survived”.","He closed the monasteries and took their wealth.","His children Edward VI, Mary I and Elizabeth I all ruled after him.","He was a talented musician and may have written songs.","His break with Rome changed England’s religion forever."]},{"n":["elizabeth i","queen elizabeth i","elizabethan"],"s":["Elizabeth I was Queen of England, the last Tudor monarch.","She lived from 1533 to 1603 and reigned from 1558.","She was the daughter of Henry VIII and Anne Boleyn.","She never married and was called the Virgin Queen.","Her navy defeated the Spanish Armada in 1588.","Her reign saw a golden age of literature, including Shakespeare.","She had her cousin Mary, Queen of Scots, executed in 1587.","She spoke several languages, including French, Italian and Latin.","The Elizabethan age is remembered as a high point of English culture and power."]},{"n":["spanish armada"],"s":["The Spanish Armada was a fleet sent by Philip II of Spain to invade England.","It sailed in 1588.","It aimed to overthrow Protestant Elizabeth I.","English ships used fire ships to break up its formation off Calais.","Storms wrecked many ships as they sailed around Scotland and Ireland.","Only about half the fleet returned home.","Commemorative medals said “God blew and they were scattered.”","Its defeat boosted England’s confidence as a naval power."]},{"n":["shakespeare","william shakespeare","hamlet","romeo and juliet","macbeth"],"s":["William Shakespeare was an English playwright and poet, widely seen as the greatest writer in the English language.","He lived from 1564 to 1616, born in Stratford-upon-Avon.","He wrote about 39 plays, 154 sonnets and several long poems.","His tragedies include Hamlet, Macbeth, Othello, King Lear and Romeo and Juliet.","His comedies include A Midsummer Night’s Dream and Twelfth Night.","Many plays were performed at the Globe Theatre in London.","He invented or popularised hundreds of English words and phrases.","His plays are performed more than those of any other playwright.","He married Anne Hathaway when he was 18.","He had three children, including twins.","He was part of the acting company the Lord Chamberlain’s Men, later the King’s Men.","The First Folio, collecting his plays, was published in 1623, after his death.","He left his wife his “second-best bed” in his will.","Some people have doubted he wrote his plays, but most scholars are sure he did.","He died on 23 April, traditionally also said to be his birthday.","Phrases like “to be or not to be” and “break the ice” come from him."]},{"n":["christopher columbus","columbus","1492"],"s":["Christopher Columbus was an Italian explorer whose voyages for Spain began lasting contact between Europe and the Americas.","He lived from 1451 to 1506 and first crossed the Atlantic in 1492.","He was trying to reach Asia by sailing west.","He landed in the Bahamas on 12 October 1492.","His ships were the Niña, the Pinta and the Santa María.","He made four voyages, but died believing he had reached Asia.","His governorship was brutal, and Indigenous peoples suffered terribly.","He died in 1506 still insisting he had reached Asia.","His voyages began the Columbian Exchange, which transformed both hemispheres, and his legacy is hotly debated."]},{"n":["vasco da gama"],"s":["Vasco da Gama was a Portuguese explorer, the first European to reach India by sea.","He lived from about 1460 to 1524 and reached India in 1498.","He sailed around the Cape of Good Hope at the tip of Africa.","He landed at Calicut on India’s west coast.","His route let Portugal build a trading empire in spices.","Scurvy killed many of his crew.","He opened a sea route between Europe and Asia that changed world trade."]},{"n":["magellan","ferdinand magellan","first circumnavigation"],"s":["Ferdinand Magellan was a Portuguese explorer who led the first expedition to sail around the world.","The voyage lasted from 1519 to 1522.","He sailed for Spain with five ships and about 270 men.","He found a passage around South America, now the Strait of Magellan.","He named the Pacific Ocean, meaning “peaceful”.","Magellan was killed in the Philippines in 1521.","Juan Sebastián Elcano led one ship and 18 survivors home.","Antonio Pigafetta kept a diary of the voyage that survives.","The voyage proved how large the Earth really is."]},{"n":["age of exploration","age of discovery"],"s":["The Age of Exploration was a period when European sailors explored and mapped much of the world.","It ran from the 15th to the 17th century.","Portugal and Spain led the way, followed by England, France and the Netherlands.","New ships and navigation tools made long ocean voyages possible.","Explorers sought trade routes to Asia’s spices.","It led to colonisation of the Americas, Africa and Asia.","It caused immense suffering, including the Atlantic slave trade.","It began truly global trade and reshaped the world map."]},{"n":["columbian exchange"],"s":["The Columbian Exchange was the transfer of plants, animals, people and diseases between the Americas and the rest of the world.","It began after 1492.","Potatoes, maize, tomatoes and chocolate went from the Americas to Europe, Africa and Asia.","Horses, cattle, wheat and sugar went the other way.","European diseases like smallpox killed a huge share of Indigenous Americans.","The potato helped Europe’s population grow.","It transformed diets, economies and populations worldwide."]},{"n":["atlantic slave trade","transatlantic slave trade","slave trade","slavery"],"s":["The transatlantic slave trade forcibly transported millions of Africans to the Americas.","It ran from the 16th to the 19th century.","An estimated 12.5 million people were taken from Africa.","Many died on the brutal sea crossing called the Middle Passage.","Enslaved people worked on plantations growing sugar, cotton and tobacco.","Britain abolished the trade in 1807 and slavery in most of its colonies in 1833.","The United States ended slavery in 1865 with the 13th Amendment.","Brazil was the last country in the Americas to abolish slavery, in 1888.","Olaudah Equiano’s autobiography, published in 1789, helped the abolition campaign.","William Wilberforce led the campaign in the British Parliament.","Its effects, including racism and inequality, are still felt today."]},{"n":["copernicus","nicolaus copernicus","heliocentrism"],"s":["Nicolaus Copernicus was a Polish astronomer who proposed that the Earth goes around the Sun.","He lived from 1473 to 1543.","Most people then believed the Earth sat still at the centre of the universe.","His book On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres was published in 1543, the year he died.","He placed the Sun near the centre, with the planets orbiting it.","His model still used perfect circles, so it wasn’t fully accurate.","He was also a church official and an economist.","The shift he started is called the Copernican Revolution."]},{"n":["galileo","galileo galilei"],"s":["Galileo Galilei was an Italian astronomer and physicist, a founder of modern science.","He lived from 1564 to 1642.","He improved the telescope and used it to observe the sky from 1609.","He discovered four large moons of Jupiter, now called the Galilean moons.","He saw that Venus has phases, supporting the idea that planets orbit the Sun.","He studied falling objects and motion.","The Catholic Church tried him in 1633, and he spent his last years under house arrest.","He is said to have muttered “and yet it moves” about the Earth.","He was formally cleared by the Catholic Church in 1992.","Einstein called him the father of modern science."]},{"n":["kepler","johannes kepler"],"s":["Johannes Kepler was a German astronomer who discovered the laws of planetary motion.","He lived from 1571 to 1630.","He showed that planets move in ellipses, not perfect circles.","His three laws describe how planets speed up and slow down.","He used the careful observations of Tycho Brahe.","He also wrote an early science-fiction story about travelling to the Moon.","His laws helped Newton develop the theory of gravity."]},{"n":["scientific revolution"],"s":["The Scientific Revolution was a period when modern science emerged in Europe.","It is usually dated from 1543, with Copernicus, to the late 17th century, with Newton.","People began testing ideas with experiments and mathematics.","Key figures include Copernicus, Galileo, Kepler, Descartes and Newton.","Scientific societies like the Royal Society were founded.","Francis Bacon promoted learning by observation and experiment.","It created the scientific method we still use today."]},{"n":["isaac newton","newton","laws of motion","gravity"],"s":["Sir Isaac Newton was an English mathematician and physicist, one of the most influential scientists ever.","He lived from 1643 to 1727.","He described the law of universal gravitation and three laws of motion.","He set them out in his book Principia, published in 1687.","The story of an apple falling inspiring him may have some truth, but it never hit his head.","He showed that white light is made of a spectrum of colours.","He co-invented calculus, independently of Leibniz.","He built the first practical reflecting telescope.","He did much of his greatest work while Cambridge was closed for plague in 1665 and 1666.","He was Master of the Royal Mint and hunted counterfeiters.","He was President of the Royal Society.","He spent a great deal of time on alchemy and theology.","He said, “If I have seen further, it is by standing on the shoulders of giants.”","He was buried in Westminster Abbey.","He was born prematurely on Christmas Day 1642 (old calendar).","He invented the cat flap, according to a popular story that is probably untrue.","His physics explained the motion of everything from apples to planets for more than two centuries."]},{"n":["thirty years war","thirty years’ war"],"s":["The Thirty Years’ War was a devastating conflict fought mostly in central Europe.","It lasted from 1618 to 1648.","It began as a struggle between Protestant and Catholic states in the Holy Roman Empire.","It grew into a wider power struggle involving France, Sweden and Spain.","Parts of Germany lost a third or more of their population.","The Peace of Westphalia ended it in 1648.","It began with the “Defenestration of Prague”, when officials were thrown from a window.","Westphalia helped establish the modern idea of sovereign states."]},{"n":["louis xiv","sun king","versailles"],"s":["Louis XIV was King of France for 72 years, the longest reign of any European monarch.","He lived from 1638 to 1715 and became king at age four in 1643.","He was called the Sun King.","He ruled as an absolute monarch; “L’État, c’est moi” (“I am the state”) is attributed to him.","He built the vast Palace of Versailles and moved the court there.","He fought many costly wars.","Versailles had about 700 rooms and huge gardens.","He became the model of absolute monarchy across Europe."]},{"n":["english civil war","oliver cromwell","cromwell"],"s":["The English Civil War was fought between supporters of King Charles I and Parliament.","It lasted from 1642 to 1651.","Parliament’s New Model Army won.","Charles I was executed in 1649.","Oliver Cromwell ruled as Lord Protector from 1653 to 1658.","The monarchy was restored under Charles II in 1660.","Supporters of the king were called Cavaliers; Parliament’s supporters were Roundheads.","It established that a monarch could not rule without Parliament."]},{"n":["peter the great"],"s":["Peter the Great was a tsar of Russia who modernised the country.","He lived from 1672 to 1725.","He travelled through Western Europe in disguise to learn shipbuilding.","He founded St Petersburg in 1703 as his new capital.","He built a navy and reformed the army.","He made nobles shave their beards.","He was about two metres tall.","He turned Russia into a major European power."]},{"n":["enlightenment","age of enlightenment","age of reason"],"s":["The Enlightenment was an intellectual movement that championed reason, science and individual rights.","It flourished in Europe in the 17th and 18th centuries.","Thinkers questioned the authority of kings and churches.","Key figures include John Locke, Voltaire, Rousseau, Montesquieu, Kant and Adam Smith.","They promoted ideas like freedom of speech, religious tolerance and separation of powers.","Diderot’s Encyclopédie tried to gather all human knowledge.","Coffee houses and salons were places where Enlightenment ideas spread.","It inspired the American and French revolutions and modern democracy."]},{"n":["john locke"],"s":["John Locke was an English philosopher, often called the father of liberalism.","He lived from 1632 to 1704.","He argued people have natural rights to life, liberty and property.","He said governments get their authority from the consent of the governed.","If a government breaks that trust, people may replace it.","He described the mind at birth as a “blank slate”.","He also wrote about religious tolerance and education.","His ideas strongly influenced the American Declaration of Independence."]},{"n":["voltaire"],"s":["Voltaire was a French writer and philosopher of the Enlightenment.","He lived from 1694 to 1778; his real name was François-Marie Arouet.","He championed freedom of speech, religious tolerance and separation of church and state.","He wrote the satirical novel Candide.","He was imprisoned in the Bastille and exiled for his writing.","He was a wealthy investor as well as a writer.","A famous summary of his views, “I disapprove of what you say, but I will defend to the death your right to say it”, was actually written by a biographer."]},{"n":["rousseau","jean-jacques rousseau"],"s":["Jean-Jacques Rousseau was a Geneva-born philosopher of the Enlightenment.","He lived from 1712 to 1778.","His book The Social Contract begins: “Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains.”","He argued legitimate power comes from the “general will” of the people.","He believed people are naturally good but corrupted by society.","He wrote an influential book on education, Emile.","He inspired the French Revolution and later Romanticism."]},{"n":["adam smith","wealth of nations"],"s":["Adam Smith was a Scottish economist and philosopher, often called the father of modern economics.","He lived from 1723 to 1790.","His book The Wealth of Nations was published in 1776.","He argued that free markets guided by an “invisible hand” can benefit society.","He explained how dividing labour into tasks boosts productivity, using a pin factory example.","He also wrote about morality and sympathy in The Theory of Moral Sentiments.","He appears on the Bank of England £20 note issued from 2007.","His ideas underpin modern capitalism and economics."]},{"n":["american revolution","american revolutionary war","american war of independence"],"s":["The American Revolution was the struggle by thirteen British colonies to win independence.","The war lasted from 1775 to 1783.","Colonists protested taxes passed without their representation in Parliament.","The Boston Tea Party in 1773 was a famous protest.","Fighting began at Lexington and Concord in April 1775.","The Declaration of Independence was adopted on 4 July 1776.","France’s help was crucial, and the British surrendered at Yorktown in 1781.","The Treaty of Paris recognised American independence in 1783.","The Stamp Act of 1765 taxed printed paper in the colonies.","George Washington’s army suffered a harsh winter at Valley Forge in 1777 to 1778.","The victory at Saratoga in 1777 convinced France to join the war.","Many Loyalists stayed loyal to Britain and fled to Canada.","Britain hired German soldiers, often called Hessians.","It created the United States and inspired revolutions elsewhere."]},{"n":["declaration of independence","july 4 1776","fourth of july","independence day"],"s":["The Declaration of Independence announced that the thirteen American colonies were free from Britain.","It was adopted on 4 July 1776.","It was mainly written by Thomas Jefferson.","It says “all men are created equal” with rights to “Life, Liberty and the pursuit of Happiness”.","It listed complaints against King George III.","John Hancock’s large signature made “John Hancock” slang for a signature.","The original document is kept in the National Archives in Washington.","Its words became a reference point for later movements for freedom and equality."]},{"n":["george washington","washington"],"s":["George Washington was the commander of the American army in the Revolution and the first President of the United States.","He lived from 1732 to 1799 and was president from 1789 to 1797.","He led the Continental Army against the British.","He crossed the icy Delaware River on Christmas night, 1776, to win at Trenton.","He presided over the convention that wrote the Constitution.","He voluntarily stepped down after two terms, setting a precedent.","The story of him chopping down a cherry tree is a myth.","He had dentures made from ivory, animal teeth and human teeth, not wood.","He owned a whiskey distillery at Mount Vernon.","He never lived in Washington, D.C.","He is called the “Father of His Country”, and the US capital is named after him."]},{"n":["thomas jefferson","jefferson"],"s":["Thomas Jefferson was a Founding Father, the main author of the Declaration of Independence and the third US President.","He lived from 1743 to 1826 and was president from 1801 to 1809.","He arranged the Louisiana Purchase in 1803, doubling the size of the country.","He sent Lewis and Clark to explore the West.","He founded the University of Virginia.","He enslaved more than 600 people during his life, a stark contradiction with his words on liberty.","He died on 4 July 1826, the same day as John Adams.","He designed his own house, Monticello.","His ideals of liberty and his contradictions both shape American history."]},{"n":["benjamin franklin","franklin"],"s":["Benjamin Franklin was an American statesman, scientist, inventor and writer.","He lived from 1706 to 1790.","He helped draft the Declaration of Independence and the Constitution.","His famous kite experiment explored electricity.","He invented the lightning rod, bifocal glasses and the Franklin stove.","He secured vital French support during the Revolution.","He published Poor Richard’s Almanack.","He founded America’s first public lending library.","He is pictured on the US hundred-dollar bill."]},{"n":["us constitution","united states constitution","constitution","bill of rights"],"s":["The United States Constitution is the supreme law of the US.","It was written in 1787 and took effect in 1789.","It set up three branches of government: legislative, executive and judicial.","It created a system of checks and balances.","The first ten amendments, the Bill of Rights, were added in 1791.","They protect freedoms like speech, religion and the press.","It has been amended 27 times.","Its preamble begins “We the People”.","It is the oldest written national constitution still in use."]},{"n":["french revolution","storming of the bastille","bastille"],"s":["The French Revolution overthrew the monarchy and transformed France.","It began in 1789 and lasted until about 1799.","France was bankrupt, food was expensive, and ordinary people paid most of the taxes.","Crowds stormed the Bastille prison in Paris on 14 July 1789, now France’s national day.","The Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen was issued in 1789.","King Louis XVI and Queen Marie Antoinette were executed in 1793.","During the Reign of Terror, thousands were guillotined.","Napoleon seized power in 1799.","The Estates-General met in May 1789 for the first time since 1614.","The Third Estate declared itself the National Assembly and swore the Tennis Court Oath.","Women marched on Versailles in October 1789 demanding bread.","France introduced the metric system during the Revolution.","It created a new calendar with ten-day weeks, later abandoned.","France went to war with much of Europe from 1792.","The guillotine was promoted as a more humane method of execution.","The Revolution abolished feudal privileges in August 1789.","Its slogan “Liberty, Equality, Fraternity” still inspires movements around the world."]},{"n":["marie antoinette"],"s":["Marie Antoinette was the last queen of France before the Revolution.","She lived from 1755 to 1793.","She was born an Austrian archduchess and married the future Louis XVI.","She became a symbol of royal extravagance.","She almost certainly never said “Let them eat cake”.","She was executed by guillotine in October 1793.","She was 14 when she married.","She remains one of history’s most famous and misunderstood royals."]},{"n":["robespierre","reign of terror","the terror"],"s":["The Reign of Terror was a period of mass executions during the French Revolution, led by Maximilien Robespierre.","It lasted from 1793 to 1794.","The revolutionary government executed suspected enemies.","Around 17,000 people were officially executed, many by guillotine.","Robespierre himself was guillotined in July 1794.","He was nicknamed “the Incorruptible”.","It became a warning about revolutions turning violent."]},{"n":["napoleon","napoleon bonaparte","bonaparte"],"s":["Napoleon Bonaparte was a French military leader who became Emperor of France.","He lived from 1769 to 1821 and crowned himself emperor in 1804.","He was born in Corsica and rose quickly during the French Revolution.","He conquered much of Europe in the Napoleonic Wars.","His Napoleonic Code reformed law and still influences legal systems worldwide.","His invasion of Russia in 1812 was a disaster.","He was exiled to Elba, escaped, and was finally defeated at Waterloo in 1815.","He died in exile on Saint Helena.","He wasn’t especially short; he was about average height for his time.","He married Joséphine de Beauharnais in 1796, and later Marie-Louise of Austria.","He won his most celebrated victory at Austerlitz in 1805.","Britain’s Admiral Nelson destroyed his navy at Trafalgar the same year.","His Grande Armée of about 600,000 men invaded Russia in 1812, and fewer than 100,000 came back.","He reorganised Germany, ending the Holy Roman Empire in 1806.","He sold Louisiana to the United States in 1803.","His escape from Elba led to the “Hundred Days” before Waterloo.","He founded institutions still found in France, like the lycée schools and the Legion of Honour.","He was crowned in Notre-Dame in 1804, taking the crown from the Pope and placing it on his own head.","He hoped to be buried by the Seine; his remains were moved to Les Invalides in Paris in 1840.","He introduced the metric system across conquered territories.","He is still one of the most studied commanders and leaders in history."]},{"n":["waterloo","battle of waterloo"],"s":["The Battle of Waterloo was Napoleon’s final defeat.","It was fought on 18 June 1815, in what is now Belgium.","The Duke of Wellington commanded a British-led allied army.","Prussian troops under Blücher arrived to help decisively.","Napoleon abdicated days later and was exiled.","Wellington called it “the nearest-run thing you ever saw in your life”.","It ended the Napoleonic Wars, and “meeting your Waterloo” now means a final defeat."]},{"n":["industrial revolution"],"s":["The Industrial Revolution was the shift from hand production to machines and factories.","It began in Britain around 1760 and spread to Europe and America in the 19th century.","Coal and steam power drove new machines.","The textile industry was transformed by inventions like the spinning jenny.","James Watt’s improved steam engine was key.","Railways and canals moved goods quickly.","Cities grew rapidly, often with terrible living conditions.","Child labour was common in factories and mines.","Britain had coal, iron, capital and colonies, which helped it industrialise first.","Factory workers often worked 12 to 16 hours a day.","The Luddites smashed machines they feared would take their jobs.","Laws gradually limited child labour and working hours.","Average living standards eventually rose dramatically.","The first factory is often said to be Richard Arkwright’s cotton mill at Cromford, in 1771.","It created the modern economy and changed how most people lived and worked."]},{"n":["james watt","steam engine"],"s":["James Watt was a Scottish engineer who greatly improved the steam engine.","He lived from 1736 to 1819.","His separate condenser, patented in 1769, made steam engines far more efficient.","His engines powered factories, mines and mills.","The unit of power, the watt, is named after him.","He coined the term “horsepower”.","His work powered the Industrial Revolution."]},{"n":["railways","first railway","stephenson","rocket locomotive"],"s":["Railways transformed travel and trade in the 19th century.","The first public steam railway, the Stockton and Darlington, opened in England in 1825.","George Stephenson and his son Robert built the famous locomotive Rocket in 1829.","The Liverpool and Manchester Railway opened in 1830.","Railways spread quickly across Europe and North America.","The US transcontinental railroad was completed in 1869.","Early critics feared people would suffocate at speeds over 30 miles per hour.","Railways created standard time zones and connected whole nations."]},{"n":["simon bolivar","bolivar","bolívar"],"s":["Simón Bolívar was a Venezuelan leader who freed much of South America from Spanish rule.","He lived from 1783 to 1830.","He is called El Libertador, “the Liberator”.","He led independence wars for Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru and Bolivia.","Bolivia is named after him.","He dreamed of a united South America, Gran Colombia, which broke apart.","He was inspired by a trip to Europe and the ideals of the Enlightenment.","He is a national hero across Latin America."]},{"n":["haitian revolution","toussaint louverture"],"s":["The Haitian Revolution was a successful uprising of enslaved people in the French colony of Saint-Domingue.","It lasted from 1791 to 1804.","It was led at first by Toussaint Louverture, a formerly enslaved man.","It defeated French, Spanish and British forces.","Haiti declared independence in 1804.","France demanded huge compensation from Haiti for recognising its independence.","It created the first nation founded by formerly enslaved people and the first Black republic."]},{"n":["queen victoria","victoria","victorian era","victorian"],"s":["Queen Victoria was Queen of the United Kingdom during a period of vast British power.","She lived from 1819 to 1901 and reigned from 1837.","She reigned for 63 years, a record later broken by Elizabeth II.","She married Prince Albert, and they had nine children.","After Albert died in 1861 she wore black for the rest of her life.","She became Empress of India in 1876.","Her children married into many royal families, earning her the nickname “grandmother of Europe”.","She survived several assassination attempts.","She popularised the white wedding dress.","The Victorian era is linked with industry, empire and strict social values."]},{"n":["british empire"],"s":["The British Empire was the largest empire in history.","It peaked in the early 20th century, covering about a quarter of the world’s land.","It was said “the sun never set” on it.","It included India, Canada, Australia and large parts of Africa.","Trade, industry and naval power drove its growth.","It brought exploitation, violence and famine to many places it ruled.","Most colonies gained independence after the Second World War.","The East India Company ruled much of India before the British crown took over in 1858.","The Commonwealth of Nations and the global spread of English are part of its legacy."]},{"n":["american civil war","civil war"],"s":["The American Civil War was fought between the northern states, the Union, and southern states that seceded, the Confederacy.","It lasted from 1861 to 1865.","The central cause was slavery and whether it would expand.","Abraham Lincoln’s election led eleven southern states to secede.","The Battle of Gettysburg in 1863 was a turning point.","About 620,000 to 750,000 soldiers died, more than in any other American war.","The Confederacy surrendered at Appomattox in April 1865.","Slavery was abolished by the 13th Amendment in 1865.","Fort Sumter in South Carolina was where the first shots were fired, in April 1861.","Ulysses S. Grant led the Union to victory and later became president.","Robert E. Lee was the leading Confederate general.","Sherman’s March to the Sea devastated Georgia in 1864.","About 180,000 Black soldiers fought for the Union.","It preserved the Union and ended slavery, but the struggle for equality continued."]},{"n":["abraham lincoln","lincoln","gettysburg address"],"s":["Abraham Lincoln was the 16th President of the United States, who led the country through the Civil War.","He lived from 1809 to 1865 and was president from 1861.","He grew up poor and largely taught himself.","He issued the Emancipation Proclamation in 1863.","His Gettysburg Address called for “government of the people, by the people, for the people”.","He pushed for the 13th Amendment to abolish slavery.","He was assassinated by John Wilkes Booth at Ford’s Theatre in April 1865.","He was largely self-taught and worked as a rail-splitter and shopkeeper.","He became a lawyer in Illinois.","His debates with Stephen Douglas in 1858 made him nationally known.","He was the tallest US president, at about 1.93 metres.","He is on the US five-dollar bill and the penny.","The Lincoln Memorial in Washington was dedicated in 1922.","He was the first president to be photographed at his inauguration.","He is often ranked as one of the greatest American presidents."]},{"n":["harriet tubman"],"s":["Harriet Tubman was an American abolitionist who escaped slavery and helped others escape.","She lived from about 1822 to 1913.","She escaped from Maryland in 1849.","She returned about thirteen times to lead around seventy people to freedom on the Underground Railroad.","She served as a scout and spy for the Union army.","She later campaigned for women’s right to vote.","She is said to have never lost a passenger on the Underground Railroad.","She is an enduring symbol of courage and freedom."]},{"n":["frederick douglass"],"s":["Frederick Douglass was an American abolitionist, writer and speaker who escaped slavery.","He lived from about 1818 to 1895.","He escaped slavery in 1838.","His autobiography, published in 1845, exposed the horrors of slavery.","He became one of the most famous speakers of his age.","He advised Abraham Lincoln.","He became the most photographed American of the 19th century.","He is remembered as one of the great voices for freedom and equality."]},{"n":["meiji restoration","meiji"],"s":["The Meiji Restoration returned power to Japan’s emperor and launched rapid modernisation.","It began in 1868.","It ended the rule of the Tokugawa shoguns.","Japan adopted Western technology, industry and military methods.","The samurai class was abolished.","Within decades, Japan became a major world power.","Japan built its first railway, from Tokyo to Yokohama, in 1872.","It is one of history’s fastest transformations of a nation."]},{"n":["unification of italy","garibaldi","risorgimento"],"s":["The unification of Italy, the Risorgimento, joined separate states into one kingdom.","It took place mainly between 1848 and 1871.","Giuseppe Garibaldi led his “Redshirts” to conquer Sicily and Naples in 1860.","Count Cavour masterminded the diplomacy.","Victor Emmanuel II became the first king of Italy in 1861.","Rome became the capital in 1871.","Garibaldi’s volunteers wore red shirts.","It created the modern Italian state."]},{"n":["unification of germany","bismarck","otto von bismarck"],"s":["Otto von Bismarck was the Prussian statesman who unified Germany.","He lived from 1815 to 1898; Germany was unified in 1871.","He used a series of short wars against Denmark, Austria and France.","Wilhelm I was proclaimed German Emperor at Versailles in 1871.","Bismarck became Germany’s first chancellor.","He introduced early forms of social insurance and pensions.","He said great questions are decided “by iron and blood”.","He was called the “Iron Chancellor”, and his diplomacy shaped Europe for decades."]},{"n":["world war i","world war 1","ww1","wwi","first world war","great war"],"s":["The First World War was a global conflict centred on Europe.","It lasted from 28 July 1914 to 11 November 1918.","It was sparked by the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria-Hungary in Sarajevo in June 1914.","The Allies, including France, Britain, Russia and later the US, fought the Central Powers, led by Germany and Austria-Hungary.","The Western Front became a deadlock of trench warfare.","New weapons included machine guns, poison gas, tanks and aircraft.","About 9 to 10 million soldiers died.","The Treaty of Versailles in 1919 imposed harsh terms on Germany.","Many soldiers expected it to be “over by Christmas” in 1914.","An unofficial Christmas truce took place on parts of the Western Front in 1914.","The Battle of Verdun in 1916 lasted nearly ten months.","Gallipoli, in 1915, was a costly Allied failure in Turkey.","The sinking of the Lusitania in 1915 turned American opinion against Germany.","The US entered the war in April 1917.","The armistice took effect at 11 a.m. on 11 November 1918.","Poppies became a symbol of remembrance.","Around 65 million soldiers were mobilised.","Women’s work in the war helped win them the vote in several countries.","It toppled four empires and set the stage for the Second World War."]},{"n":["franz ferdinand","archduke franz ferdinand","assassination of franz ferdinand","gavrilo princip"],"s":["Archduke Franz Ferdinand was heir to the throne of Austria-Hungary; his assassination triggered the First World War.","He was shot in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914.","The assassin was Gavrilo Princip, a young Bosnian Serb nationalist.","His driver took a wrong turn, stopping right where Princip stood.","His wife, Sophie, was killed too.","Austria-Hungary declared war on Serbia a month later, and alliances pulled in the other powers.","The car he was shot in is now in a Vienna museum.","Two shots in Sarajevo set off one of the deadliest wars in history."]},{"n":["trench warfare","trenches","the somme","battle of the somme"],"s":["Trench warfare defined the Western Front in the First World War.","The Battle of the Somme was fought from July to November 1916.","Soldiers lived in muddy trenches separated by a deadly “no man’s land”.","On the first day of the Somme, the British army suffered about 57,000 casualties.","Over a million men were killed or wounded at the Somme.","Tanks were used for the first time there.","Soldiers suffered from “trench foot” caused by cold, wet conditions.","The Somme became a symbol of the war’s horror and waste."]},{"n":["treaty of versailles","versailles treaty"],"s":["The Treaty of Versailles was the peace settlement that ended the First World War with Germany.","It was signed on 28 June 1919.","Germany had to accept blame for the war.","It lost territory, its colonies and most of its military.","It had to pay huge reparations.","It also created the League of Nations.","It was signed in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles.","Resentment of the treaty helped Hitler rise to power."]},{"n":["russian revolution","october revolution","bolsheviks","bolshevik revolution"],"s":["The Russian Revolution overthrew the tsar and brought the Communist Bolsheviks to power.","It happened in 1917, in two stages: February and October.","Tsar Nicholas II abdicated in March 1917.","In November (October in Russia’s old calendar) Lenin’s Bolsheviks seized power.","A civil war followed until about 1922.","The tsar and his family were executed in 1918.","The Soviet Union was formed in 1922.","The Bolsheviks promised “peace, land and bread”.","The storming of the Winter Palace became a symbol of the October Revolution.","It created the world’s first communist state and shaped the 20th century."]},{"n":["lenin","vladimir lenin"],"s":["Vladimir Lenin was the leader of the Bolshevik Revolution and the founder of the Soviet state.","He lived from 1870 to 1924.","He led the October Revolution of 1917.","He pulled Russia out of the First World War.","His government used violence against opponents during the Civil War.","His body is still displayed in a mausoleum in Moscow.","He returned to Russia in 1917 on a sealed train arranged by Germany.","His ideas, called Leninism, shaped communist movements worldwide."]},{"n":["joseph stalin","stalin"],"s":["Joseph Stalin was the dictator of the Soviet Union for nearly three decades.","He lived from 1878 to 1953 and held power from the mid-1920s until his death.","He forced rapid industrialisation and the collectivisation of farms.","Collectivisation caused famines, including the Holodomor in Ukraine, killing millions.","His Great Purge of the late 1930s killed hundreds of thousands, and millions went to Gulag labour camps.","The USSR helped defeat Nazi Germany in the Second World War at enormous cost.","After the war he built a Soviet bloc in Eastern Europe.","His name means “man of steel”.","He is remembered as one of the most brutal dictators in history."]},{"n":["great depression","wall street crash","1929 crash"],"s":["The Great Depression was the worst economic downturn of the industrialised world.","It began with the Wall Street Crash in October 1929 and lasted through the 1930s.","Stock prices collapsed, and banks failed.","In the US, unemployment reached about 25 per cent.","It spread worldwide through trade and finance.","US President Franklin D. Roosevelt responded with the New Deal.","Hardship helped extremist movements rise in Europe.","Drought turned the American plains into the “Dust Bowl”.","Many unemployed people lived in shanty towns called “Hoovervilles”.","World trade fell by about two-thirds between 1929 and 1934.","It reshaped how governments manage economies."]},{"n":["franklin roosevelt","fdr","franklin d roosevelt","new deal"],"s":["Franklin D. Roosevelt was the 32nd US President, who led the country through the Depression and most of the Second World War.","He lived from 1882 to 1945 and was president from 1933.","He was the only president elected four times.","His New Deal created jobs, banking reforms and Social Security.","He had polio and used a wheelchair, which he mostly kept from public view.","He said, “The only thing we have to fear is fear itself.”","He died in April 1945, weeks before Germany surrendered.","He gave radio talks called “fireside chats”.","He transformed the role of the US government."]},{"n":["world war ii","world war 2","ww2","wwii","second world war"],"s":["The Second World War was the deadliest conflict in human history.","It lasted from 1 September 1939 to 2 September 1945.","It began when Germany invaded Poland.","The Allies, mainly Britain, the Soviet Union, the US and China, fought the Axis: Germany, Italy and Japan.","Nazi Germany conquered much of Europe by 1941.","Japan’s attack on Pearl Harbor in December 1941 brought the US into the war.","An estimated 70 to 85 million people died, most of them civilians.","Germany surrendered in May 1945, and Japan in September after the atomic bombings.","Germany’s “Blitzkrieg” conquered France in about six weeks in 1940.","The Battle of the Atlantic was fought to keep supply ships moving to Britain.","Germany invaded the Soviet Union in June 1941, in Operation Barbarossa.","The Battle of Midway in June 1942 turned the Pacific war against Japan.","Codebreaking at Bletchley Park gave the Allies vital intelligence.","Victory in Europe Day, VE Day, was 8 May 1945.","Women took on huge roles in factories and services.","Germany and Japan were occupied and rebuilt as democracies after the war.","Japan invaded China in 1937, which some count as the start of the war in Asia.","The Lend-Lease program sent American supplies to Britain and the USSR.","The Siege of Leningrad lasted nearly 900 days.","It led to the United Nations, the Cold War and decolonisation."]},{"n":["adolf hitler","hitler","nazi","nazis","nazi germany","third reich"],"s":["Adolf Hitler was the dictator of Nazi Germany, responsible for the Second World War in Europe and the Holocaust.","He lived from 1889 to 1945 and ruled Germany from 1933.","He was born in Austria and fought in the First World War.","He led the Nazi Party, which spread racist, antisemitic and nationalist ideas.","He became chancellor in January 1933 and quickly made himself dictator.","He invaded Poland in 1939, starting the war.","His regime murdered six million Jews and millions of others.","He took his own life in a Berlin bunker in April 1945.","He was rejected twice by an art academy in Vienna.","He tried to seize power in the failed Beer Hall Putsch of 1923.","He wrote Mein Kampf in prison.","The Nazis took power legally, then destroyed democracy from within.","Germany annexed Austria in 1938.","Britain and France tried to appease him at Munich in 1938.","A group of officers tried to assassinate him in July 1944 and failed.","He was named Time magazine’s Man of the Year in 1938, meaning the most newsworthy, not an honour.","His name is a byword for evil, and the horrors of his regime led to modern human rights laws."]},{"n":["holocaust","the holocaust","shoah","auschwitz"],"s":["The Holocaust was the genocide of European Jews by Nazi Germany and its collaborators.","It took place from 1941 to 1945, after years of persecution from 1933.","About six million Jews were murdered, around two-thirds of Europe’s Jewish population.","Roma, disabled people, Poles, Soviet prisoners, gay men and others were also killed.","Victims were shot in mass killings or murdered in death camps.","Auschwitz-Birkenau, in occupied Poland, was the largest camp; over a million people died there.","Allied troops liberated the camps in 1945.","Nazi leaders were tried at Nuremberg.","It was carried out through ghettos, mass shootings and gas chambers.","The Nazis called it the “Final Solution”.","People like Oskar Schindler and Raoul Wallenberg risked their lives to save Jews.","International Holocaust Remembrance Day is 27 January, the day Auschwitz was liberated in 1945.","The Warsaw Ghetto Uprising in 1943 was the largest Jewish revolt against the Nazis.","Survivors and testimony collections keep its memory alive.","It is remembered so that such crimes are never repeated: “Never again”."]},{"n":["anne frank"],"s":["Anne Frank was a Jewish girl whose wartime diary became one of the most famous books in the world.","She lived from 1929 to 1945.","Her family hid from the Nazis in a secret annex in Amsterdam for over two years.","She wrote her diary from 1942 to 1944.","The family was discovered and deported in August 1944.","She died at Bergen-Belsen concentration camp, aged 15.","Her father, Otto, survived and published her diary in 1947.","Her diary was a gift for her 13th birthday.","Her diary puts a human face on the Holocaust for millions of readers."]},{"n":["winston churchill","churchill"],"s":["Winston Churchill was Britain’s Prime Minister during most of the Second World War.","He lived from 1874 to 1965 and was PM from 1940 to 1945 and 1951 to 1955.","He became PM in May 1940, as Germany invaded France.","His speeches rallied Britain: “We shall fight on the beaches” and “their finest hour”.","He worked closely with Roosevelt and, uneasily, Stalin.","He won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1953.","His record on empire and some wartime decisions remain controversial.","He was a war correspondent and was captured in the Boer War, then escaped.","He was a talented painter.","He was voted out in 1945, shortly after victory in Europe.","He is remembered as a great wartime leader."]},{"n":["battle of britain"],"s":["The Battle of Britain was an air campaign in which the RAF defended Britain against the German Luftwaffe.","It was fought from July to October 1940.","Germany needed control of the skies to invade Britain.","Radar gave the RAF a vital advantage.","Pilots came from many countries, including Poland and Czechoslovakia.","Churchill said, “Never was so much owed by so many to so few.”","Women of the Women’s Auxiliary Air Force plotted enemy raids on map tables.","It was Hitler’s first major defeat."]},{"n":["pearl harbor","pearl harbour"],"s":["Pearl Harbor is a US naval base in Hawaii that Japan attacked by surprise.","The attack came on 7 December 1941.","Japanese aircraft struck the US Pacific Fleet.","More than 2,400 Americans were killed.","Eight battleships were damaged or sunk, though US aircraft carriers were at sea.","Roosevelt called it “a date which will live in infamy”.","The USS Arizona memorial stands over the sunken battleship.","It brought the United States into the Second World War."]},{"n":["d-day","d day","normandy landings","operation overlord"],"s":["D-Day was the Allied invasion of Nazi-occupied France.","It took place on 6 June 1944, in Normandy.","It was the largest seaborne invasion in history.","About 156,000 Allied troops landed on five beaches: Utah, Omaha, Gold, Juno and Sword.","It was led by US General Dwight Eisenhower.","Elaborate deception made Germany expect the landing elsewhere.","By August, Paris was liberated.","Paratroopers landed behind enemy lines the night before.","Its code name was Operation Overlord; the naval part was Operation Neptune.","Bad weather delayed it by a day.","The landings were supported by about 7,000 ships and boats.","It opened a second front in Europe and sped Germany’s defeat."]},{"n":["stalingrad","battle of stalingrad"],"s":["The Battle of Stalingrad was a turning point on the Eastern Front.","It lasted from August 1942 to February 1943.","German forces fought their way into the city, street by street.","A Soviet counter-attack encircled the German Sixth Army.","Around 90,000 Germans surrendered.","Total casualties are estimated at nearly two million.","Soldiers fought for individual buildings, like “Pavlov’s House”.","After Stalingrad, Germany was largely on the retreat in the east."]},{"n":["hiroshima","nagasaki","atomic bomb","atomic bombings"],"s":["The US dropped atomic bombs on the Japanese cities of Hiroshima and Nagasaki.","They were dropped on 6 and 9 August 1945.","Hiroshima was hit by a bomb called “Little Boy”, Nagasaki by “Fat Man”.","Together they killed an estimated 110,000 to 210,000 people.","Many survivors, called hibakusha, suffered radiation illness.","Japan announced its surrender on 15 August 1945.","The Hiroshima Peace Memorial, the “A-Bomb Dome”, still stands.","Sadako Sasaki’s paper cranes became a symbol of peace.","They are the only nuclear weapons ever used in war, and they shaped the nuclear age."]},{"n":["manhattan project","oppenheimer","robert oppenheimer"],"s":["The Manhattan Project was the secret US-led program that built the first atomic bombs.","It ran from 1942 to 1946.","J. Robert Oppenheimer led the Los Alamos laboratory in New Mexico.","Britain and Canada contributed.","The first test, Trinity, took place on 16 July 1945.","Oppenheimer later recalled the line, “Now I am become Death, the destroyer of worlds.”","It cost about $2 billion at the time.","It began the nuclear age."]},{"n":["nuremberg trials"],"s":["The Nuremberg trials prosecuted leaders of Nazi Germany for war crimes.","The main trial ran from 1945 to 1946.","Twenty-four leading Nazis were charged.","Twelve were sentenced to death.","They established crimes against humanity as an international legal idea.","Judges came from the US, Britain, France and the Soviet Union.","They became a foundation of international criminal law."]},{"n":["united nations","un"],"s":["The United Nations is an international organisation that promotes peace and cooperation.","It was founded on 24 October 1945.","It replaced the failed League of Nations.","It has 193 member states.","Its Security Council has five permanent members with vetoes: the US, UK, France, Russia and China.","Its headquarters are in New York.","The Universal Declaration of Human Rights was adopted in 1948.","Its first Secretary-General was Trygve Lie of Norway.","It remains the main forum for global diplomacy."]},{"n":["cold war"],"s":["The Cold War was a long rivalry between the United States and the Soviet Union.","It lasted from about 1947 to 1991.","It was “cold” because the two never fought each other directly.","They competed through an arms race, the Space Race and proxy wars.","NATO and the Warsaw Pact were rival military alliances.","The Cuban Missile Crisis of 1962 brought the world close to nuclear war.","The Berlin Wall fell in 1989.","The Soviet Union dissolved in December 1991.","The Truman Doctrine of 1947 pledged to contain communism.","The Berlin Airlift of 1948 to 1949 supplied West Berlin by air.","Both sides built enough nuclear weapons to destroy each other, a situation called mutually assured destruction.","Spies and propaganda were weapons of the Cold War too.","The Soviet invasion of Afghanistan in 1979 renewed tensions.","Arms control treaties slowly limited nuclear weapons.","The Korean and Vietnam wars were proxy conflicts.","The “Iron Curtain” divided Europe, a phrase Churchill popularised in 1946.","It shaped world politics for nearly half a century."]},{"n":["berlin wall","fall of the berlin wall"],"s":["The Berlin Wall divided communist East Berlin from West Berlin.","It stood from 13 August 1961 to 9 November 1989.","East Germany built it to stop people fleeing west.","At least 140 people died trying to cross it.","US President Reagan said in 1987, “Mr Gorbachev, tear down this wall!”","It opened after an East German official mistakenly announced that border crossings were open immediately.","Germany was reunified on 3 October 1990.","East Germany called it the “Anti-Fascist Protection Rampart”.","There were actually two walls with a “death strip” between them.","Pieces of it are now displayed around the world.","David Hasselhoff sang at the wall on New Year’s Eve 1989.","Its fall became a symbol of the end of the Cold War."]},{"n":["cuban missile crisis"],"s":["The Cuban Missile Crisis was a confrontation that brought the US and USSR close to nuclear war.","It lasted thirteen days in October 1962.","US spy planes found Soviet nuclear missiles in Cuba.","President Kennedy ordered a naval “quarantine” of Cuba.","Soviet leader Khrushchev agreed to remove the missiles.","Secretly, the US agreed to remove its missiles from Turkey.","A hotline between Washington and Moscow was set up afterwards.","A Soviet submarine officer, Vasili Arkhipov, refused to approve a nuclear launch during the crisis.","It is often called the closest the world has come to nuclear war."]},{"n":["korean war"],"s":["The Korean War was fought between North and South Korea, with international involvement.","It lasted from 1950 to 1953.","North Korea invaded the South in June 1950.","A UN force led by the US defended the South.","China joined on the North’s side.","It ended in an armistice, not a peace treaty.","About 2.5 million civilians died.","Korea remains divided today."]},{"n":["vietnam war"],"s":["The Vietnam War was fought between communist North Vietnam and South Vietnam, which the US supported.","US involvement grew from the 1950s; combat troops fought from 1965 to 1973.","The North was backed by the USSR and China.","Guerrilla fighters, the Viet Cong, fought in the South.","The Tet Offensive in 1968 shocked the American public.","Large anti-war protests took place in the US.","Saigon fell to the North in April 1975.","Millions of Vietnamese died.","The US dropped more bombs in the Vietnam War than in the whole of the Second World War.","It deeply shaped American politics and its view of war."]},{"n":["mikhail gorbachev","gorbachev"],"s":["Mikhail Gorbachev was the last leader of the Soviet Union.","He lived from 1931 to 2022 and led the USSR from 1985 to 1991.","He introduced glasnost, “openness”, and perestroika, “restructuring”.","He signed arms reduction treaties with the US.","He did not use force to stop Eastern Europe leaving communism in 1989.","He won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1990.","He later appeared in a Pizza Hut advert.","He is praised abroad for ending the Cold War, but viewed more critically in Russia."]},{"n":["mahatma gandhi","gandhi"],"s":["Mahatma Gandhi was the leader of India’s non-violent independence movement against British rule.","He lived from 1869 to 1948.","He trained as a lawyer in London and worked in South Africa for over twenty years.","He developed satyagraha, non-violent resistance.","His Salt March of 1930 protested a British tax on salt.","India won independence in 1947.","He was assassinated by a Hindu nationalist in January 1948.","“Mahatma” is an honorific meaning “great soul”.","He was born in Porbandar, Gujarat.","In South Africa he was thrown off a train for refusing to leave a whites-only carriage.","He lived simply, spinning his own cloth.","He fasted several times to protest violence.","He was nominated for the Nobel Peace Prize five times but never won.","His birthday, 2 October, is the International Day of Non-Violence.","He spent about six years in prison in total.","He inspired civil rights movements worldwide, including Martin Luther King Jr."]},{"n":["indian independence","partition of india","partition"],"s":["India gained independence from Britain, and was partitioned into India and Pakistan.","Independence came on 15 August 1947.","Partition split British India along mostly religious lines.","Around 15 million people were displaced.","Violence killed somewhere between several hundred thousand and two million people.","Jawaharlal Nehru became India’s first prime minister.","Muhammad Ali Jinnah founded Pakistan.","Nehru gave his “Tryst with Destiny” speech at midnight on 14 August 1947.","Partition still shapes relations between India and Pakistan."]},{"n":["martin luther king","martin luther king jr","mlk","i have a dream"],"s":["Martin Luther King Jr. was an American Baptist minister and leader of the civil rights movement.","He lived from 1929 to 1968.","He led the Montgomery bus boycott in 1955 and 1956.","He championed non-violent protest, inspired by Gandhi.","He gave his “I Have a Dream” speech at the March on Washington in August 1963.","He won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1964.","His work helped pass the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965.","He was assassinated in Memphis on 4 April 1968.","He earned a doctorate in theology from Boston University.","He was arrested about 30 times.","He wrote the Letter from Birmingham Jail in 1963.","He led the Selma to Montgomery marches in 1965.","He was 35 when he won the Nobel Peace Prize, then the youngest winner.","His final speech, “I’ve Been to the Mountaintop”, came the night before he died.","His birth name was Michael King; his father changed both their names.","The US honours him with a national holiday each January."]},{"n":["rosa parks"],"s":["Rosa Parks was an American civil rights activist.","She lived from 1913 to 2005.","On 1 December 1955 she refused to give up her bus seat to a white passenger in Montgomery, Alabama.","Her arrest sparked the Montgomery bus boycott.","The boycott lasted over a year and ended segregation on the city’s buses.","She had been an active member of the NAACP for years.","She was awarded the Congressional Gold Medal in 1999.","She is called “the mother of the civil rights movement”."]},{"n":["civil rights movement"],"s":["The civil rights movement was a struggle to end racial segregation and discrimination against Black Americans.","It was at its height from the mid-1950s to the late 1960s.","In 1954 the Supreme Court ruled school segregation unconstitutional in Brown v. Board of Education.","Protests included boycotts, sit-ins, freedom rides and marches.","Leaders included Martin Luther King Jr., Rosa Parks, John Lewis and Malcolm X.","The Civil Rights Act of 1964 banned discrimination.","The Voting Rights Act of 1965 protected voting rights.","Freedom Riders rode buses through the South in 1961 to challenge segregation.","It transformed American law and inspired movements around the world."]},{"n":["malcolm x"],"s":["Malcolm X was an American Muslim minister and human rights activist.","He lived from 1925 to 1965.","He was a powerful spokesman for the Nation of Islam.","He called for Black self-defence and pride.","After a pilgrimage to Mecca in 1964 he softened his views on race.","He was assassinated in February 1965.","The Autobiography of Malcolm X became a classic.","His birth name was Malcolm Little.","He remains a major figure in the history of Black empowerment."]},{"n":["nelson mandela","mandela","apartheid"],"s":["Nelson Mandela was a South African anti-apartheid leader who became the country’s first Black president.","He lived from 1918 to 2013 and was president from 1994 to 1999.","Apartheid was a system of racial segregation in South Africa from 1948 to the early 1990s.","Mandela was imprisoned for 27 years, much of it on Robben Island.","He was released in 1990.","He and President F. W. de Klerk shared the Nobel Peace Prize in 1993.","He promoted reconciliation rather than revenge.","His prison number, 46664, became a symbol of his campaign.","His birthday, 18 July, is Nelson Mandela International Day.","He is a global symbol of forgiveness and the fight for justice."]},{"n":["john f kennedy","jfk","kennedy","kennedy assassination"],"s":["John F. Kennedy was the 35th President of the United States.","He lived from 1917 to 1963 and was president from 1961.","At 43 he was the youngest person elected president.","He faced the Cuban Missile Crisis in 1962.","He set the goal of landing a man on the Moon before the decade was out.","He was assassinated in Dallas on 22 November 1963.","Lee Harvey Oswald was charged, but was himself shot before trial.","He was the first Catholic US president.","His famous line was “Ask not what your country can do for you; ask what you can do for your country.”","His death remains one of the most discussed events in American history."]},{"n":["mao zedong","mao","chairman mao","cultural revolution"],"s":["Mao Zedong was the founder of the People’s Republic of China and its leader until his death.","He lived from 1893 to 1976 and proclaimed the PRC in 1949.","He led Communist forces in the Chinese Civil War.","The Long March of 1934 and 1935 made him a legend.","His Great Leap Forward caused a famine that killed tens of millions.","His Cultural Revolution from 1966 brought persecution and chaos.","His “Little Red Book” of quotations was printed in about a billion copies.","He remains a towering and controversial figure in China."]},{"n":["ronald reagan","reagan"],"s":["Ronald Reagan was the 40th US President, and formerly a Hollywood actor.","He lived from 1911 to 2004 and was president from 1981 to 1989.","He cut taxes and championed free markets.","He survived an assassination attempt in 1981.","He negotiated arms reduction with Gorbachev.","He is seen as one of the most influential modern conservatives."]},{"n":["margaret thatcher","thatcher"],"s":["Margaret Thatcher was the first female Prime Minister of the United Kingdom.","She lived from 1925 to 2013 and was PM from 1979 to 1990.","She was nicknamed the “Iron Lady”.","She privatised many state industries and weakened trade unions.","She led Britain in the Falklands War in 1982.","She trained as a chemist before becoming a lawyer.","She remains one of Britain’s most admired and most divisive leaders."]},{"n":["queen elizabeth ii","elizabeth ii","the queen"],"s":["Elizabeth II was Queen of the United Kingdom and other Commonwealth realms.","She lived from 1926 to 2022 and reigned from 1952.","She reigned for 70 years, the longest of any British monarch.","She served as a mechanic and driver in the Second World War.","Her coronation in 1953 was one of the first to be televised.","She was succeeded by her son, Charles III.","She had corgis as pets throughout her life.","She met 13 US presidents during her reign.","She was a symbol of continuity through enormous change."]},{"n":["barack obama","obama"],"s":["Barack Obama was the 44th President of the United States and the first African American to hold the office.","He was born in 1961 and was president from 2009 to 2017.","He was born in Hawaii.","He won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2009.","His Affordable Care Act expanded health insurance.","He led the US through the aftermath of the 2008 financial crisis.","He was a senator from Illinois before becoming president.","His election was a landmark in American history."]},{"n":["che guevara","che"],"s":["Che Guevara was an Argentine Marxist revolutionary.","He lived from 1928 to 1967.","He trained as a doctor.","He was a key leader of the Cuban Revolution with Fidel Castro.","He was captured and executed in Bolivia in 1967.","A famous photograph of him became a worldwide icon.","He is seen as a hero by some and a ruthless figure by others."]},{"n":["fidel castro","castro","cuban revolution"],"s":["Fidel Castro was the revolutionary leader of Cuba.","He lived from 1926 to 2016 and led Cuba from 1959 to 2008.","His revolution overthrew the dictator Fulgencio Batista in 1959.","He made Cuba a one-party communist state allied with the USSR.","He survived many US assassination attempts.","He is remembered as both a symbol of defiance and an authoritarian ruler."]},{"n":["suffragettes","women’s suffrage","womens suffrage","emmeline pankhurst","votes for women"],"s":["The suffragettes and suffragists campaigned for women’s right to vote.","The movement was strongest from the late 19th century to the 1920s.","New Zealand was the first country to give women the vote, in 1893.","In Britain, Emmeline Pankhurst led the militant Women’s Social and Political Union.","British women over 30 won the vote in 1918, and on equal terms with men in 1928.","American women won the vote with the 19th Amendment in 1920.","Their campaign transformed democracy."]},{"n":["marie curie","curie"],"s":["Marie Curie was a physicist and chemist who pioneered research on radioactivity.","She lived from 1867 to 1934.","She was born in Poland and worked in France.","She discovered the elements polonium and radium with her husband, Pierre.","She was the first woman to win a Nobel Prize.","She is the only person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences: physics and chemistry.","Her notebooks are still too radioactive to handle without protection.","She died of a blood disorder probably caused by radiation exposure.","She coined the word “radioactivity”.","She and Pierre shared the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics with Henri Becquerel.","During the First World War she organised mobile X-ray units, nicknamed “little Curies”.","Her daughter Irène also won a Nobel Prize.","She was the first woman buried on her own merit in Paris’s Panthéon.","She carried test tubes of radium in her pockets.","She opened the door to women in science and to modern nuclear physics."]},{"n":["albert einstein","einstein","relativity","e=mc2","theory of relativity"],"s":["Albert Einstein was a German-born physicist, one of the greatest scientists of all time.","He lived from 1879 to 1955.","In 1905 he published four groundbreaking papers.","His special relativity showed that time and space depend on how fast you move.","It produced the equation E = mc², linking mass and energy.","His general relativity of 1915 explained gravity as the bending of spacetime.","He won the 1921 Nobel Prize for explaining the photoelectric effect.","He fled Nazi Germany and settled in the United States in 1933.","He worked as a patent clerk in Bern when he wrote his famous 1905 papers.","A solar eclipse in 1919 confirmed his prediction that gravity bends light, making him world-famous.","He was offered the presidency of Israel in 1952 and declined.","He wrote to President Roosevelt in 1939 warning that Germany might build an atomic bomb.","He later regretted that letter.","He played the violin and loved sailing.","His brain was preserved for study after his death.","He famously said, “Imagination is more important than knowledge.”","He died in Princeton, New Jersey, in 1955.","GPS satellites have to correct for relativity to work."]},{"n":["charles darwin","darwin","evolution","natural selection","origin of species"],"s":["Charles Darwin was an English naturalist whose theory of evolution by natural selection transformed biology.","He lived from 1809 to 1882.","He sailed around the world on HMS Beagle from 1831 to 1836.","Finches and tortoises on the Galápagos Islands helped shape his ideas.","He published On the Origin of Species in 1859.","Natural selection means individuals better suited to their environment tend to survive and have more offspring.","Alfred Russel Wallace reached similar ideas independently.","He waited about twenty years before publishing.","He studied medicine at Edinburgh but couldn’t stand the sight of surgery.","He suffered from chronic illness for much of his life.","He also wrote The Descent of Man in 1871.","He spent eight years studying barnacles.","He was buried in Westminster Abbey near Isaac Newton.","He married his cousin, Emma Wedgwood.","Evolution is the unifying idea of modern biology."]},{"n":["gregor mendel","mendel","genetics"],"s":["Gregor Mendel was an Austrian monk known as the father of genetics.","He lived from 1822 to 1884.","He bred about 28,000 pea plants in his monastery garden.","He found that traits are passed on in predictable patterns.","He described dominant and recessive traits.","His work was ignored until about 1900.","He later became abbot of his monastery.","His discoveries are the foundation of modern genetics."]},{"n":["dna","double helix","watson and crick","rosalind franklin"],"s":["DNA is the molecule that carries genetic instructions in living things.","Its double-helix structure was described in 1953.","James Watson and Francis Crick published the model.","Rosalind Franklin’s X-ray image, “Photo 51”, was crucial evidence.","Franklin died in 1958 and was not included in the 1962 Nobel Prize.","DNA is made of four bases: A, T, C and G.","The Human Genome Project finished sequencing human DNA in 2003.","If uncoiled, the DNA in one human cell would be about two metres long.","It launched modern molecular biology and genetic medicine."]},{"n":["louis pasteur","pasteur","pasteurisation","pasteurization"],"s":["Louis Pasteur was a French chemist and microbiologist.","He lived from 1822 to 1895.","He showed that germs cause disease and fermentation.","He developed pasteurisation, heating liquids to kill microbes.","He created vaccines for rabies and anthrax.","He disproved the idea that life arises spontaneously.","He was not a medical doctor but a chemist.","His work saved countless lives."]},{"n":["vaccines","edward jenner","smallpox","vaccination"],"s":["Vaccines train the immune system to fight diseases.","Edward Jenner developed the first vaccine, against smallpox, in 1796.","Jenner noticed milkmaids who caught cowpox seemed protected from smallpox.","The word vaccine comes from the Latin vacca, meaning cow.","A global campaign eradicated smallpox, declared in 1980.","It is the only human disease ever fully eradicated.","The first COVID-19 vaccines used new mRNA technology.","Vaccines are among the greatest public health achievements in history."]},{"n":["penicillin","alexander fleming","fleming","antibiotics"],"s":["Penicillin was the first widely used antibiotic.","Alexander Fleming discovered it in 1928.","He noticed mould killing bacteria on a dish left out while he was on holiday.","Howard Florey and Ernst Chain turned it into a usable medicine in the 1940s.","It saved many soldiers in the Second World War.","The three shared the Nobel Prize in 1945.","Overuse of antibiotics has led to resistant bacteria.","Mass production in the US made it widely available by D-Day.","Antibiotics transformed medicine."]},{"n":["florence nightingale","nightingale"],"s":["Florence Nightingale was a British nurse and statistician who founded modern nursing.","She lived from 1820 to 1910.","She led nurses in the Crimean War in the 1850s.","She was called “the Lady with the Lamp” for her night rounds.","She improved hygiene, sharply cutting death rates.","She used pioneering charts to show data.","She invented a type of pie chart called a polar area diagram.","International Nurses Day is held on her birthday, 12 May."]},{"n":["nikola tesla","tesla","alternating current"],"s":["Nikola Tesla was a Serbian-American inventor and electrical engineer.","He lived from 1856 to 1943.","He developed the alternating current (AC) electrical system.","AC won the “war of the currents” against Edison’s direct current.","He invented the induction motor and the Tesla coil.","He experimented with wireless power.","He died poor in New York.","The unit of magnetic flux density, the tesla, is named after him.","Most of the world’s electricity runs on AC."]},{"n":["thomas edison","edison","light bulb","lightbulb"],"s":["Thomas Edison was an American inventor and businessman.","He lived from 1847 to 1931.","He held 1,093 US patents.","He invented the phonograph in 1877.","He developed a practical, long-lasting light bulb in 1879.","He built the first electric power station in New York.","His research lab at Menlo Park was a model for modern labs.","He was partly deaf from childhood.","He helped build the electrified modern world."]},{"n":["alexander graham bell","telephone","graham bell"],"s":["Alexander Graham Bell was a Scottish-born inventor credited with the telephone.","He lived from 1847 to 1922 and patented the telephone in 1876.","His first words over it were, “Mr Watson, come here, I want to see you.”","He also worked as a teacher of deaf people.","Others, like Antonio Meucci and Elisha Gray, worked on similar devices.","He refused to have a telephone in his study, finding it a distraction.","The telephone changed communication forever."]},{"n":["wright brothers","first flight","kitty hawk"],"s":["The Wright brothers, Orville and Wilbur, made the first powered, controlled airplane flight.","It happened on 17 December 1903, at Kitty Hawk, North Carolina.","The first flight lasted 12 seconds and covered 37 metres.","They ran a bicycle shop in Dayton, Ohio.","Their key breakthrough was controlling the plane in the air.","Neither brother finished high school.","Within decades, flight had transformed travel and warfare."]},{"n":["henry ford","ford","model t","assembly line"],"s":["Henry Ford was an American industrialist who made cars affordable.","He lived from 1863 to 1947.","He launched the Model T in 1908.","His moving assembly line, from 1913, cut build time dramatically.","He paid workers $5 a day, high for the time.","He said customers could have any colour “so long as it is black”.","He also spread antisemitic views.","Ford Motor Company was founded in 1903.","Mass production transformed industry worldwide."]},{"n":["stephen hawking","hawking"],"s":["Stephen Hawking was an English theoretical physicist.","He lived from 1942 to 2018.","He was diagnosed with motor neurone disease at 21.","He predicted that black holes give off radiation, now called Hawking radiation.","His book A Brief History of Time sold over 10 million copies.","He communicated using a speech-generating device.","He appeared in The Simpsons and Star Trek.","He made cosmology popular with millions."]},{"n":["periodic table","mendeleev","dmitri mendeleev"],"s":["The periodic table organises the chemical elements.","Dmitri Mendeleev published the first widely recognised version in 1869.","He arranged elements by atomic weight and properties.","He left gaps for elements not yet discovered and predicted their properties.","Today there are 118 known elements.","Element 101 is named mendelevium in his honour.","It is the central map of chemistry."]},{"n":["big bang","origin of the universe"],"s":["The Big Bang theory explains how the universe began expanding from a hot, dense state.","It happened about 13.8 billion years ago.","Georges Lemaître, a Belgian priest and physicist, first proposed it.","Edwin Hubble showed galaxies are moving away from us.","The leftover heat, the cosmic microwave background, was found in 1965.","The name “Big Bang” was coined, half-mockingly, by Fred Hoyle in 1949.","It is the leading scientific explanation of the universe’s origin."]},{"n":["edwin hubble","hubble","hubble telescope","hubble space telescope"],"s":["Edwin Hubble was an American astronomer, and the Hubble Space Telescope is named after him.","He lived from 1889 to 1953; the telescope launched in 1990.","He proved other galaxies exist beyond the Milky Way.","He showed the universe is expanding.","The telescope orbits about 540 kilometres above Earth.","Its mirror had a flaw fixed by astronauts in 1993.","A unit of the universe’s expansion rate, the Hubble constant, is named after him.","Hubble’s images transformed our view of the cosmos."]},{"n":["quantum mechanics","quantum physics","quantum"],"s":["Quantum mechanics is the physics of very small things, like atoms and particles.","It developed in the early 20th century.","Max Planck proposed in 1900 that energy comes in packets called quanta.","Niels Bohr, Werner Heisenberg and Erwin Schrödinger were key figures.","Heisenberg’s uncertainty principle says you can’t know a particle’s exact position and momentum at once.","Schrödinger’s cat is a thought experiment about a cat both alive and dead.","Einstein disliked its randomness, saying God “does not play dice”.","It underlies lasers, computer chips and MRI scanners."]},{"n":["internet","world wide web","tim berners-lee","www"],"s":["The internet is a global network of computers; the World Wide Web is a system of pages that runs on it.","ARPANET began in 1969; the Web was invented in 1989.","ARPANET was funded by the US Department of Defense.","Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn designed TCP/IP, the internet’s core protocols.","Tim Berners-Lee invented the Web at CERN.","He made it free for everyone.","The first message sent over ARPANET, in 1969, was meant to be “LOGIN”, but the system crashed after “LO”.","It changed how billions of people live, work and communicate."]},{"n":["alan turing","turing","enigma","codebreakers"],"s":["Alan Turing was an English mathematician, codebreaker and a founder of computer science.","He lived from 1912 to 1954.","He helped break the German Enigma code at Bletchley Park in the Second World War.","Their work may have shortened the war by years.","He described a theoretical “Turing machine”, the basis of computers.","He proposed the Turing test for machine intelligence.","He was prosecuted for being gay in 1952, and received a royal pardon in 2013.","His story was told in the 2014 film The Imitation Game.","He is now honoured on the UK £50 note."]},{"n":["computer","first computer","computers","ada lovelace","charles babbage"],"s":["Computers have their roots in 19th-century machines and 20th-century electronics.","Charles Babbage designed his Analytical Engine in the 1830s.","Ada Lovelace wrote what is seen as the first computer program for it.","ENIAC, finished in 1945, was an early electronic general-purpose computer.","The transistor was invented in 1947.","Microchips followed in the late 1950s.","Personal computers spread from the late 1970s.","Early computers filled entire rooms.","Computers now run almost everything in modern life."]},{"n":["space race"],"s":["The Space Race was a Cold War competition between the US and the Soviet Union in spaceflight.","It ran from 1957 to about 1975.","The USSR launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, on 4 October 1957.","Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space in 1961.","The US landed Apollo 11 on the Moon in 1969.","The joint Apollo–Soyuz mission in 1975 marked a thaw.","Laika, a Soviet dog, became the first animal to orbit Earth in 1957.","The first American in space was Alan Shepard, in May 1961.","John Glenn became the first American to orbit Earth in 1962.","The Soviet Luna 2 was the first spacecraft to reach the Moon, crashing into it in 1959.","It drove huge advances in technology and science."]},{"n":["sputnik","sputnik 1"],"s":["Sputnik 1 was the first artificial satellite.","The Soviet Union launched it on 4 October 1957.","It was a metal sphere 58 centimetres across with four antennae.","It sent out radio beeps that people around the world could hear.","It burned up in the atmosphere in January 1958.","Its launch led the US to create NASA in 1958.","It shocked the US and started the Space Race."]},{"n":["yuri gagarin","gagarin","first man in space","first person in space"],"s":["Yuri Gagarin was a Soviet pilot and the first human in space.","He flew on 12 April 1961.","His spacecraft, Vostok 1, orbited Earth once in about 108 minutes.","He became a worldwide celebrity.","He died in a jet crash in 1968.","He was 27 years old when he flew.","12 April is celebrated as Cosmonautics Day in Russia."]},{"n":["valentina tereshkova","first woman in space"],"s":["Valentina Tereshkova was a Soviet cosmonaut and the first woman in space.","She flew in June 1963.","She orbited Earth 48 times in Vostok 6.","She had been a textile worker and amateur parachutist.","She later became a politician.","Her call sign was “Chaika”, meaning seagull.","She remains the only woman to have flown a solo space mission."]},{"n":["apollo 11","moon landing","neil armstrong","buzz aldrin","first man on the moon","apollo program","apollo"],"s":["Apollo 11 was the first mission to land humans on the Moon.","Neil Armstrong stepped onto the Moon on 20 July 1969.","The crew were Neil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin and Michael Collins, who stayed in orbit.","Armstrong said, “That’s one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.”","Their lunar module was called Eagle.","About 600 million people watched on TV.","Twelve people walked on the Moon in six Apollo landings, the last in 1972.","Apollo 13 famously suffered an explosion in 1970 but returned safely.","It launched from Kennedy Space Center on 16 July 1969, aboard a Saturn V rocket.","The landing site was the Sea of Tranquility.","Eagle landed with only about 25 seconds of fuel to spare.","Armstrong and Aldrin spent about two and a half hours walking on the surface.","They planted a US flag and left a plaque saying “We came in peace for all mankind”.","They splashed down in the Pacific on 24 July.","The astronauts were quarantined for three weeks afterwards.","The computer guiding Eagle had far less power than a modern calculator.","The Saturn V remains one of the most powerful rockets ever flown.","The Apollo program employed around 400,000 people.","Apollo 8 was the first crewed mission to orbit the Moon, at Christmas 1968.","Its crew took the famous “Earthrise” photograph.","Michael Collins orbited alone, out of radio contact each time he passed behind the Moon.","It is one of humanity’s greatest achievements."]},{"n":["international space station","iss","space station"],"s":["The International Space Station is a large orbiting laboratory.","Construction began in 1998, and people have lived aboard continuously since November 2000.","It was built by the US, Russia, Europe, Japan and Canada.","It orbits Earth about every 90 minutes.","It is about the size of a football pitch.","Astronauts study how space affects the body.","It travels at about 28,000 kilometres per hour.","It is one of the largest international scientific projects ever."]},{"n":["space shuttle","challenger","columbia disaster"],"s":["The Space Shuttle was NASA’s reusable spacecraft.","It flew from 1981 to 2011.","It launched satellites and helped build the ISS.","Challenger broke apart after launch in 1986, killing seven astronauts.","Columbia broke apart on re-entry in 2003, killing seven more.","It flew 135 missions.","The shuttle Enterprise was a test vehicle that never flew in space.","It showed both the promise and the risks of spaceflight."]},{"n":["voyager","voyager 1","voyager 2","golden record"],"s":["The Voyager probes are two NASA spacecraft exploring the outer solar system and beyond.","Both launched in 1977.","They flew past Jupiter and Saturn; Voyager 2 also visited Uranus and Neptune.","Voyager 1 entered interstellar space in 2012.","Each carries a Golden Record with sounds and images of Earth.","Voyager 1 took the “Pale Blue Dot” photo of Earth in 1990.","Their radio signals take many hours to reach Earth.","They are the farthest human-made objects from Earth."]},{"n":["vincent van gogh","van gogh","starry night"],"s":["Vincent van Gogh was a Dutch Post-Impressionist painter.","He lived from 1853 to 1890.","He painted The Starry Night in 1889.","He made about 2,100 artworks in about a decade.","He sold very few paintings in his lifetime.","He struggled with mental illness and famously cut off part of his ear.","He died at 37.","His brother Theo supported him financially and emotionally.","Hundreds of their letters survive.","He painted Sunflowers in a series of works.","He is now one of the most famous and influential artists ever."]},{"n":["picasso","pablo picasso","cubism","guernica"],"s":["Pablo Picasso was a Spanish artist, one of the most influential of the 20th century.","He lived from 1881 to 1973.","He co-founded Cubism with Georges Braque.","His painting Guernica (1937) shows the horror of a bombing in the Spanish Civil War.","He went through a “Blue Period” and a “Rose Period”.","He made tens of thousands of works.","His full name had more than twenty words.","He co-founded collage as an art form.","He changed what art could look like."]},{"n":["impressionism","claude monet","monet"],"s":["Impressionism was an art movement that captured light and everyday moments.","It began in France in the 1860s and 1870s.","Claude Monet’s painting Impression, Sunrise gave it its name.","Artists painted outdoors with quick, visible brushstrokes.","Other Impressionists include Renoir, Degas and Morisot.","Monet painted his water lilies at Giverny.","Critics first used the name as an insult.","It opened the way to modern art."]},{"n":["frida kahlo","kahlo"],"s":["Frida Kahlo was a Mexican painter famous for her self-portraits.","She lived from 1907 to 1954.","A bus accident at 18 left her in pain for life.","She painted 55 self-portraits.","Her work draws on Mexican culture.","She married the muralist Diego Rivera.","Her house in Mexico City, the Blue House, is now a museum.","She is an icon of art, identity and resilience."]},{"n":["rembrandt","the night watch"],"s":["Rembrandt was a Dutch painter of the 17th century.","He lived from 1606 to 1669.","He was a master of light and shadow.","His most famous painting is The Night Watch (1642).","He painted many self-portraits throughout his life.","He went bankrupt despite his fame.","He made almost 100 self-portraits.","He is one of the greatest painters in history."]},{"n":["beethoven","ludwig van beethoven"],"s":["Ludwig van Beethoven was a German composer, one of the greatest in Western music.","He lived from 1770 to 1827.","He bridged the Classical and Romantic eras.","He began losing his hearing in his late twenties.","He composed his Ninth Symphony while almost completely deaf.","Its “Ode to Joy” is now the anthem of the European Union.","His Fifth Symphony opens with four famous notes.","He was born in Bonn and moved to Vienna.","He wrote one opera, Fidelio.","Around 20,000 people attended his funeral.","His music is still among the most performed in the world."]},{"n":["mozart","wolfgang amadeus mozart"],"s":["Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart was an Austrian composer of the Classical era.","He lived from 1756 to 1791.","He was a child prodigy who composed from age five.","He wrote over 600 works.","His operas include The Magic Flute and The Marriage of Figaro.","He died at 35, leaving his Requiem unfinished.","He toured Europe as a child, playing for kings and queens.","He could write music down after hearing it once.","His rivalry with Salieri is mostly a legend.","He wrote his first symphony at about eight years old.","He is one of the most celebrated composers ever."]},{"n":["bach","johann sebastian bach"],"s":["Johann Sebastian Bach was a German composer of the Baroque era.","He lived from 1685 to 1750.","He wrote the Brandenburg Concertos and the Mass in B minor.","He was a brilliant organist.","He had twenty children.","He was more famous as an organist than a composer in his lifetime.","He once walked about 400 kilometres to hear a famous organist play.","Many musicians consider him the greatest composer of all."]},{"n":["the beatles","beatles"],"s":["The Beatles were an English rock band, the best-selling music act in history.","They formed in Liverpool in 1960 and split in 1970.","The members were John Lennon, Paul McCartney, George Harrison and Ringo Starr.","“Beatlemania” swept Britain and the US.","Their albums include Sgt. Pepper’s Lonely Hearts Club Band and Abbey Road.","Lennon and McCartney wrote most of their songs.","They played hundreds of shows in Hamburg before becoming famous.","Their last public performance was on the roof of Apple Corps in London in 1969.","John Lennon was murdered in New York in 1980.","Their 1964 appearance on The Ed Sullivan Show drew about 73 million viewers.","They transformed popular music and youth culture."]},{"n":["elvis presley","elvis"],"s":["Elvis Presley was an American singer known as the “King of Rock and Roll”.","He lived from 1935 to 1977.","His hits include Heartbreak Hotel and Hound Dog.","He starred in 31 films.","He served in the US Army in Germany.","He lived at Graceland in Memphis.","He was a twin; his brother died at birth.","He is one of the best-selling solo artists ever."]},{"n":["michael jackson"],"s":["Michael Jackson was an American singer and dancer called the “King of Pop”.","He lived from 1958 to 2009.","He began in the Jackson 5 as a child.","His album Thriller (1982) is often cited as the best-selling album of all time.","He made the moonwalk famous.","He won 13 Grammy Awards.","His Thriller video is one of the most famous music videos ever.","His music videos changed pop culture."]},{"n":["jane austen","pride and prejudice"],"s":["Jane Austen was an English novelist.","She lived from 1775 to 1817.","Her novels include Pride and Prejudice, Sense and Sensibility and Emma.","She wrote with wit about marriage, money and class.","Her books were published anonymously.","She was buried in Winchester Cathedral.","She is one of the most loved writers in English."]},{"n":["charles dickens","dickens","a christmas carol","oliver twist"],"s":["Charles Dickens was an English novelist of the Victorian era.","He lived from 1812 to 1870.","His novels include Oliver Twist, A Christmas Carol, Great Expectations and David Copperfield.","As a boy he worked in a factory while his father was in debtors’ prison.","His books exposed poverty and injustice.","Many were published in instalments.","He gave hugely popular public readings of his work.","He created characters like Scrooge who are still famous."]},{"n":["tolstoy","leo tolstoy","war and peace"],"s":["Leo Tolstoy was a Russian writer, one of the greatest novelists.","He lived from 1828 to 1910.","He wrote War and Peace and Anna Karenina.","War and Peace is set during Napoleon’s invasion of Russia.","He later championed non-violence, influencing Gandhi.","He is often ranked among the greatest authors ever."]},{"n":["george orwell","orwell","1984","animal farm"],"s":["George Orwell was an English writer known for warning against totalitarianism.","He lived from 1903 to 1950; his real name was Eric Blair.","He wrote Animal Farm (1945) and Nineteen Eighty-Four (1949).","Nineteen Eighty-Four gave us “Big Brother” and “thought police”.","He fought in the Spanish Civil War.","He was shot in the throat in the Spanish Civil War and survived.","“Orwellian” describes oppressive, controlling governments."]},{"n":["tolkien","j r r tolkien","lord of the rings","the hobbit"],"s":["J. R. R. Tolkien was an English writer and scholar, author of The Hobbit and The Lord of the Rings.","He lived from 1892 to 1973.","He was a professor of Old English at Oxford.","The Hobbit came out in 1937.","The Lord of the Rings was published in 1954 and 1955.","He invented whole languages for his Elves.","He was a close friend of C. S. Lewis, author of Narnia.","He is called the father of modern fantasy."]},{"n":["homer simpson","the simpsons"],"s":["The Simpsons is an American animated sitcom created by Matt Groening.","It first aired as a series in December 1989.","It follows Homer, Marge, Bart, Lisa and Maggie in Springfield.","It is the longest-running American scripted primetime series.","It is famous for satire and for seeming to predict future events."]},{"n":["dante","divine comedy","dante alighieri"],"s":["Dante Alighieri was an Italian poet, author of the Divine Comedy.","He lived from about 1265 to 1321.","The Divine Comedy follows Dante through Hell, Purgatory and Paradise.","He wrote in Italian rather than Latin.","He was exiled from Florence.","He is called the father of the Italian language."]},{"n":["cervantes","don quixote"],"s":["Miguel de Cervantes was a Spanish writer, author of Don Quixote.","He lived from 1547 to 1616.","Don Quixote was published in two parts, in 1605 and 1615.","It follows a man who reads so many romances he decides to become a knight.","He tilts at windmills thinking they are giants.","It is often called the first modern novel."]},{"n":["united states","usa","america","the us","united states of america"],"s":["The United States is a federal republic of 50 states in North America.","It declared independence from Britain on 4 July 1776.","Its capital is Washington, D.C., and its largest city is New York.","It has about 330 million people, the third-largest population in the world.","It expanded west across the continent in the 19th century.","It became a superpower after the Second World War.","Its economy is the largest in the world by nominal GDP.","Its flag has 50 stars, one for each state, and 13 stripes for the original colonies.","Alaska was bought from Russia in 1867.","Hawaii became the 50th state in 1959.","Its national motto is “In God We Trust”.","Its culture, technology and politics influence the whole world."]},{"n":["united kingdom","uk","britain","great britain","england"],"s":["The United Kingdom is a country made up of England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland.","The Kingdom of Great Britain was formed in 1707; Ireland joined in 1801, and most of it left in 1922.","Its capital is London.","It is a constitutional monarchy with a parliamentary democracy.","It led the Industrial Revolution and once ruled the largest empire in history.","It left the European Union in 2020.","English, its main language, is spoken worldwide.","Its flag, the Union Jack, combines the crosses of England, Scotland and Ireland.","Parliament sits in the Palace of Westminster.","It has no single written constitution.","Its parliament, law and language have shaped many countries."]},{"n":["france","french"],"s":["France is a country in Western Europe.","Its modern republic dates back to the Revolution of 1789; the current Fifth Republic began in 1958.","Its capital is Paris.","It has about 68 million people.","It is famous for its food, wine, fashion and art.","The Eiffel Tower was built for the 1889 World’s Fair.","It was a founding member of the European Union.","It is one of the most visited countries in the world."]},{"n":["germany","german"],"s":["Germany is a country in central Europe.","It was first unified in 1871, divided after 1945, and reunified on 3 October 1990.","Its capital is Berlin.","It has about 84 million people, the most in the EU.","It has Europe’s largest economy.","It is known for engineering, cars and beer.","It was split into East and West during the Cold War.","It rebuilt itself into a stable democracy after the Second World War."]},{"n":["italy","italian"],"s":["Italy is a country in southern Europe, shaped like a boot.","It was unified in 1861 and became a republic in 1946.","Its capital is Rome.","It was the heart of the Roman Empire and the birthplace of the Renaissance.","It has more UNESCO World Heritage Sites than almost any other country.","Vatican City, the world’s smallest country, lies inside Rome.","Its food, art and history are loved worldwide."]},{"n":["spain","spanish"],"s":["Spain is a country in south-west Europe, on the Iberian Peninsula.","It was unified in the late 15th century.","Its capital is Madrid.","Muslim rulers controlled much of it for centuries until 1492.","It built a huge empire in the Americas.","It had a civil war from 1936 to 1939, followed by Franco’s dictatorship until 1975.","Spanish is spoken by around 500 million people.","It is a major centre of art, from Velázquez to Picasso."]},{"n":["russia","russian federation"],"s":["Russia is the largest country in the world by area.","The Russian state traces back to the 9th century; the Russian Federation formed in 1991.","Its capital is Moscow.","It spans eleven time zones, across Europe and Asia.","It was ruled by tsars until 1917, then was the core of the Soviet Union.","It has about 145 million people.","Lake Baikal, in Siberia, is the world’s deepest lake.","It has produced great writers, composers and scientists."]},{"n":["china","people’s republic of china","prc"],"s":["China is a country in East Asia with one of the world’s oldest civilisations.","The People’s Republic was founded on 1 October 1949.","Its capital is Beijing, and its largest city is Shanghai.","It has about 1.4 billion people.","It is the world’s second-largest economy.","It is ruled by the Communist Party.","Its economic reforms began under Deng Xiaoping from 1978.","It is now a global power in trade and technology."]},{"n":["japan","japanese"],"s":["Japan is an island country in East Asia.","It has had an emperor for over 1,500 years.","Its capital is Tokyo, one of the largest cities in the world.","It is made up of four main islands and thousands of smaller ones.","It modernised rapidly after 1868.","It rebuilt after the Second World War into a major economy.","It is famous for technology, anime and its cuisine.","It blends ancient tradition with cutting-edge modern life."]},{"n":["india","indian"],"s":["India is a country in South Asia, the world’s most populous nation.","It became independent from Britain in 1947 and a republic in 1950.","Its capital is New Delhi.","It has over 1.4 billion people.","It is the world’s largest democracy.","Hundreds of languages are spoken there, with Hindi and English widely used.","It is the birthplace of Hinduism, Buddhism, Jainism and Sikhism.","Its culture, food and film industry reach worldwide."]},{"n":["brazil","brazilian"],"s":["Brazil is the largest country in South America.","It became independent from Portugal in 1822.","Its capital is Brasília; its largest cities are São Paulo and Rio de Janeiro.","Its official language is Portuguese.","It has about 215 million people.","Most of the Amazon rainforest is in Brazil.","It has won the football World Cup five times, more than any other country.","Its music, from samba to bossa nova, is loved worldwide."]},{"n":["canada","canadian"],"s":["Canada is a country in North America, the second-largest in the world by area.","It became a self-governing dominion in 1867.","Its capital is Ottawa.","Its official languages are English and French.","It has about 40 million people.","It has the world’s longest coastline.","Its head of state is the British monarch.","It is known for its natural beauty and multicultural society."]},{"n":["australia","australian"],"s":["Australia is a country and continent in the southern hemisphere.","Aboriginal Australians have lived there for at least 50,000 years; it federated in 1901.","Its capital is Canberra.","British colonisation began in 1788.","It has about 26 million people.","It is home to kangaroos, koalas and the Great Barrier Reef.","Most people live near the coast.","It has the world’s oldest continuous cultures."]},{"n":["egypt modern","modern egypt","egypt"],"s":["Egypt is a country in North Africa and the Middle East.","Its modern state became independent from Britain in 1922.","Its capital is Cairo.","It has over 100 million people.","Most Egyptians live along the Nile.","The Suez Canal opened in 1869.","Its ancient monuments attract visitors from all over the world."]},{"n":["mexico","mexican"],"s":["Mexico is a country in North America.","It became independent from Spain in 1821.","Its capital is Mexico City.","It has about 130 million people.","It was home to the Olmec, Maya and Aztec civilisations.","Chocolate, chilli and maize all come from Mexico.","Its culture, food and Day of the Dead festival are famous worldwide."]},{"n":["south africa"],"s":["South Africa is the southernmost country in Africa.","It became a democracy in 1994, after apartheid.","It has three capital cities: Pretoria, Cape Town and Bloemfontein.","It has 12 official languages.","Nelson Mandela was its first democratically elected president.","It is called the “Rainbow Nation” for its diversity."]},{"n":["israel"],"s":["Israel is a country in the Middle East.","It declared independence on 14 May 1948.","It was founded as a Jewish state after the Holocaust.","Its creation led to war with neighbouring Arab states.","The Israeli–Palestinian conflict has continued since.","Jerusalem is holy to Jews, Christians and Muslims."]},{"n":["south korea","korea"],"s":["South Korea is a country in East Asia, on the southern half of the Korean Peninsula.","It was founded in 1948.","Its capital is Seoul.","Korea was divided after the Second World War.","It grew from poverty to a rich, high-tech economy.","It is known for K-pop, film and companies like Samsung.","Its rapid growth is called the “Miracle on the Han River”."]},{"n":["north korea"],"s":["North Korea is a country on the northern half of the Korean Peninsula.","It was founded in 1948.","Its capital is Pyongyang.","It has been ruled by the Kim family since its founding.","It is one of the most isolated countries in the world.","It has developed nuclear weapons.","It remains technically at war with South Korea."]},{"n":["turkey","türkiye"],"s":["Turkey is a country spanning south-east Europe and western Asia.","The Republic was founded in 1923 by Mustafa Kemal Atatürk.","Its capital is Ankara, and its largest city is Istanbul.","It was the heart of the Ottoman Empire.","Atatürk modernised the country and introduced the Latin alphabet.","It bridges Europe and Asia in culture and geography."]},{"n":["iran"],"s":["Iran is a country in western Asia, formerly known as Persia.","The Islamic Republic was established after the 1979 revolution.","Its capital is Tehran.","It has one of the world’s oldest civilisations.","Persian, or Farsi, is its main language.","Persian poetry, art and architecture are world-famous."]},{"n":["ireland","irish"],"s":["Ireland is an island in the North Atlantic, mostly occupied by the Republic of Ireland.","Most of Ireland became independent from the UK in 1922.","Its capital is Dublin.","The Great Famine of 1845 to 1852 killed about a million people.","Millions emigrated, especially to the US.","Northern Ireland remains part of the UK.","Irish literature and music are famous worldwide."]},{"n":["greece modern","modern greece","greece"],"s":["Greece is a country in south-east Europe.","It won independence from the Ottoman Empire in 1830.","Its capital is Athens.","It has thousands of islands.","It is the birthplace of democracy and the Olympics.","Its ancient ruins attract millions of visitors."]},{"n":["netherlands","holland","dutch"],"s":["The Netherlands is a country in north-western Europe.","It became independent from Spain in the 17th century.","Its capital is Amsterdam.","About a quarter of it lies below sea level.","It built dykes and windmills to drain land.","It had a golden age of trade and art in the 1600s.","It is famous for tulips, bicycles and canals."]},{"n":["switzerland","swiss"],"s":["Switzerland is a landlocked country in central Europe.","It traces its founding to 1291.","Its capital is Bern.","It has four national languages.","It has stayed neutral in wars for centuries.","It is famous for watches, chocolate and banks.","It is known for direct democracy, with frequent national votes."]},{"n":["nigeria"],"s":["Nigeria is a country in West Africa, the most populous in Africa.","It became independent from Britain in 1960.","Its capital is Abuja, and its largest city is Lagos.","It has over 200 million people.","It has hundreds of ethnic groups.","Its film industry, Nollywood, is one of the largest in the world.","It is a major cultural and economic power in Africa."]},{"n":["argentina"],"s":["Argentina is a country in South America.","It declared independence from Spain in 1816.","Its capital is Buenos Aires.","It is famous for tango, beef and football.","It has won the World Cup three times.","It stretches from the tropics to near Antarctica."]},{"n":["titanic","rms titanic","sinking of the titanic"],"s":["The RMS Titanic was a British passenger liner that sank on her maiden voyage.","She hit an iceberg late on 14 April 1912 and sank early on 15 April.","She was sailing from Southampton to New York.","She was the largest ship afloat at the time and was called “practically unsinkable”.","There were lifeboats for only about half the people aboard.","More than 1,500 people died.","The wreck was found in 1985, about 3,800 metres down.","She had a swimming pool, a gym and a Turkish bath.","Her band reportedly kept playing as she sank.","The nearby ship Carpathia rescued about 700 survivors.","The film Titanic (1997) became one of the highest-grossing films ever.","Survivors included Violet Jessop, who also survived the sinking of her sister ship Britannic.","She was built in Belfast by Harland and Wolff.","Her loss led to new safety rules, including lifeboats for everyone."]},{"n":["chernobyl","chernobyl disaster"],"s":["The Chernobyl disaster was the worst nuclear power accident in history.","It happened on 26 April 1986, in Soviet Ukraine.","A reactor exploded during a safety test.","Radioactive material spread across Europe.","The nearby town of Pripyat was evacuated and remains abandoned.","Thirty-one people died in the immediate aftermath, and many more later from radiation.","A large exclusion zone still surrounds the site.","Wildlife has returned to the exclusion zone.","It changed attitudes to nuclear safety worldwide."]},{"n":["9/11","september 11","september 11 attacks","twin towers","world trade center"],"s":["The September 11 attacks were terrorist attacks on the United States by al-Qaeda.","They happened on 11 September 2001.","Nineteen hijackers took over four passenger planes.","Two hit the Twin Towers of the World Trade Center in New York, which collapsed.","One hit the Pentagon near Washington.","Passengers fought back on the fourth plane, which crashed in Pennsylvania.","Nearly 3,000 people were killed.","The US launched a war in Afghanistan soon after.","The 9/11 Memorial in New York has two pools where the towers stood.","They reshaped security, travel and world politics."]},{"n":["moon landing hoax"],"s":["The Moon landings were real.","Six Apollo missions landed between 1969 and 1972.","Astronauts brought back 382 kilograms of Moon rock.","Mirrors left on the Moon are still used to bounce lasers from Earth.","Independent countries, including the USSR, tracked the missions.","Spacecraft orbiting the Moon have photographed the landing sites."]},{"n":["covid","covid-19","coronavirus","pandemic","covid 19"],"s":["COVID-19 is a disease caused by the SARS-CoV-2 coronavirus that caused a global pandemic.","It was first identified in late 2019, and the WHO declared a pandemic in March 2020.","Countries introduced lockdowns, masks and social distancing.","Vaccines were developed in under a year.","Millions of people died worldwide.","The WHO ended the global health emergency in May 2023.","It changed how people work, travel and think about public health."]},{"n":["spanish flu","1918 flu","1918 pandemic"],"s":["The 1918 influenza pandemic, often called the Spanish flu, was one of the deadliest in history.","It spread from 1918 to 1920.","It infected about a third of the world’s population.","It killed an estimated 50 million people.","Unusually, it killed many healthy young adults.","It was called “Spanish” because neutral Spain reported on it freely.","It shaped modern public health."]},{"n":["great fire of london"],"s":["The Great Fire of London destroyed much of the medieval city.","It burned from 2 to 6 September 1666.","It began in a bakery on Pudding Lane.","It destroyed over 13,000 houses and St Paul’s Cathedral.","Surprisingly few deaths were recorded.","Christopher Wren rebuilt St Paul’s and many churches.","It led to new building rules using brick and stone."]},{"n":["fall of the soviet union","collapse of the soviet union","soviet union","ussr"],"s":["The Soviet Union was a communist state that existed from 1922 to 1991.","It dissolved on 26 December 1991.","It was made up of 15 republics, with Russia the largest.","It was a superpower rival to the United States in the Cold War.","Its economy stagnated in the 1970s and 1980s.","Gorbachev’s reforms loosened control.","Its republics declared independence one after another in 1991.","Its collapse ended the Cold War."]},{"n":["european union","eu"],"s":["The European Union is a political and economic union of European countries.","It grew from the European Coal and Steel Community of 1951 and became the EU in 1993.","It has 27 member states.","Many members share the euro currency, introduced in 2002.","People and goods can move freely between most members.","The UK left in 2020.","It won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2012.","It was created to make war between European nations unthinkable."]},{"n":["brexit"],"s":["Brexit was the United Kingdom’s departure from the European Union.","The referendum was held on 23 June 2016, and the UK left on 31 January 2020.","About 52 per cent voted to leave.","Negotiations took years.","The UK and EU agreed a trade deal in December 2020.","It remains one of the most debated decisions in modern British politics."]},{"n":["arab spring"],"s":["The Arab Spring was a wave of protests and uprisings across the Arab world.","It began in Tunisia in December 2010 and spread in 2011.","It started after a street vendor, Mohamed Bouazizi, set himself on fire in protest.","Leaders fell in Tunisia, Egypt, Libya and Yemen.","Syria descended into a long civil war.","Its outcomes ranged from democracy in Tunisia to war and repression elsewhere."]},{"n":["2008 financial crisis","financial crisis","great recession"],"s":["The 2008 financial crisis was the worst global economic crisis since the Great Depression.","It peaked in 2008.","It began with risky mortgage lending in the United States.","The investment bank Lehman Brothers collapsed in September 2008.","Governments bailed out banks.","Unemployment rose sharply worldwide.","It led to tighter banking rules."]},{"n":["chinese civil war"],"s":["The Chinese Civil War was fought between the Nationalists and the Communists.","It was fought mainly from 1927 to 1949.","The Nationalists were led by Chiang Kai-shek.","The Communists were led by Mao Zedong.","It paused when both fought Japan.","The Communists won, and the Nationalists retreated to Taiwan.","It created the People’s Republic of China."]},{"n":["spanish civil war","franco","francisco franco"],"s":["The Spanish Civil War was fought between Republicans and Nationalists.","It lasted from 1936 to 1939.","The Nationalists were led by General Francisco Franco.","Nazi Germany and Fascist Italy supported Franco.","International volunteers fought for the Republic.","The bombing of Guernica became a symbol of its horror.","Franco ruled Spain as a dictator until his death in 1975.","It was a rehearsal for the Second World War."]},{"n":["rwandan genocide","rwanda"],"s":["The Rwandan genocide was the mass murder of Tutsi people in Rwanda.","It happened over about 100 days from April to July 1994.","Around 800,000 people, mostly Tutsi, were killed.","Many killers were ordinary neighbours, urged on by radio propaganda.","The world largely failed to intervene.","The Rwandan Patriotic Front ended it.","It is remembered as a terrible failure of the world to act."]},{"n":["gulf war"],"s":["The Gulf War was fought by a US-led coalition against Iraq after it invaded Kuwait.","Iraq invaded in August 1990; the war ran from January to February 1991.","Saddam Hussein’s forces occupied Kuwait.","A coalition of 35 countries was formed.","The ground war lasted about 100 hours.","It was the first war widely broadcast live on TV."]},{"n":["iraq war"],"s":["The Iraq War began with a US-led invasion of Iraq.","It began in March 2003, and US forces withdrew in 2011.","The invasion overthrew Saddam Hussein.","Claims that Iraq had weapons of mass destruction proved false.","A long insurgency followed.","Hundreds of thousands of people died.","It remains highly controversial."]},{"n":["afghanistan war","war in afghanistan"],"s":["The war in Afghanistan followed the September 11 attacks.","It lasted from 2001 to 2021.","A US-led coalition toppled the Taliban government.","Osama bin Laden was killed in Pakistan in 2011.","The Taliban returned to power when US forces withdrew in August 2021.","It was America’s longest war."]},{"n":["french and indian war","seven years war","seven years’ war"],"s":["The Seven Years’ War was a global conflict between Britain and France and their allies.","It lasted from 1756 to 1763.","In North America it was called the French and Indian War.","Britain won Canada from France.","It left Britain with large debts.","Britain’s efforts to tax the colonies to pay for it helped spark the American Revolution."]},{"n":["opium wars"],"s":["The Opium Wars were two wars between China and Western powers, mainly Britain.","They were fought from 1839 to 1842 and 1856 to 1860.","Britain was selling opium to China against Chinese law.","China lost both wars.","Hong Kong was ceded to Britain.","China was forced to open ports to foreign trade.","China remembers this as the start of its “century of humiliation”."]},{"n":["hong kong"],"s":["Hong Kong is a city on China’s southern coast.","It was a British colony from 1842 until 1997.","It returned to China under “one country, two systems”.","It is a major financial centre.","It has one of the world’s most famous skylines.","Its status and freedoms remain a major political issue."]},{"n":["suez canal"],"s":["The Suez Canal is a waterway in Egypt linking the Mediterranean and the Red Sea.","It opened in 1869.","It lets ships avoid sailing around Africa.","It was built by a French company led by Ferdinand de Lesseps.","Egypt nationalised it in 1956, triggering the Suez Crisis.","In 2021 the ship Ever Given blocked it for six days.","It is one of the world’s most important trade routes."]},{"n":["panama canal"],"s":["The Panama Canal is a waterway connecting the Atlantic and Pacific oceans.","It opened in 1914.","France first tried to build it in the 1880s and failed.","The US completed it.","Thousands of workers died, many from disease.","Locks lift ships about 26 metres.","Panama took full control in 1999.","It transformed global shipping."]},{"n":["gold rush","california gold rush"],"s":["The California Gold Rush was a mass migration after gold was found in California.","It began in 1848.","Around 300,000 people rushed there.","They were called “forty-niners”.","San Francisco grew from a small town to a city.","Native Americans suffered terribly.","It sped California to statehood in 1850."]},{"n":["wild west","american frontier","cowboys"],"s":["The Wild West refers to the American frontier in the late 19th century.","It is usually associated with roughly 1865 to 1895.","Settlers, ranchers and miners pushed west.","Cowboys drove cattle to railways.","Native American nations were forced onto reservations.","Famous figures include Wild Bill Hickok, Calamity Jane and Billy the Kid.","It became a lasting myth in films and books."]},{"n":["salem witch trials"],"s":["The Salem witch trials were a series of witchcraft prosecutions in colonial Massachusetts.","They took place in 1692 and 1693.","Over 200 people were accused.","Nineteen were hanged, and one man was pressed to death with stones.","Several accusers were young girls.","Massachusetts later apologised.","They became a lesson about hysteria and injustice."]},{"n":["boston tea party"],"s":["The Boston Tea Party was a protest against British taxation.","It took place on 16 December 1773.","Colonists dumped 342 chests of tea into Boston Harbor.","They protested the Tea Act and “taxation without representation”.","Britain responded with harsh laws.","It helped lead to the American Revolution."]},{"n":["john adams"],"s":["John Adams was the second President of the United States.","He lived from 1735 to 1826 and was president from 1797 to 1801.","He was a leading voice for independence.","He was the first president to live in the White House.","His son, John Quincy Adams, became the sixth president.","He died on 4 July 1826, the same day as Jefferson."]},{"n":["andrew jackson"],"s":["Andrew Jackson was the seventh President of the United States.","He lived from 1767 to 1845 and was president from 1829 to 1837.","He became a hero after the Battle of New Orleans in 1815.","He championed the “common man”.","He signed the Indian Removal Act, leading to the Trail of Tears.","He is pictured on the US twenty-dollar bill."]},{"n":["theodore roosevelt","teddy roosevelt"],"s":["Theodore Roosevelt was the 26th President of the United States.","He lived from 1858 to 1919 and was president from 1901 to 1909.","At 42 he became the youngest person to hold the office.","He protected huge areas of land as national parks and forests.","He won the Nobel Peace Prize for helping end the Russo-Japanese War.","The teddy bear is named after him.","He is carved on Mount Rushmore."]},{"n":["woodrow wilson"],"s":["Woodrow Wilson was the 28th President of the United States.","He lived from 1856 to 1924 and was president from 1913 to 1921.","He led the US into the First World War in 1917.","He proposed the Fourteen Points for peace.","He championed the League of Nations, though the US never joined.","He also re-segregated federal offices."]},{"n":["harry truman","truman"],"s":["Harry S. Truman was the 33rd President of the United States.","He lived from 1884 to 1972 and was president from 1945 to 1953.","He took office when Roosevelt died in April 1945.","He ordered the atomic bombings of Japan.","He launched the Marshall Plan to rebuild Europe.","He kept a sign on his desk: “The buck stops here.”"]},{"n":["dwight eisenhower","eisenhower","ike"],"s":["Dwight D. Eisenhower was a Second World War general and the 34th US President.","He lived from 1890 to 1969 and was president from 1953 to 1961.","He was Supreme Allied Commander for D-Day.","He ended the Korean War.","He built the Interstate Highway System.","He warned against the “military-industrial complex”."]},{"n":["richard nixon","nixon","watergate"],"s":["Richard Nixon was the 37th US President, the only one to resign.","He lived from 1913 to 1994 and was president from 1969 to 1974.","He opened relations with China, visiting in 1972.","He ended US involvement in Vietnam.","The Watergate scandal began with a break-in at Democratic offices in 1972.","Facing impeachment, he resigned in August 1974."]},{"n":["lyndon johnson","lbj"],"s":["Lyndon B. Johnson was the 36th US President.","He lived from 1908 to 1973 and was president from 1963 to 1969.","He took office after Kennedy was assassinated.","He signed the Civil Rights Act and the Voting Rights Act.","His “Great Society” created Medicare and Medicaid.","He escalated the Vietnam War."]},{"n":["bill clinton","clinton"],"s":["Bill Clinton was the 42nd President of the United States.","He was born in 1946 and was president from 1993 to 2001.","His presidency saw strong economic growth and a budget surplus.","He was impeached in 1998 but acquitted by the Senate.","He helped broker peace efforts in Northern Ireland and the Middle East."]},{"n":["george w bush","george bush"],"s":["George W. Bush was the 43rd President of the United States.","He was born in 1946 and was president from 2001 to 2009.","He led the US response to the September 11 attacks.","He launched wars in Afghanistan and Iraq.","His father, George H. W. Bush, was the 41st president."]},{"n":["charles de gaulle","de gaulle"],"s":["Charles de Gaulle was a French general and statesman.","He lived from 1890 to 1970.","He led the Free French against Nazi Germany from London.","He founded the Fifth Republic in 1958 and was its first president.","He granted independence to Algeria in 1962.","Paris’s main airport is named after him."]},{"n":["mussolini","benito mussolini","fascism"],"s":["Benito Mussolini was the fascist dictator of Italy.","He lived from 1883 to 1945 and ruled from 1922 to 1943.","He founded fascism, a far-right nationalist movement.","He allied Italy with Nazi Germany.","He was overthrown in 1943.","He was captured and executed by Italian partisans in 1945."]},{"n":["emperor hirohito","hirohito"],"s":["Hirohito was Emperor of Japan from 1926 to 1989.","He lived from 1901 to 1989.","He reigned through the Second World War.","He announced Japan’s surrender on the radio in 1945, the first time most Japanese had heard his voice.","After the war he became a symbolic monarch."]},{"n":["ataturk","mustafa kemal ataturk","atatürk"],"s":["Mustafa Kemal Atatürk was the founder of modern Turkey.","He lived from 1881 to 1938.","He led the Turkish War of Independence.","He became the first president in 1923.","He made Turkey secular, adopted the Latin alphabet and gave women the vote.","His name means “Father of the Turks”."]},{"n":["nehru","jawaharlal nehru"],"s":["Jawaharlal Nehru was the first Prime Minister of India.","He lived from 1889 to 1964 and was PM from 1947 until his death.","He was a leader of the independence movement alongside Gandhi.","He built India as a secular democracy.","His daughter, Indira Gandhi, also became prime minister."]},{"n":["deng xiaoping"],"s":["Deng Xiaoping was the leader of China from 1978 to the late 1980s.","He lived from 1904 to 1997.","He launched market reforms that transformed China’s economy.","He said “it doesn’t matter if a cat is black or white, as long as it catches mice”.","He ordered the crackdown on Tiananmen Square protesters in 1989."]},{"n":["kim jong un","kim jong-un"],"s":["Kim Jong Un is the leader of North Korea.","He came to power in December 2011.","He succeeded his father, Kim Jong Il, and grandfather, Kim Il Sung.","He expanded North Korea’s nuclear weapons program.","He met US President Trump in 2018 and 2019."]},{"n":["angela merkel","merkel"],"s":["Angela Merkel was Chancellor of Germany.","She was born in 1954 and served from 2005 to 2021.","She was the first woman to hold the post.","She grew up in East Germany and trained as a physicist.","She guided Germany through the eurozone crisis and the 2015 refugee crisis."]},{"n":["volodymyr zelensky","zelensky","zelenskyy"],"s":["Volodymyr Zelensky is the President of Ukraine.","He was elected in 2019.","He was formerly an actor and comedian.","He played a president in the TV series Servant of the People.","He has led Ukraine since Russia’s full-scale invasion in February 2022."]},{"n":["vladimir putin","putin"],"s":["Vladimir Putin is the long-time leader of Russia.","He has been president or prime minister since 1999.","He was formerly a KGB officer.","He annexed Crimea from Ukraine in 2014.","He launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine in February 2022.","Critics accuse his government of suppressing opposition and the press."]},{"n":["donald trump","trump"],"s":["Donald Trump is an American businessman and politician who has served as President of the United States.","He was the 45th president from 2017 to 2021 and returned in 2025 as the 47th.","He was a real-estate developer and TV personality.","He was impeached twice during his first term and acquitted both times.","He is a highly polarising figure in American politics."]},{"n":["joe biden","biden"],"s":["Joe Biden was the 46th President of the United States.","He was born in 1942 and served from 2021 to 2025.","He was Barack Obama’s vice president from 2009 to 2017.","He served as a US senator for 36 years.","He was the oldest person to be inaugurated president at the time."]},{"n":["cleisthenes"],"s":["Cleisthenes was an Athenian lawmaker known as the father of Athenian democracy.","He introduced his reforms around 508 BC.","He reorganised citizens into ten tribes.","He gave the Assembly more power.","He was a member of the powerful Alcmaeonid family.","He is credited with introducing ostracism, a vote to exile a dangerous person."]},{"n":["pericles"],"s":["Pericles was the leading statesman of Athens in its golden age.","He lived from about 495 to 429 BC.","He championed democracy and the arts.","He oversaw the building of the Parthenon.","His funeral oration praised Athenian democracy.","He died of plague early in the Peloponnesian War."]},{"n":["attila the hun","attila","huns"],"s":["Attila was the ruler of the Huns, a nomadic people who terrorised the Roman Empire.","He ruled from 434 to 453.","He invaded the Balkans, Gaul and Italy.","He was defeated at the Battle of the Catalaunian Plains in 451.","Romans called him the “Scourge of God”.","His empire collapsed soon after his death."]},{"n":["william wallace","braveheart","robert the bruce"],"s":["William Wallace and Robert the Bruce were leaders of Scotland’s wars of independence against England.","Wallace won at Stirling Bridge in 1297; Bruce won at Bannockburn in 1314.","Wallace was captured and executed in London in 1305.","Bruce was crowned King of Scots in 1306.","England recognised Scottish independence in 1328.","His sword is displayed at the Wallace Monument near Stirling.","The film Braveheart loosely retells Wallace’s story."]},{"n":["king arthur","camelot"],"s":["King Arthur is a legendary British king.","Stories place him around the late 5th or early 6th century.","Historians are unsure whether a real Arthur existed.","Tales feature the Round Table, Camelot, Merlin and the sword Excalibur.","Medieval writers like Geoffrey of Monmouth built up the legend.","Tintagel in Cornwall is linked with his legend.","He remains one of the most famous figures of legend."]},{"n":["robin hood"],"s":["Robin Hood is a legendary English outlaw.","Ballads about him date from at least the 15th century.","He is said to have robbed the rich to give to the poor.","He lived in Sherwood Forest with his Merry Men.","His enemy was the Sheriff of Nottingham.","There is no firm evidence he was a real person."]},{"n":["ivan the terrible"],"s":["Ivan IV, known as Ivan the Terrible, was the first Tsar of Russia.","He lived from 1530 to 1584 and was crowned tsar in 1547.","He expanded Russia into Siberia.","He set up a brutal secret police, the oprichnina.","He killed his own son in a fit of rage.","“Terrible” is closer in meaning to “formidable” or “awe-inspiring”."]},{"n":["catherine the great"],"s":["Catherine the Great was Empress of Russia.","She lived from 1729 to 1796 and ruled from 1762.","She was born a German princess.","She took power after her husband, Peter III, was overthrown.","She expanded Russia and supported art and Enlightenment ideas.","She founded the Smolny Institute, Russia’s first school for women.","She corresponded with Voltaire."]},{"n":["suleiman the magnificent","suleiman"],"s":["Suleiman the Magnificent was the longest-reigning Ottoman sultan.","He ruled from 1520 to 1566.","He expanded the empire into Hungary and the Middle East.","He besieged Vienna in 1529.","Ottomans called him “the Lawgiver” for his legal reforms.","He wrote poetry under a pen name.","His reign was the empire’s golden age."]},{"n":["democracy"],"s":["Democracy is a system of government in which power belongs to the people.","It began in ancient Athens around 508 BC.","In a direct democracy, citizens vote on laws themselves.","In a representative democracy, citizens elect people to decide for them.","Modern democracies usually protect rights like free speech and fair elections.","Voting rights were gradually extended to all adults over the 19th and 20th centuries.","Switzerland uses frequent referendums, a form of direct democracy.","Winston Churchill joked that it is the worst form of government except all the others."]},{"n":["communism","karl marx","marx","communist manifesto","marxism"],"s":["Communism is a political idea that aims for a classless society where property is owned in common.","Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels published The Communist Manifesto in 1848.","Marx argued that history is driven by struggle between social classes.","He predicted workers would overthrow capitalism.","Communist states, like the USSR and China, were run by single parties.","Many of them became repressive and suffered economic failures.","Marx’s ideas still influence economics, history and sociology."]},{"n":["capitalism"],"s":["Capitalism is an economic system based on private ownership and free markets.","It grew in Europe from the 16th century and spread with the Industrial Revolution.","Businesses compete to make profits.","Prices are mostly set by supply and demand.","Adam Smith is often seen as its first great theorist.","Most countries today mix capitalism with government regulation and welfare.","Supporters credit it with growth and innovation; critics point to inequality."]},{"n":["socialism"],"s":["Socialism is a family of ideas in which the community or state owns or controls key parts of the economy.","It developed in the 19th century in response to the Industrial Revolution.","Socialists aim to reduce inequality.","Democratic socialists seek change through elections.","Many European countries combine markets with strong social programs.","The word means different things in different countries."]},{"n":["stoicism","stoics","seneca","epictetus"],"s":["Stoicism is an ancient philosophy about living calmly and virtuously.","It was founded in Athens by Zeno of Citium around 300 BC.","Stoics teach that we should focus only on what we can control.","They value wisdom, courage, justice and self-control.","Famous Stoics include Seneca, Epictetus and Marcus Aurelius.","Epictetus was born enslaved.","The phrase “stoic” now means enduring hardship without complaint.","It has become popular again as a practical guide to resilience."]},{"n":["nietzsche","friedrich nietzsche","god is dead"],"s":["Friedrich Nietzsche was a German philosopher.","He lived from 1844 to 1900.","He wrote that “God is dead”, meaning traditional religious values were losing their hold.","He wrote about the “will to power” and the Übermensch, or “overman”.","His books include Thus Spoke Zarathustra.","The Nazis misused his ideas, partly through his sister’s editing.","He was a professor at the University of Basel at just 24.","He had a mental breakdown in 1889."]},{"n":["descartes","rene descartes","i think therefore i am"],"s":["René Descartes was a French philosopher and mathematician.","He lived from 1596 to 1650.","He wrote “I think, therefore I am”.","He tried to doubt everything to find what was certain.","He invented the Cartesian coordinate system used in graphs.","He is called the father of modern philosophy."]},{"n":["kant","immanuel kant"],"s":["Immanuel Kant was a German philosopher of the Enlightenment.","He lived from 1724 to 1804.","He wrote the Critique of Pure Reason.","He argued we should act only by rules we could want everyone to follow, the “categorical imperative”.","He reportedly walked at the same time every day, so neighbours could set their clocks by him.","He never travelled far from his hometown of Königsberg."]},{"n":["machiavelli","the prince","niccolo machiavelli"],"s":["Niccolò Machiavelli was a Florentine diplomat and political writer.","He lived from 1469 to 1527.","He wrote The Prince, a handbook on gaining and keeping power.","He argued rulers sometimes must act ruthlessly.","He asked whether it is better to be loved or feared, concluding feared is safer if you cannot be both.","He was imprisoned and tortured after the Medici returned to power in 1512.","“Machiavellian” now means cunning and unscrupulous."]},{"n":["christianity","jesus","jesus christ"],"s":["Christianity is a religion based on the life and teachings of Jesus of Nazareth.","Jesus lived in the 1st century AD in Roman-ruled Judea and was crucified around AD 30 to 33.","Christians believe Jesus is the Son of God and rose from the dead.","His teachings stress love, forgiveness and care for the poor.","The New Testament tells his story.","Christianity spread across the Roman Empire and became its official religion.","It is the world’s largest religion, with over two billion followers."]},{"n":["judaism","jewish"],"s":["Judaism is one of the oldest monotheistic religions, the religion of the Jewish people.","Its roots go back more than 3,000 years.","Its core text is the Torah, the first five books of the Hebrew Bible.","It traces back to Abraham and to Moses, who received the law at Mount Sinai.","Its holy days include Shabbat, Passover and Yom Kippur.","It shaped both Christianity and Islam."]},{"n":["hinduism","hindu"],"s":["Hinduism is a major religion of South Asia, often called the oldest living religion.","Its roots go back thousands of years, to the Vedic period.","It has no single founder.","Key ideas include dharma (duty), karma (action and consequence) and reincarnation.","Its sacred texts include the Vedas, the Upanishads and the Bhagavad Gita.","Diwali, the festival of lights, is one of its most important celebrations.","It has about 1.2 billion followers."]},{"n":["sikhism","guru nanak"],"s":["Sikhism is a religion founded in the Punjab region of South Asia.","It was founded by Guru Nanak, who lived from 1469 to 1539.","It teaches belief in one God and the equality of all people.","Its holy book is the Guru Granth Sahib.","The Golden Temple in Amritsar is its holiest site.","Sikh temples serve free meals to all visitors in a langar."]},{"n":["vatican","vatican city","pope"],"s":["Vatican City is the world’s smallest country and the home of the Pope.","It became an independent state in 1929.","It lies inside Rome.","The Pope is the leader of the Catholic Church.","It contains St Peter’s Basilica and the Sistine Chapel.","It is guarded by the Swiss Guard."]},{"n":["human rights","universal declaration of human rights"],"s":["Human rights are basic rights and freedoms that belong to every person.","The UN adopted the Universal Declaration of Human Rights on 10 December 1948.","It was drafted by a committee chaired by Eleanor Roosevelt.","It includes rights to life, liberty, education and freedom of thought.","It is the most translated document in the world.","The European Convention on Human Rights followed in 1950.","10 December is now Human Rights Day."]},{"n":["feminism"],"s":["Feminism is the movement for equal rights and opportunities for women.","It is often described in “waves”, starting in the 19th century.","The first wave focused on votes and legal rights.","The second wave, from the 1960s, focused on work, family and equality.","Mary Wollstonecraft wrote A Vindication of the Rights of Woman in 1792.","It has transformed laws, workplaces and family life."]},{"n":["philosophy"],"s":["Philosophy is the study of big questions about knowledge, reality, ethics and meaning.","In the West it is usually traced to ancient Greece, around the 6th century BC.","The word means “love of wisdom”.","Major branches include ethics, logic, metaphysics and epistemology.","Great traditions also developed in China, India and the Islamic world.","Philosophy gave birth to many sciences."]},{"n":["existentialism","sartre","jean-paul sartre","camus","albert camus"],"s":["Existentialism is a philosophy focused on individual freedom, choice and meaning.","It flourished in mid-20th-century Europe.","Jean-Paul Sartre said “existence precedes essence”: we create our own meaning.","Albert Camus wrote about the “absurd”, using the myth of Sisyphus.","Simone de Beauvoir applied it to women’s lives in The Second Sex.","Camus won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1957.","Its ideas influenced literature, theatre and film."]},{"n":["lao tzu","laozi","taoism","daoism","tao te ching"],"s":["Laozi is the legendary founder of Taoism, a Chinese philosophy and religion.","He is traditionally said to have lived in the 6th century BC.","He is credited with the Tao Te Ching.","Taoism teaches living in harmony with the Tao, “the Way”.","It values simplicity, humility and going with the flow of nature.","Legend says he wrote the Tao Te Ching at a border gate before vanishing west.","The yin-yang symbol is linked with Taoist thought."]},{"n":["ancient philosophy","presocratics","heraclitus"],"s":["The Presocratic philosophers were the first Greek thinkers to explain nature without myths.","They lived in the 6th and 5th centuries BC.","Thales thought everything was made of water.","Heraclitus said you cannot step in the same river twice.","Democritus proposed that everything is made of tiny atoms.","They began the tradition of science and philosophy in the West."]},{"n":["eiffel tower"],"s":["The Eiffel Tower is a wrought-iron tower in Paris.","It was completed in 1889 for the World’s Fair.","It was designed by Gustave Eiffel’s company.","It is about 330 metres tall.","Many Parisians hated it at first.","It was meant to stand for only 20 years but became France’s symbol.","It was the world’s tallest structure until 1930.","It grows several centimetres taller in summer heat."]},{"n":["statue of liberty"],"s":["The Statue of Liberty is a giant copper statue in New York Harbor.","It was dedicated on 28 October 1886.","It was a gift from France to the United States.","It was designed by Frédéric Auguste Bartholdi; Gustave Eiffel built its frame.","It turned green as its copper oxidised.","Her full name is “Liberty Enlightening the World”.","It became a symbol of welcome for immigrants."]},{"n":["taj mahal"],"s":["The Taj Mahal is a white marble mausoleum in Agra, India.","It was built between about 1632 and 1653.","The Mughal emperor Shah Jahan built it for his wife, Mumtaz Mahal.","About 20,000 workers built it.","Its marble changes colour with the light.","It took about 1,000 elephants to transport materials, according to tradition.","It is seen as a masterpiece of Mughal architecture."]},{"n":["stonehenge"],"s":["Stonehenge is a prehistoric stone circle in southern England.","It was built in stages from about 3000 to 1500 BC.","Some stones came from Wales, about 240 kilometres away.","It lines up with the sunrise at the summer solstice.","Its purpose is still debated, but it was probably a ceremonial site.","Its largest stones, the sarsens, weigh around 25 tonnes.","It is one of the most famous prehistoric monuments in the world."]},{"n":["parthenon","acropolis"],"s":["The Parthenon is an ancient temple on the Acropolis of Athens.","It was built between 447 and 432 BC.","It was dedicated to the goddess Athena.","It was built under Pericles.","It was badly damaged by an explosion in 1687.","Its columns bulge slightly to look straight to the human eye.","Some of its sculptures, the Elgin Marbles, are in the British Museum."]},{"n":["petra"],"s":["Petra is an ancient city carved into rock in southern Jordan.","It was the capital of the Nabataean kingdom from around the 4th century BC.","Its most famous building is the Treasury.","It was a major trading hub.","It was rediscovered by Europeans in 1812.","It appeared in the film Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade.","It is one of the New Seven Wonders of the World."]},{"n":["angkor wat"],"s":["Angkor Wat is a vast temple complex in Cambodia.","It was built in the early 12th century.","It was built by King Suryavarman II of the Khmer Empire.","It was first a Hindu temple, later Buddhist.","It is the largest religious monument in the world.","It faces west, unusually for a Hindu temple.","It appears on Cambodia’s flag."]},{"n":["seven wonders","seven wonders of the ancient world"],"s":["The Seven Wonders of the Ancient World were famous structures listed by Greek writers.","The list dates from around the 2nd century BC.","They were the Great Pyramid, the Hanging Gardens of Babylon, the Statue of Zeus at Olympia, the Temple of Artemis, the Mausoleum at Halicarnassus, the Colossus of Rhodes and the Lighthouse of Alexandria.","Only the Great Pyramid survives.","The Hanging Gardens may never have existed."]},{"n":["library of alexandria"],"s":["The Library of Alexandria was one of the largest libraries of the ancient world.","It was founded in the 3rd century BC in Egypt.","It may have held hundreds of thousands of scrolls.","Scholars there measured the Earth’s size and edited Homer.","It declined gradually, rather than in one great fire.","It is a symbol of lost knowledge."]},{"n":["notre dame","notre-dame"],"s":["Notre-Dame de Paris is a medieval Gothic cathedral in Paris.","Construction began in 1163 and was mostly finished by 1260.","It is famous for its flying buttresses and rose windows.","Victor Hugo’s The Hunchback of Notre-Dame made it famous.","A fire destroyed its spire and roof in April 2019.","Its bells each have names; the largest is Emmanuel.","It reopened in December 2024 after restoration."]},{"n":["big ben"],"s":["Big Ben is the nickname of the great bell in the clock tower of the Palace of Westminster in London.","The bell was cast in 1858, and the clock started in 1859.","The tower was renamed the Elizabeth Tower in 2012.","The bell weighs over 13 tonnes.","It is one of London’s most famous landmarks."]},{"n":["kremlin"],"s":["The Kremlin is a fortified complex in the centre of Moscow.","Its current walls date from the late 15th century.","It is the official residence of the Russian president.","It contains palaces and cathedrals.","It stands beside Red Square and St Basil’s Cathedral."]},{"n":["machu picchu ruins","lost city of the incas"],"s":["Machu Picchu is a 15th-century Inca citadel high in the Andes of Peru.","It was built around 1450.","It was abandoned about a century later.","Hiram Bingham brought it to world attention in 1911.","It sits about 2,430 metres above sea level.","It is one of the New Seven Wonders of the World."]},{"n":["mount everest","everest","edmund hillary","tenzing norgay"],"s":["Mount Everest is the highest mountain on Earth.","Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay first reached the summit on 29 May 1953.","It stands about 8,849 metres above sea level.","It sits on the border of Nepal and China.","In Nepal it is called Sagarmatha.","It grows by a few millimetres each year.","Hundreds of people now climb it each year."]},{"n":["golden gate bridge"],"s":["The Golden Gate Bridge is a suspension bridge in San Francisco.","It opened in 1937.","It spans the strait between San Francisco Bay and the Pacific.","Its colour is called “International Orange”.","Fog often hides the towers.","It was the longest suspension bridge in the world when it opened."]},{"n":["mount rushmore"],"s":["Mount Rushmore is a mountain sculpture in South Dakota.","It was carved between 1927 and 1941.","It shows Washington, Jefferson, Theodore Roosevelt and Lincoln.","Each face is about 18 metres tall.","The land is sacred to the Lakota Sioux."]},{"n":["sydney opera house"],"s":["The Sydney Opera House is a performing arts centre in Australia.","It opened in 1973.","It was designed by Danish architect Jørn Utzon.","Its roof looks like sails.","Utzon resigned before it was finished and never saw it completed.","It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."]},{"n":["christ the redeemer"],"s":["Christ the Redeemer is a giant statue of Jesus overlooking Rio de Janeiro.","It was completed in 1931.","It stands 30 metres tall, not counting its base.","It is made of reinforced concrete and soapstone.","It is one of the New Seven Wonders of the World."]},{"n":["michael faraday","faraday"],"s":["Michael Faraday was an English scientist who pioneered electricity and magnetism.","He lived from 1791 to 1867.","He discovered electromagnetic induction in 1831.","This principle powers electric generators and transformers.","He invented an early electric motor.","He had little formal schooling and began as a bookbinder’s apprentice.","He started the Royal Institution Christmas Lectures for young people."]},{"n":["james clerk maxwell","maxwell"],"s":["James Clerk Maxwell was a Scottish physicist.","He lived from 1831 to 1879.","His equations unified electricity, magnetism and light.","He showed light is an electromagnetic wave.","He took the first colour photograph in 1861.","Einstein kept his picture on his study wall."]},{"n":["niels bohr","bohr"],"s":["Niels Bohr was a Danish physicist who shaped our understanding of the atom.","He lived from 1885 to 1962.","His model put electrons in fixed orbits around the nucleus.","He won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1922.","He had famous debates with Einstein about quantum theory.","He escaped Nazi-occupied Denmark in 1943."]},{"n":["ernest rutherford","rutherford"],"s":["Ernest Rutherford was a New Zealand-born physicist, the father of nuclear physics.","He lived from 1871 to 1937.","He discovered that atoms have a small, dense nucleus.","His gold foil experiment showed most of an atom is empty space.","He won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1908.","His team first split the atom in 1932."]},{"n":["richard feynman","feynman"],"s":["Richard Feynman was an American theoretical physicist.","He lived from 1918 to 1988.","He won the 1965 Nobel Prize for work on quantum electrodynamics.","He invented Feynman diagrams.","He worked on the Manhattan Project.","He showed why the Challenger shuttle failed using a glass of ice water.","He was famous as a brilliant teacher and storyteller."]},{"n":["hypatia"],"s":["Hypatia was a mathematician, astronomer and philosopher in Alexandria.","She lived from about 355 to 415.","She taught philosophy and mathematics.","She is one of the first women mathematicians whose life is well recorded.","She was murdered by a Christian mob in 415.","She became a symbol of learning and reason."]},{"n":["al-khwarizmi","al khwarizmi","algebra"],"s":["Al-Khwarizmi was a Persian mathematician of the Islamic Golden Age.","He lived from about 780 to 850, working in Baghdad’s House of Wisdom.","His book on equations gave us the word “algebra”.","The word “algorithm” comes from his name.","He helped spread Hindu-Arabic numerals.","He is often called the father of algebra."]},{"n":["ibn sina","avicenna"],"s":["Ibn Sina, known in Europe as Avicenna, was a Persian physician and philosopher.","He lived from about 980 to 1037.","He wrote The Canon of Medicine.","It was a standard medical textbook in Europe for centuries.","He wrote on philosophy, astronomy and music too.","He is one of the most influential thinkers of the Islamic Golden Age."]},{"n":["hippocrates","hippocratic oath"],"s":["Hippocrates was an ancient Greek physician, called the father of medicine.","He lived from about 460 to 370 BC.","He taught that diseases have natural causes, not divine ones.","The Hippocratic Oath, an ethical code for doctors, is named after him.","Its principle is often summarised as “first, do no harm”."]},{"n":["alfred nobel","nobel prize","nobel"],"s":["Alfred Nobel was a Swedish chemist and inventor who founded the Nobel Prizes.","He lived from 1833 to 1896; the first prizes were awarded in 1901.","He invented dynamite in 1867.","A newspaper mistakenly printed his obituary, calling him a “merchant of death”.","He left his fortune to fund prizes for those who benefit humanity.","Prizes are given in physics, chemistry, medicine, literature and peace; economics was added later."]},{"n":["carl sagan","sagan","pale blue dot"],"s":["Carl Sagan was an American astronomer and science communicator.","He lived from 1934 to 1996.","He presented the TV series Cosmos in 1980.","He helped design the Voyager Golden Records.","He wrote about the “Pale Blue Dot” photo of Earth.","He made astronomy exciting for millions."]},{"n":["jane goodall","goodall"],"s":["Jane Goodall is an English primatologist famous for studying chimpanzees.","She was born in 1934 and began her research in Tanzania in 1960.","She discovered that chimpanzees make and use tools.","She showed they have personalities and complex social lives.","She founded the Jane Goodall Institute.","She became a global voice for conservation."]},{"n":["david attenborough","attenborough"],"s":["Sir David Attenborough is an English broadcaster and naturalist.","He was born in 1926.","He has made nature documentaries for over 70 years.","His series include Life on Earth, Planet Earth and Blue Planet.","He is a leading voice on climate and conservation.","Several species are named after him."]},{"n":["rachel carson","silent spring"],"s":["Rachel Carson was an American marine biologist and writer.","She lived from 1907 to 1964.","Her book Silent Spring (1962) warned about pesticides like DDT.","It helped launch the modern environmental movement.","It led to a US ban on DDT for farm use in 1972."]},{"n":["grace hopper"],"s":["Grace Hopper was an American computer scientist and navy rear admiral.","She lived from 1906 to 1992.","She developed one of the first compilers.","She helped create the programming language COBOL.","She popularised the term “bug” after a moth was found in a computer.","She was nicknamed “Amazing Grace”."]},{"n":["katherine johnson","hidden figures"],"s":["Katherine Johnson was an American mathematician at NASA.","She lived from 1918 to 2020.","She calculated flight paths for early US space missions.","John Glenn asked her personally to check the computer’s numbers before his 1962 orbit.","She worked on the Apollo 11 mission.","The film Hidden Figures tells her story."]},{"n":["steve jobs","iphone"],"s":["Steve Jobs was an American entrepreneur who co-founded Apple.","He lived from 1955 to 2011.","He founded Apple with Steve Wozniak in 1976.","He was forced out in 1985 and returned in 1997.","He launched the iPod, iPhone and iPad.","He also led Pixar Animation Studios.","He was famous for his product launches in a black turtleneck."]},{"n":["bill gates","microsoft"],"s":["Bill Gates is an American businessman who co-founded Microsoft.","He was born in 1955 and founded Microsoft with Paul Allen in 1975.","Microsoft’s Windows became the dominant PC operating system.","He was the world’s richest person for many years.","He co-founded a major charitable foundation focused on global health."]},{"n":["mark zuckerberg","facebook"],"s":["Mark Zuckerberg is an American businessman who co-founded Facebook.","He was born in 1984 and launched Facebook in 2004.","He started it as a student at Harvard.","The company renamed itself Meta in 2021.","It also owns Instagram and WhatsApp."]},{"n":["elon musk","spacex"],"s":["Elon Musk is a businessman known for leading SpaceX and Tesla.","He was born in South Africa in 1971.","SpaceX was founded in 2002 and developed reusable rockets.","He led the electric car company Tesla.","He bought Twitter in 2022 and renamed it X.","He is a prominent and polarising public figure."]},{"n":["jeff bezos","amazon company"],"s":["Jeff Bezos is an American businessman who founded Amazon.","He was born in 1964 and founded Amazon in 1994.","Amazon began as an online bookshop.","It grew into one of the world’s largest companies.","He founded the space company Blue Origin."]},{"n":["hedy lamarr"],"s":["Hedy Lamarr was an Austrian-American film star and inventor.","She lived from 1914 to 2000.","She was a major Hollywood star in the 1930s and 1940s.","She co-invented a frequency-hopping system to stop torpedoes being jammed.","Ideas like it later helped make Wi-Fi and Bluetooth possible."]},{"n":["galen"],"s":["Galen was a Greek physician in the Roman Empire.","He lived from about AD 129 to 216.","He was physician to emperors, including Marcus Aurelius.","He dissected animals to study anatomy.","His ideas dominated medicine for over a thousand years, mistakes included."]},{"n":["andreas vesalius","vesalius"],"s":["Andreas Vesalius was a Flemish anatomist, the founder of modern anatomy.","He lived from 1514 to 1564.","His book On the Fabric of the Human Body appeared in 1543.","He corrected many of Galen’s errors through dissection.","Its illustrations were stunningly accurate."]},{"n":["william harvey"],"s":["William Harvey was an English physician.","He lived from 1578 to 1657.","He discovered that blood circulates around the body, pumped by the heart.","He published his findings in 1628.","Many doctors at first rejected his idea."]},{"n":["carl linnaeus","linnaeus"],"s":["Carl Linnaeus was a Swedish botanist who created the modern system for naming living things.","He lived from 1707 to 1778.","He gave each species a two-part Latin name, like Homo sapiens.","This is called binomial nomenclature.","He is called the father of taxonomy."]},{"n":["lavoisier","antoine lavoisier"],"s":["Antoine Lavoisier was a French chemist, the father of modern chemistry.","He lived from 1743 to 1794.","He named oxygen and hydrogen.","He showed that mass is conserved in chemical reactions.","He was guillotined during the French Revolution because he had been a tax collector."]},{"n":["joseph lister","lister","antiseptic"],"s":["Joseph Lister was a British surgeon who pioneered antiseptic surgery.","He lived from 1827 to 1912.","He used carbolic acid to clean wounds and instruments.","This sharply reduced deaths after surgery.","The mouthwash Listerine is named after him."]},{"n":["max planck","planck"],"s":["Max Planck was a German physicist, the founder of quantum theory.","He lived from 1858 to 1947.","In 1900 he proposed that energy comes in small packets, or quanta.","He won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1918.","Planck’s constant is one of the fundamental numbers of physics."]},{"n":["heisenberg","werner heisenberg","uncertainty principle"],"s":["Werner Heisenberg was a German physicist and a founder of quantum mechanics.","He lived from 1901 to 1976.","He formulated the uncertainty principle in 1927.","It says you cannot know a particle’s exact position and momentum at the same time.","He won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1932."]},{"n":["schrodinger","schrödinger","schrodingers cat","schrödinger’s cat"],"s":["Erwin Schrödinger was an Austrian physicist, a founder of quantum mechanics.","He lived from 1887 to 1961.","His equation describes how quantum systems change.","His “cat” thought experiment imagined a cat both alive and dead until observed.","He meant it to show how strange quantum theory is.","He won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1933."]},{"n":["robert hooke","hooke"],"s":["Robert Hooke was an English scientist and polymath.","He lived from 1635 to 1703.","He coined the word “cell” after looking at cork through a microscope.","His book Micrographia appeared in 1665.","He described the law of elasticity, Hooke’s law.","He had a bitter rivalry with Isaac Newton."]},{"n":["antonie van leeuwenhoek","leeuwenhoek","microscope"],"s":["Antonie van Leeuwenhoek was a Dutch scientist, the father of microbiology.","He lived from 1632 to 1723.","He built his own powerful microscopes.","He was the first to see bacteria and single-celled organisms, which he called “animalcules”.","He had no university training."]},{"n":["captain cook","james cook"],"s":["Captain James Cook was a British navigator and explorer.","He lived from 1728 to 1779.","He made three great voyages across the Pacific.","He mapped New Zealand and the east coast of Australia.","He crossed the Antarctic Circle.","He was killed in Hawaii in 1779.","He fought scurvy by making his crew eat sauerkraut.","He rose from humble origins as a farm labourer’s son.","His voyages opened the way to British colonisation, with lasting harm to Indigenous peoples."]},{"n":["lewis and clark"],"s":["Lewis and Clark led the first US expedition across the American West to the Pacific.","The journey lasted from 1804 to 1806.","President Jefferson sent Meriwether Lewis and William Clark after the Louisiana Purchase.","A Shoshone woman, Sacagawea, guided and interpreted for them.","They mapped land and recorded hundreds of new species.","They reached the Pacific coast in November 1805."]},{"n":["roald amundsen","amundsen","south pole"],"s":["Roald Amundsen was a Norwegian explorer who led the first expedition to reach the South Pole.","He reached it on 14 December 1911.","He beat Robert Falcon Scott’s British team by about five weeks.","He used dogs and skis expertly.","Scott and his companions died on their return journey.","Amundsen also first sailed the Northwest Passage."]},{"n":["ernest shackleton","shackleton","endurance"],"s":["Ernest Shackleton was an Anglo-Irish Antarctic explorer.","His Endurance expedition lasted from 1914 to 1917.","His ship, Endurance, was crushed by sea ice.","He led his men across ice and open sea to safety.","He sailed a small boat 1,300 kilometres to South Georgia for help.","Remarkably, all his men survived.","His recruiting advert, “Men wanted for hazardous journey”, is probably a legend.","The wreck of Endurance was found in 2022."]},{"n":["ibn battuta"],"s":["Ibn Battuta was a Moroccan scholar and one of history’s greatest travellers.","He lived from 1304 to about 1369.","He travelled for nearly 30 years.","He visited Africa, the Middle East, India, Central Asia and China.","He covered about 117,000 kilometres, far more than Marco Polo.","His account is called the Rihla."]},{"n":["zheng he"],"s":["Zheng He was a Chinese admiral who led huge naval expeditions.","His voyages took place from 1405 to 1433.","He served the Ming dynasty.","His fleets had hundreds of ships, some enormous.","He sailed to South-East Asia, India, Arabia and East Africa.","China later ended the voyages and turned inward."]},{"n":["amelia earhart","earhart"],"s":["Amelia Earhart was an American aviation pioneer.","She lived from 1897 to 1937 (presumed).","In 1932 she became the first woman to fly solo across the Atlantic.","She disappeared over the Pacific in 1937 while trying to fly around the world.","Her fate is still a mystery.","She was a bestselling author and fashion designer too.","She inspired generations of women in aviation."]},{"n":["charles lindbergh","lindbergh"],"s":["Charles Lindbergh was an American aviator.","He lived from 1902 to 1974.","In 1927 he made the first solo non-stop flight across the Atlantic.","His plane was called the Spirit of St. Louis.","The flight took about 33.5 hours.","His baby son’s kidnapping in 1932 was a notorious crime."]},{"n":["jacques cousteau","cousteau"],"s":["Jacques Cousteau was a French ocean explorer and filmmaker.","He lived from 1910 to 1997.","He co-invented the Aqua-Lung, an early scuba device, in 1943.","His films and TV shows revealed the underwater world.","He sailed his ship, Calypso, around the world.","He became a passionate conservationist."]},{"n":["muhammad ali","cassius clay"],"s":["Muhammad Ali was an American boxer, often called the greatest of all time.","He lived from 1942 to 2016.","He was born Cassius Clay.","He won Olympic gold in 1960 and was heavyweight champion three times.","He said he would “float like a butterfly, sting like a bee”.","He refused to fight in Vietnam and was stripped of his title.","He lit the Olympic flame in Atlanta in 1996.","His fights included the “Rumble in the Jungle” and “Thrilla in Manila”."]},{"n":["pele","pelé"],"s":["Pelé was a Brazilian footballer, one of the greatest of all time.","He lived from 1940 to 2022.","He won three World Cups, in 1958, 1962 and 1970.","He scored over 1,000 goals in his career, by his count.","He was 17 when he won his first World Cup.","He was named after the inventor Thomas Edison: Edson Arantes do Nascimento.","He was known as “O Rei”, the King."]},{"n":["jesse owens"],"s":["Jesse Owens was an American track and field athlete.","He lived from 1913 to 1980.","He won four gold medals at the 1936 Berlin Olympics.","His victories challenged Nazi ideas of racial superiority.","He faced racism at home despite his success."]},{"n":["michael jordan"],"s":["Michael Jordan is an American basketball player, widely seen as the greatest.","He was born in 1963.","He won six NBA championships with the Chicago Bulls.","He won two Olympic gold medals.","His Air Jordan shoes became a global brand."]},{"n":["usain bolt"],"s":["Usain Bolt is a Jamaican sprinter, the fastest man ever recorded.","He was born in 1986.","He set the 100-metre world record of 9.58 seconds in 2009.","He won eight Olympic gold medals.","His “lightning bolt” pose became famous."]},{"n":["serena williams"],"s":["Serena Williams is an American tennis player, one of the greatest ever.","She was born in 1981.","She won 23 Grand Slam singles titles.","She won one while pregnant, in 2017.","She and her sister Venus dominated women’s tennis."]},{"n":["lionel messi","messi"],"s":["Lionel Messi is an Argentine footballer, one of the greatest of all time.","He was born in 1987.","He spent most of his career at Barcelona.","He has won a record eight Ballon d’Or awards.","He led Argentina to win the 2022 World Cup."]},{"n":["cristiano ronaldo","ronaldo"],"s":["Cristiano Ronaldo is a Portuguese footballer.","He was born in 1985.","He has played for Manchester United, Real Madrid and Juventus.","He is the all-time top scorer in men’s international football.","He has won five Ballon d’Or awards."]},{"n":["world cup","fifa world cup"],"s":["The FIFA World Cup is the world’s biggest football tournament.","The first was held in Uruguay in 1930.","It is held every four years.","Brazil has won it five times, the most.","Uruguay won the first one.","Billions of people watch it."]},{"n":["babe ruth"],"s":["Babe Ruth was an American baseball player.","He lived from 1895 to 1948.","He played mainly for the New York Yankees.","He hit 714 home runs, a record for decades.","He was called “the Sultan of Swat”."]},{"n":["wimbledon"],"s":["Wimbledon is the oldest tennis tournament in the world.","It was first held in 1877.","It is played on grass in London.","Players must wear mostly white.","Strawberries and cream are a tradition there."]},{"n":["tour de france"],"s":["The Tour de France is the world’s most famous cycling race.","It was first held in 1903.","It lasts about three weeks.","The leader wears the yellow jersey.","It ends in Paris."]},{"n":["wheel","invention of the wheel"],"s":["The wheel is one of humanity’s most important inventions.","It was invented in Mesopotamia around 3500 BC.","It was first used for pottery, then carts.","Spoked wheels came later, around 2000 BC.","Some ancient American cultures used wheels only on toys.","It transformed transport and machinery."]},{"n":["writing","invention of writing","cuneiform","hieroglyphs"],"s":["Writing was invented independently in several places.","The earliest known writing appeared in Mesopotamia around 3200 BC.","Sumerian cuneiform began as a way to keep accounts.","Egyptian hieroglyphs appeared around the same time.","Writing also developed in China and Mesoamerica.","The alphabet developed around 1800 BC and was spread by the Phoenicians."]},{"n":["paper","invention of paper","cai lun"],"s":["Paper was invented in China.","Cai Lun, an imperial official, is credited with improving it around AD 105.","It was made from bark, hemp and rags.","Knowledge of papermaking spread to the Islamic world in the 8th century.","It reached Europe by the 11th century.","It made books and records far cheaper."]},{"n":["gunpowder"],"s":["Gunpowder was invented in China.","It was discovered around the 9th century.","Chinese alchemists found it while searching for an elixir of life.","It is a mix of saltpetre, charcoal and sulphur.","It was used in fireworks and weapons.","It transformed warfare worldwide."]},{"n":["compass","magnetic compass"],"s":["The magnetic compass was invented in China.","It was used for navigation by the 11th century.","Early compasses used magnetised needles or lodestone.","It spread to Europe and the Islamic world.","It made long ocean voyages possible."]},{"n":["telescope"],"s":["The telescope was invented in the Netherlands.","Hans Lippershey applied for a patent in 1608.","Galileo built improved versions in 1609.","Newton built the first practical reflecting telescope in 1668.","Telescopes revealed moons, galaxies and the expanding universe."]},{"n":["radio","marconi","guglielmo marconi"],"s":["Radio uses electromagnetic waves to send sound without wires.","Guglielmo Marconi sent the first transatlantic radio signal in 1901.","Others, including Tesla and Popov, made key contributions.","Radio broadcasting began in the 1920s.","Radio helped save survivors of the Titanic.","It brought news and music into homes worldwide."]},{"n":["television","tv","john logie baird"],"s":["Television transmits moving images and sound.","John Logie Baird demonstrated a working television in 1926.","Philo Farnsworth developed an all-electronic system.","TV became common in homes in the 1950s.","Colour TV spread in the 1960s.","It transformed entertainment, news and politics."]},{"n":["car","automobile","first car","history of the car"],"s":["The automobile changed how people travel.","Karl Benz built the first practical petrol-powered car in 1885 and patented it in 1886.","His wife Bertha made the first long-distance car journey in 1888.","Henry Ford’s Model T made cars affordable.","Electric cars have grown rapidly in the 21st century."]},{"n":["airplane","aeroplane","aircraft","plane"],"s":["Airplanes are powered aircraft with fixed wings.","The Wright brothers made the first powered flight in 1903.","Aircraft were used in the First World War.","Jet engines were developed in the 1930s.","Commercial air travel became common after the 1950s.","Concorde flew faster than sound from 1976 to 2003."]},{"n":["electricity"],"s":["Electricity is the flow of electric charge.","It was studied seriously from the 18th century.","Benjamin Franklin showed lightning is electrical.","Alessandro Volta built the first battery in 1800.","Faraday discovered how to generate it with magnets.","Edison and Tesla helped bring it into homes."]},{"n":["smartphone","mobile phone","cell phone"],"s":["Mobile phones let people talk without wires.","The first handheld mobile call was made by Martin Cooper of Motorola in 1973.","Early mobile phones were large and expensive.","Text messaging began in the 1990s.","The iPhone, launched in 2007, popularised smartphones.","Billions of people now carry one."]},{"n":["photography","camera","first photograph"],"s":["Photography captures images using light.","The oldest surviving photograph was taken by Nicéphore Niépce around 1826.","It needed an exposure of hours.","Louis Daguerre developed the daguerreotype in 1839.","Kodak made cameras easy for everyone from 1888.","Digital cameras replaced film in the 2000s."]},{"n":["vaccine history","first vaccine"],"s":["The first vaccine was developed by Edward Jenner in 1796.","It protected against smallpox.","Louis Pasteur later made vaccines for rabies and anthrax.","The polio vaccine was developed by Jonas Salk in the 1950s.","Vaccines have saved hundreds of millions of lives."]},{"n":["jonas salk","polio","polio vaccine"],"s":["Jonas Salk was an American researcher who developed the first successful polio vaccine.","It was announced in 1955.","Polio paralysed many children.","Salk did not patent the vaccine.","Asked who owned it, he said, “Could you patent the sun?”","Polio is now close to eradication."]},{"n":["artificial intelligence","ai history","machine learning"],"s":["Artificial intelligence is the science of making machines that can perform tasks needing intelligence.","The term was coined at a conference at Dartmouth College in 1956.","Alan Turing proposed the Turing test in 1950.","IBM’s Deep Blue beat world chess champion Garry Kasparov in 1997.","Machine learning lets computers learn patterns from data.","Large language models became widely used in the 2020s."]},{"n":["mark twain","huckleberry finn","tom sawyer"],"s":["Mark Twain was an American writer and humorist.","He lived from 1835 to 1910; his real name was Samuel Clemens.","He wrote The Adventures of Tom Sawyer and Adventures of Huckleberry Finn.","He worked as a Mississippi riverboat pilot; “mark twain” was a river depth call.","He was famous for wit, like “the reports of my death are greatly exaggerated”.","He was born and died in years when Halley’s Comet appeared, as he predicted.","Hemingway said all modern American literature comes from Huckleberry Finn."]},{"n":["ernest hemingway","hemingway"],"s":["Ernest Hemingway was an American novelist.","He lived from 1899 to 1961.","His books include The Old Man and the Sea and A Farewell to Arms.","He wrote in short, simple sentences.","He was an ambulance driver in the First World War.","He won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1954."]},{"n":["virginia woolf"],"s":["Virginia Woolf was an English modernist writer.","She lived from 1882 to 1941.","Her novels include Mrs Dalloway and To the Lighthouse.","Her essay A Room of One’s Own argued women need money and space to write.","She was part of the Bloomsbury Group."]},{"n":["edgar allan poe","poe","the raven"],"s":["Edgar Allan Poe was an American writer of mystery and horror.","He lived from 1809 to 1849.","His poem The Raven made him famous.","He is credited with inventing the detective story.","His tales include The Tell-Tale Heart.","His death remains a mystery."]},{"n":["arthur conan doyle","sherlock holmes","conan doyle"],"s":["Sir Arthur Conan Doyle was a Scottish writer who created Sherlock Holmes.","He lived from 1859 to 1930; Holmes first appeared in 1887.","He was a doctor.","Holmes lived at 221B Baker Street with Dr Watson.","Doyle killed Holmes off in 1893 but brought him back due to public demand.","Holmes never actually says “Elementary, my dear Watson” in the books."]},{"n":["agatha christie","christie"],"s":["Agatha Christie was an English crime novelist.","She lived from 1890 to 1976.","She created the detectives Hercule Poirot and Miss Marple.","She is the best-selling novelist of all time.","Her play The Mousetrap has run in London since 1952.","She worked in a hospital pharmacy and learned about poisons.","She once disappeared for 11 days in 1926."]},{"n":["j k rowling","harry potter","rowling"],"s":["J. K. Rowling is a British author best known for the Harry Potter series.","The first book was published in 1997.","She wrote it partly in Edinburgh cafés.","It was rejected by several publishers.","The seven books have sold over 500 million copies.","Her pen name uses the initial K for her grandmother Kathleen.","They were adapted into eight films."]},{"n":["victor hugo","les miserables","les misérables"],"s":["Victor Hugo was a French writer.","He lived from 1802 to 1885.","He wrote Les Misérables and The Hunchback of Notre-Dame.","He was a passionate campaigner against the death penalty.","He lived in exile for nearly 20 years.","His funeral drew around two million people."]},{"n":["emily dickinson"],"s":["Emily Dickinson was an American poet.","She lived from 1830 to 1886.","She wrote nearly 1,800 poems.","Fewer than a dozen were published in her lifetime.","She lived mostly in seclusion in Massachusetts.","Her unusual punctuation and short lines made her a pioneer."]},{"n":["maya angelou"],"s":["Maya Angelou was an American poet, memoirist and activist.","She lived from 1928 to 2014.","Her memoir I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings appeared in 1969.","She worked with Martin Luther King Jr. and Malcolm X.","She read a poem at Bill Clinton’s inauguration in 1993."]},{"n":["chopin","frederic chopin"],"s":["Frédéric Chopin was a Polish composer and pianist.","He lived from 1810 to 1849.","He wrote mainly for the piano.","His works include nocturnes, waltzes and mazurkas.","He lived in Paris from 1831.","His heart was buried in Warsaw."]},{"n":["tchaikovsky"],"s":["Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky was a Russian composer.","He lived from 1840 to 1893.","He wrote the ballets Swan Lake, The Sleeping Beauty and The Nutcracker.","His 1812 Overture includes real cannon fire.","He is one of the most popular composers."]},{"n":["vivaldi","four seasons"],"s":["Antonio Vivaldi was an Italian Baroque composer.","He lived from 1678 to 1741.","He wrote The Four Seasons, a set of violin concertos.","He was a priest, nicknamed “the Red Priest” for his hair.","He taught music at an orphanage for girls in Venice."]},{"n":["louis armstrong","jazz"],"s":["Louis Armstrong was an American jazz trumpeter and singer.","He lived from 1901 to 1971.","He grew up poor in New Orleans, the birthplace of jazz.","He helped make jazz a soloist’s art.","His hits include What a Wonderful World.","He was nicknamed “Satchmo”."]},{"n":["bob marley","reggae"],"s":["Bob Marley was a Jamaican reggae musician.","He lived from 1945 to 1981.","He made reggae famous worldwide.","His songs include No Woman, No Cry and One Love.","He was a follower of Rastafari.","He was shot in 1976 but played a concert two days later."]},{"n":["queen band","freddie mercury","bohemian rhapsody"],"s":["Queen was a British rock band fronted by Freddie Mercury.","They formed in 1970.","Their hits include Bohemian Rhapsody and We Will Rock You.","Their Live Aid performance in 1985 is legendary.","Freddie Mercury died in 1991."]},{"n":["hollywood","charlie chaplin","chaplin"],"s":["Hollywood is the centre of the American film industry, and Charlie Chaplin was one of its first great stars.","Chaplin lived from 1889 to 1977.","He created the character of the Tramp.","His films include The Kid and Modern Times.","He mocked Hitler in The Great Dictator in 1940.","Hollywood grew in the 1910s thanks to sunny weather and cheap land."]},{"n":["walt disney","disney","mickey mouse"],"s":["Walt Disney was an American animator and entrepreneur.","He lived from 1901 to 1966.","He co-created Mickey Mouse, who debuted in Steamboat Willie in 1928.","Snow White (1937) was the first full-length cel-animated feature film.","He opened Disneyland in 1955.","He was the original voice of Mickey Mouse.","He won a record 22 Academy Awards."]},{"n":["marilyn monroe"],"s":["Marilyn Monroe was an American actress and icon.","She lived from 1926 to 1962.","She was born Norma Jeane Mortenson.","Her films include Some Like It Hot.","She sang “Happy Birthday” to President Kennedy in 1962.","She died at 36."]},{"n":["andy warhol","pop art"],"s":["Andy Warhol was an American artist and leader of Pop Art.","He lived from 1928 to 1987.","He made famous prints of Campbell’s soup cans and Marilyn Monroe.","He predicted everyone would be “world-famous for 15 minutes”.","His studio was called The Factory."]},{"n":["salvador dali","dali","surrealism"],"s":["Salvador Dalí was a Spanish Surrealist painter.","He lived from 1904 to 1989.","His painting The Persistence of Memory shows melting clocks.","Surrealism explored dreams and the unconscious.","He was famous for his upturned moustache."]},{"n":["botticelli","birth of venus"],"s":["Sandro Botticelli was an Italian Renaissance painter.","He lived from about 1445 to 1510.","He painted The Birth of Venus and Primavera.","He worked for the Medici.","His work fell out of fashion until the 19th century."]},{"n":["raphael"],"s":["Raphael was an Italian painter and architect of the High Renaissance.","He lived from 1483 to 1520.","He painted The School of Athens in the Vatican.","It shows Plato, Aristotle and other philosophers.","He died at 37.","He is one of the trio of great High Renaissance masters with Leonardo and Michelangelo."]},{"n":["banksy"],"s":["Banksy is an anonymous street artist from England.","His work became famous from the 1990s.","He uses stencils to paint satirical images on walls.","His identity has never been officially confirmed.","In 2018 one of his paintings shredded itself just after it sold at auction."]},{"n":["great zimbabwe"],"s":["Great Zimbabwe is a ruined medieval city in south-east Africa.","It flourished from about the 11th to the 15th century.","Its huge stone walls were built without mortar.","It was the capital of a wealthy trading kingdom.","It traded gold and ivory as far as China.","The modern country of Zimbabwe is named after it."]},{"n":["kingdom of kush","kush","nubia"],"s":["The Kingdom of Kush was an ancient African kingdom in Nubia, south of Egypt.","It lasted from around 1070 BC to AD 350.","Kushite kings ruled Egypt as its 25th Dynasty.","Its capital later moved to Meroë.","Sudan has more pyramids than Egypt, built by the Kushites."]},{"n":["aksum","axum"],"s":["Aksum was a powerful ancient kingdom in what is now Ethiopia and Eritrea.","It flourished from about the 1st to the 7th century AD.","It traded with Rome, Persia and India.","It minted its own coins.","It adopted Christianity in the 4th century.","Its giant stone obelisks still stand."]},{"n":["ethiopia"],"s":["Ethiopia is a country in the Horn of Africa, one of the oldest nations in the world.","It traces its history back thousands of years.","Its capital is Addis Ababa.","It was never colonised, apart from an Italian occupation from 1936 to 1941.","It defeated Italy at the Battle of Adwa in 1896.","Coffee is thought to originate there."]},{"n":["scramble for africa"],"s":["The Scramble for Africa was the rapid colonisation of Africa by European powers.","It happened mostly between 1881 and 1914.","The Berlin Conference of 1884 to 1885 set rules for claiming land.","By 1914, only Ethiopia and Liberia were independent.","Borders were drawn with little regard for local peoples.","Most African countries won independence in the 1950s and 1960s."]},{"n":["shaka zulu","zulu"],"s":["Shaka was the founder of the Zulu Kingdom in southern Africa.","He lived from about 1787 to 1828.","He reorganised the Zulu army with new tactics.","He introduced a short stabbing spear.","He built a powerful kingdom.","The Zulu later defeated the British at Isandlwana in 1879."]},{"n":["mughal empire","mughals","akbar"],"s":["The Mughal Empire ruled most of the Indian subcontinent.","It lasted from 1526 to 1857.","It was founded by Babur, a descendant of Genghis Khan.","Akbar the Great was famed for religious tolerance.","Shah Jahan built the Taj Mahal.","It declined as British power grew."]},{"n":["khmer empire"],"s":["The Khmer Empire was a powerful state in South-East Asia.","It lasted from 802 to 1431.","Its capital was Angkor, in Cambodia.","It built Angkor Wat.","It had sophisticated water management systems."]},{"n":["tang dynasty"],"s":["The Tang dynasty is often seen as a golden age of Chinese civilisation.","It ruled from 618 to 907.","Its capital, Chang’an, was one of the largest cities in the world.","Poetry flourished, with poets like Li Bai and Du Fu.","Woodblock printing developed.","Wu Zetian, China’s only female emperor, ruled during this period."]},{"n":["ming dynasty","forbidden city"],"s":["The Ming dynasty ruled China, and built the Forbidden City in Beijing.","It ruled from 1368 to 1644.","The Forbidden City was completed in 1420.","It has hundreds of buildings.","Ming porcelain is world-famous.","Much of the Great Wall seen today was built under the Ming."]},{"n":["qing dynasty","last emperor","puyi"],"s":["The Qing was China’s last imperial dynasty.","It ruled from 1644 to 1912.","It was founded by the Manchus.","It lost wars to Britain, France and Japan.","The last emperor, Puyi, abdicated in 1912 at age six.","China then became a republic."]},{"n":["sun yat-sen"],"s":["Sun Yat-sen was a Chinese revolutionary and the first president of the Republic of China.","He lived from 1866 to 1925.","He led the movement that overthrew the Qing dynasty.","He is called the “Father of the Nation” in Taiwan.","He is honoured in mainland China too."]},{"n":["tiananmen square","tiananmen"],"s":["Tiananmen Square is a large public square in Beijing.","In 1989 it was the centre of pro-democracy protests.","Students and workers gathered there for weeks.","The government declared martial law.","On 3 and 4 June 1989 the army cleared the protests, killing many people.","The image of “Tank Man” became world-famous."]},{"n":["american revolution heroes","paul revere"],"s":["Paul Revere was an American silversmith and patriot.","He lived from 1735 to 1818.","On 18 April 1775 he rode to warn that British troops were coming.","He did not actually shout “The British are coming!”","A poem by Longfellow made his ride famous."]},{"n":["louisiana purchase"],"s":["The Louisiana Purchase was the United States’ purchase of a vast territory from France.","It was made in 1803.","It doubled the size of the US.","The price was $15 million, about four cents an acre.","Napoleon sold it to fund his wars."]},{"n":["trail of tears"],"s":["The Trail of Tears was the forced removal of Native American nations from their lands in the south-eastern US.","It took place mainly between 1830 and 1850.","It followed the Indian Removal Act of 1830.","The Cherokee, Muscogee, Seminole, Chickasaw and Choctaw were forced west.","Thousands died from cold, disease and hunger."]},{"n":["native americans","first nations","indigenous americans"],"s":["Native Americans are the Indigenous peoples of the Americas.","Their ancestors arrived at least 15,000 years ago.","Hundreds of distinct nations developed, with many languages.","European diseases killed a large share after 1492.","Wars and forced removals took much of their land.","Many nations keep their cultures and governments today."]},{"n":["sitting bull","little bighorn","crazy horse"],"s":["Sitting Bull was a Hunkpapa Lakota leader.","He lived from about 1831 to 1890.","He led resistance to US government policies.","Lakota and Cheyenne warriors defeated Custer at the Little Bighorn in 1876.","Crazy Horse was a leading war chief there.","Sitting Bull was killed by police in 1890."]},{"n":["mexican revolution","pancho villa","emiliano zapata"],"s":["The Mexican Revolution was a long armed struggle in Mexico.","It lasted from 1910 to about 1920.","It began against the dictator Porfirio Díaz.","Leaders included Pancho Villa and Emiliano Zapata.","Zapata fought for land for poor farmers.","It led to the constitution of 1917."]},{"n":["canada history","confederation"],"s":["Canada became a self-governing country through Confederation.","Confederation happened on 1 July 1867, now Canada Day.","It began with four provinces.","Sir John A. Macdonald was its first prime minister.","It gained full legal independence in 1982."]},{"n":["australian history","first fleet"],"s":["Modern Australian history began with British colonisation.","The First Fleet arrived at Sydney Cove on 26 January 1788.","It carried convicts and marines.","About 160,000 convicts were sent to Australia.","The colonies federated in 1901.","Aboriginal peoples suffered dispossession and violence."]},{"n":["new zealand","aotearoa","treaty of waitangi"],"s":["New Zealand is an island country in the South Pacific.","Māori settled it around 1300.","Its Māori name is Aotearoa.","The Treaty of Waitangi was signed in 1840.","It was the first country to give women the vote, in 1893.","Its capital is Wellington."]},{"n":["wars of the roses"],"s":["The Wars of the Roses were English civil wars between the houses of Lancaster and York.","They lasted from 1455 to 1487.","The red rose stood for Lancaster, the white for York.","Richard III was killed at Bosworth in 1485.","Henry VII founded the Tudor dynasty.","Richard III’s skeleton was found under a car park in 2012."]},{"n":["tudors","tudor"],"s":["The Tudors were a royal dynasty that ruled England.","They ruled from 1485 to 1603.","They began with Henry VII.","Henry VIII broke with the Catholic Church.","Elizabeth I was the last Tudor.","Their era saw the English Reformation and Renaissance."]},{"n":["scotland"],"s":["Scotland is a country that is part of the United Kingdom.","It united with England in 1707.","Its capital is Edinburgh.","It has its own legal system and parliament.","It voted to stay in the UK in a 2014 referendum.","Its inventors gave the world the telephone, TV and penicillin."]},{"n":["wales"],"s":["Wales is a country that is part of the United Kingdom.","It was conquered by England in the 13th century.","Its capital is Cardiff.","Welsh is one of Europe’s oldest living languages.","It has its own parliament, the Senedd."]},{"n":["northern ireland","the troubles"],"s":["Northern Ireland is part of the United Kingdom, and the Troubles were a conflict there.","The Troubles lasted from the late 1960s to 1998.","It was a conflict between mainly Catholic nationalists and mainly Protestant unionists.","About 3,500 people were killed.","The Good Friday Agreement of 1998 largely ended it."]},{"n":["french resistance"],"s":["The French Resistance fought Nazi occupation in France.","It was active from 1940 to 1944.","Members sabotaged railways and gathered intelligence.","Many were women.","They helped the Allies during D-Day."]},{"n":["marshall plan"],"s":["The Marshall Plan was an American program to rebuild Western Europe after the Second World War.","It ran from 1948 to 1952.","The US gave over $13 billion.","It was named after Secretary of State George Marshall.","It helped Europe recover quickly."]},{"n":["boudica","boudicca"],"s":["Boudica was a queen of the Iceni tribe in Britain who led a revolt against Roman rule.","Her revolt took place around AD 60 or 61.","Romans had flogged her and seized her lands.","Her army burned Colchester, London and St Albans.","She was finally defeated by the Roman governor Suetonius.","A statue of her stands near the Houses of Parliament."]},{"n":["hatshepsut"],"s":["Hatshepsut was one of the few women to rule ancient Egypt as pharaoh.","She reigned from about 1479 to 1458 BC.","She was often shown with a false beard, as pharaohs traditionally were.","Her reign was peaceful and prosperous.","She sent a trading expedition to the land of Punt.","Later rulers tried to erase her from history."]},{"n":["nefertiti"],"s":["Nefertiti was a queen of ancient Egypt, wife of the pharaoh Akhenaten.","She lived in the 14th century BC.","Her painted bust in Berlin is one of the most famous artworks of the ancient world.","She and Akhenaten promoted worship of the sun disk, the Aten.","Her final fate is uncertain."]},{"n":["akhenaten"],"s":["Akhenaten was an Egyptian pharaoh who tried to change Egypt’s religion.","He ruled from about 1353 to 1336 BC.","He promoted worship of one god, the Aten, the sun disk.","He built a new capital at Amarna.","His changes were reversed after his death.","He was probably the father of Tutankhamun."]},{"n":["wu zetian"],"s":["Wu Zetian was the only woman to rule China as emperor in her own right.","She ruled from 690 to 705.","She rose from concubine to empress.","She expanded the civil service exams.","She supported Buddhism.","Traditional historians portrayed her harshly."]},{"n":["eleanor of aquitaine"],"s":["Eleanor of Aquitaine was one of the most powerful women of the Middle Ages.","She lived from about 1122 to 1204.","She was queen of France and then of England.","She went on the Second Crusade.","Two of her sons, Richard the Lionheart and John, became kings of England."]},{"n":["queen isabella","isabella of castile","ferdinand and isabella"],"s":["Isabella I of Castile and Ferdinand II of Aragon united Spain through their marriage.","Isabella lived from 1451 to 1504.","They completed the conquest of Granada in 1492.","They sponsored Columbus’s voyage the same year.","They expelled Jews from Spain in 1492.","They set up the Spanish Inquisition."]},{"n":["mary queen of scots"],"s":["Mary, Queen of Scots, ruled Scotland in the 16th century.","She lived from 1542 to 1587.","She became queen at six days old.","She was forced to abdicate in 1567 and fled to England.","Elizabeth I held her captive for 19 years.","She was executed in 1587 for plotting against Elizabeth.","Her son, James VI, later united the crowns of Scotland and England."]},{"n":["eleanor roosevelt"],"s":["Eleanor Roosevelt was an American First Lady, diplomat and activist.","She lived from 1884 to 1962.","She was married to Franklin D. Roosevelt.","She held her own press conferences and wrote a newspaper column.","She chaired the committee that drafted the Universal Declaration of Human Rights."]},{"n":["indira gandhi"],"s":["Indira Gandhi was India’s first and only female prime minister.","She lived from 1917 to 1984.","She was the daughter of Nehru.","She declared a state of Emergency from 1975 to 1977.","She was assassinated by her bodyguards in 1984."]},{"n":["golda meir"],"s":["Golda Meir was Prime Minister of Israel.","She lived from 1898 to 1978 and served from 1969 to 1974.","She grew up in the United States.","She was one of the world’s first female heads of government.","She led Israel during the Yom Kippur War in 1973."]},{"n":["malala yousafzai","malala"],"s":["Malala Yousafzai is a Pakistani activist for girls’ education.","She was born in 1997.","She was shot by the Taliban in 2012 for speaking out.","She survived and continued campaigning.","In 2014 she became the youngest Nobel Peace Prize laureate, at 17."]},{"n":["mother teresa"],"s":["Mother Teresa was a Catholic nun who cared for the poor in India.","She lived from 1910 to 1997.","She was born in what is now North Macedonia.","She founded the Missionaries of Charity in Kolkata in 1950.","She won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1979.","She was declared a saint in 2016."]},{"n":["helen keller"],"s":["Helen Keller was an American author and activist who was deaf and blind.","She lived from 1880 to 1968.","She lost her sight and hearing after an illness as a baby.","Her teacher, Anne Sullivan, taught her to communicate.","She was the first deafblind person to earn a bachelor’s degree.","She campaigned for disability rights and women’s suffrage."]},{"n":["sacagawea"],"s":["Sacagawea was a Lemhi Shoshone woman who helped the Lewis and Clark expedition.","She lived from about 1788 to 1812.","She served as interpreter and guide.","She travelled with her baby son.","Her presence signalled to other nations that the expedition was peaceful."]},{"n":["pocahontas"],"s":["Pocahontas was a Powhatan woman linked to the early English colony at Jamestown.","She lived from about 1596 to 1617.","The story that she saved John Smith may be exaggerated.","She married the colonist John Rolfe.","She travelled to England, where she died."]},{"n":["spartacus"],"s":["Spartacus was a gladiator who led a major slave revolt against Rome.","The revolt lasted from 73 to 71 BC.","He escaped from a gladiator school in Capua.","His army grew to perhaps 70,000 or more.","He defeated several Roman armies.","He was killed in battle, and 6,000 captured rebels were crucified along the Appian Way."]},{"n":["hannibal elephants"],"s":["Hannibal took war elephants across the Alps to invade Italy.","He crossed in 218 BC.","He started with around 37 elephants.","Most died in the cold or in the following months.","He still won huge victories in Italy."]},{"n":["genghis khan descendants","timur","tamerlane"],"s":["Timur, known in the West as Tamerlane, was a Turco-Mongol conqueror.","He lived from 1336 to 1405.","He built an empire across Central Asia, Persia and beyond.","His capital was Samarkand.","His campaigns were extremely brutal.","His descendant Babur founded the Mughal Empire."]},{"n":["vlad the impaler","dracula"],"s":["Vlad III, known as Vlad the Impaler, was a prince of Wallachia, in modern Romania.","He lived from about 1431 to 1476.","He was famous for impaling his enemies.","He fought the Ottoman Empire.","His family name, Dracula, inspired Bram Stoker’s vampire novel of 1897."]},{"n":["blackbeard","pirates","golden age of piracy"],"s":["Blackbeard was one of the most famous pirates of the Golden Age of Piracy.","He died in 1718; the golden age lasted roughly from 1650 to 1730.","His real name was probably Edward Teach.","He reportedly tied lit fuses in his beard to scare enemies.","He blockaded Charleston harbour in 1718.","He was killed in battle by the Royal Navy."]},{"n":["nostradamus"],"s":["Nostradamus was a French astrologer famous for prophecies.","He lived from 1503 to 1566.","He published Les Prophéties in 1555.","His verses are vague enough to be matched to many events.","He was also a physician who treated plague victims."]},{"n":["dinosaurs","dinosaur","t rex","tyrannosaurus"],"s":["Dinosaurs were reptiles that dominated life on land for over 160 million years.","They lived from about 230 to 66 million years ago.","Their era is divided into the Triassic, Jurassic and Cretaceous periods.","Tyrannosaurus rex lived near the very end, about 68 to 66 million years ago.","Some, like Argentinosaurus, were among the largest land animals ever.","Birds are living descendants of dinosaurs.","Many dinosaurs had feathers.","The word dinosaur means “terrible lizard”, coined by Richard Owen in 1842.","An asteroid impact near Mexico about 66 million years ago wiped out the non-bird dinosaurs."]},{"n":["asteroid impact","chicxulub","extinction of the dinosaurs"],"s":["The Chicxulub impact was the asteroid strike that ended the age of dinosaurs.","It happened about 66 million years ago.","The asteroid was about 10 kilometres wide.","It hit what is now Mexico’s Yucatán Peninsula.","It caused fires, tsunamis and years of darkness.","About three-quarters of all species died out.","Mammals then spread and diversified."]},{"n":["stone age","paleolithic","neolithic"],"s":["The Stone Age was the long period when humans made tools mainly from stone.","It began over 3 million years ago and ended with the use of metals, around 3000 BC in many places.","It is divided into the Paleolithic (Old), Mesolithic (Middle) and Neolithic (New) Stone Ages.","Paleolithic people were hunter-gatherers.","They made cave paintings, like those at Lascaux in France.","The Neolithic began with farming, around 10,000 BC in the Middle East.","Farming led to permanent villages and eventually cities."]},{"n":["ice age","last ice age","woolly mammoth","mammoths"],"s":["An ice age is a long period when ice sheets cover large parts of the Earth.","The last glacial period ended about 11,700 years ago.","Ice sheets covered much of North America and northern Europe.","Sea levels were about 120 metres lower than today.","Woolly mammoths, cave bears and sabre-toothed cats lived then.","Some mammoths survived on Wrangel Island until about 4,000 years ago, after the pyramids were built.","Humans crossed from Asia to the Americas over a land bridge."]},{"n":["early humans","human evolution","homo sapiens","neanderthals","neanderthal"],"s":["Human evolution is the story of how modern humans developed from earlier species.","Homo sapiens appeared in Africa about 300,000 years ago.","Earlier relatives include Australopithecus, like the famous “Lucy”.","Homo erectus was the first to spread out of Africa, about 1.9 million years ago.","Neanderthals lived in Europe and Asia until about 40,000 years ago.","Many people today carry a little Neanderthal DNA.","The oldest known human art includes cave paintings in Indonesia over 40,000 years old.","Modern humans spread across the world from Africa."]},{"n":["lucy","australopithecus"],"s":["Lucy is a famous fossil of an early human relative, Australopithecus afarensis.","She lived about 3.2 million years ago.","She was found in Ethiopia in 1974.","She walked upright but had a small brain.","She was named after the Beatles song Lucy in the Sky with Diamonds."]},{"n":["bronze age","iron age"],"s":["The Bronze Age and Iron Age are periods named after the metals people used.","The Bronze Age began around 3300 BC in the Middle East; the Iron Age followed from about 1200 BC.","Bronze is a mix of copper and tin.","Bronze Age civilisations include Egypt, Mesopotamia and the Minoans.","Many Bronze Age societies collapsed around 1200 to 1150 BC.","Iron was more common than tin, so iron tools spread widely."]},{"n":["lascaux","cave paintings"],"s":["The Lascaux caves in France contain some of the most famous prehistoric paintings.","They are about 17,000 years old.","They show horses, bulls, deer and other animals.","They were discovered by teenagers in 1940.","The caves were closed to the public in 1963 to protect the art."]},{"n":["zeus","greek gods","greek mythology","olympians","mount olympus"],"s":["Zeus was the king of the gods in Greek mythology.","Greek myths were told from at least the 8th century BC.","He ruled from Mount Olympus and threw thunderbolts.","The twelve Olympians included Hera, Poseidon, Athena, Apollo, Artemis, Ares, Aphrodite, Hermes, Hephaestus, Demeter and Dionysus (or Hestia).","Zeus overthrew his father, Cronus, and the Titans.","The Romans called him Jupiter.","Greek myths still shape stories, art and language today."]},{"n":["hercules","heracles","twelve labours"],"s":["Heracles, called Hercules by the Romans, was the greatest hero of Greek myth.","His stories were told in ancient Greece from early times.","He was the son of Zeus and a mortal woman.","He performed twelve labours, like slaying the Nemean lion and the Hydra.","He was famed for his great strength.","He became a god after his death."]},{"n":["odin","thor","norse mythology","norse gods","valhalla","loki"],"s":["Norse mythology is the mythology of the Vikings and other Scandinavian peoples.","Most of what we know was written down in Iceland in the 13th century.","Odin was the chief god, wise and one-eyed.","Thor was the god of thunder, wielding the hammer Mjölnir.","Loki was a trickster god.","Warriors who died in battle went to Valhalla, Odin’s hall.","Ragnarök was the prophesied end of the world."]},{"n":["egyptian gods","osiris","anubis","isis"],"s":["The ancient Egyptians worshipped many gods.","Their religion lasted for around 3,000 years.","Ra was the sun god.","Osiris was god of the dead and the afterlife.","Anubis, with a jackal’s head, guided souls and protected tombs.","Isis was a powerful goddess of magic and motherhood.","The dead were judged by weighing their heart against a feather."]},{"n":["medusa"],"s":["Medusa was a monster in Greek myth, a Gorgon with snakes for hair.","Her story is told in ancient Greek myth.","Anyone who looked at her turned to stone.","The hero Perseus cut off her head, using a shield as a mirror.","The winged horse Pegasus sprang from her blood."]},{"n":["atlantis"],"s":["Atlantis is a legendary island described by Plato.","Plato wrote about it around 360 BC.","He said it sank into the sea in a single day and night.","It was probably invented as a moral story.","No evidence of a real Atlantis has been found.","The eruption of Thera near Crete may have inspired the idea."]},{"n":["minoans","knossos","minotaur"],"s":["The Minoans were a Bronze Age civilisation on the island of Crete.","They flourished from about 3000 to 1100 BC.","Their great palace was at Knossos.","They were named after the legendary King Minos.","In myth, the Minotaur, half man and half bull, lived in a labyrinth there.","Their script, Linear A, has not been deciphered."]},{"n":["stonehenge builders","druids","celts"],"s":["The Celts were peoples who lived across much of Europe in the Iron Age.","Celtic culture flourished from around 800 BC.","Druids were their priests and teachers.","They left few written records of their own.","Celtic languages survive as Irish, Scottish Gaelic, Welsh and Breton.","Stonehenge is far older than the Celts."]},{"n":["phoenicians"],"s":["The Phoenicians were seafaring traders from the coast of modern Lebanon.","They flourished from about 1500 to 300 BC.","They developed an alphabet that inspired Greek and Latin letters.","They founded Carthage.","They were famous for a costly purple dye."]},{"n":["assyria","assyrians"],"s":["Assyria was a powerful empire in ancient Mesopotamia.","It was at its height from the 9th to the 7th century BC.","Its capital was Nineveh.","It was famous for a ruthless army.","King Ashurbanipal built a great library.","It fell in 612 BC."]},{"n":["babylon","nebuchadnezzar","hanging gardens"],"s":["Babylon was a great city of ancient Mesopotamia.","It flourished under Hammurabi and later under Nebuchadnezzar II, who ruled from 605 to 562 BC.","Nebuchadnezzar rebuilt it with the blue Ishtar Gate.","He captured Jerusalem and exiled many Jews to Babylon.","The Hanging Gardens of Babylon may be a legend.","Cyrus the Great took the city in 539 BC."]},{"n":["olmecs"],"s":["The Olmecs were the earliest known major civilisation of Mexico.","They flourished from about 1200 to 400 BC.","They carved giant stone heads.","They may have played an early form of the Mesoamerican ballgame.","They influenced the Maya and later cultures."]},{"n":["moai","easter island","rapa nui"],"s":["The moai are giant stone statues on Easter Island, Rapa Nui, in the Pacific.","They were carved mostly between about 1250 and 1500.","There are nearly 1,000 of them.","They represent ancestors.","Islanders probably “walked” them upright with ropes."]},{"n":["battle of marathon","marathon"],"s":["The Battle of Marathon was an Athenian victory over a Persian invasion.","It was fought in 490 BC.","The Athenians were outnumbered but won decisively.","Legend says a runner, Pheidippides, ran about 40 kilometres to Athens with the news and then died.","The modern marathon race is based on that legend.","It boosted Greek confidence against Persia."]},{"n":["battle of salamis","salamis"],"s":["The Battle of Salamis was a naval battle in which the Greeks defeated the Persian fleet.","It was fought in 480 BC.","The Athenian leader Themistocles lured the Persians into narrow straits.","The smaller Greek triremes rammed the crowded Persian ships.","Xerxes watched the defeat from the shore.","It saved Greece from Persian conquest."]},{"n":["battle of gaugamela","gaugamela"],"s":["The Battle of Gaugamela was Alexander the Great’s decisive victory over Persia.","It was fought in 331 BC, in modern Iraq.","Alexander faced a much larger army under Darius III.","He led a cavalry charge at Darius, who fled.","It effectively ended the Persian Empire."]},{"n":["battle of cannae","cannae"],"s":["The Battle of Cannae was Hannibal’s greatest victory over Rome.","It was fought in 216 BC in southern Italy.","Hannibal surrounded a much larger Roman army in a double envelopment.","Tens of thousands of Romans were killed in one day.","It is still studied in military academies."]},{"n":["battle of actium","actium"],"s":["The Battle of Actium was the naval battle that made Octavian master of Rome.","It was fought on 2 September 31 BC, off western Greece.","Octavian’s fleet, led by Agrippa, faced Antony and Cleopatra.","Cleopatra’s ships fled, and Antony followed.","It led to the end of the Republic and the start of the Empire."]},{"n":["battle of tours","charles martel"],"s":["The Battle of Tours was a Frankish victory over an Umayyad army.","It was fought in 732, in France.","Charles Martel led the Franks.","It halted the northward advance of Muslim forces from Spain into France.","Charles became known as “the Hammer”."]},{"n":["battle of trafalgar","trafalgar","horatio nelson","nelson"],"s":["The Battle of Trafalgar was a decisive British naval victory over France and Spain.","It was fought on 21 October 1805, off south-west Spain.","Admiral Horatio Nelson led the British fleet.","He signalled, “England expects that every man will do his duty.”","Nelson was killed by a sniper during the battle.","It ended Napoleon’s hopes of invading Britain.","Nelson’s Column in London’s Trafalgar Square honours him."]},{"n":["battle of gettysburg","gettysburg"],"s":["The Battle of Gettysburg was a turning point of the American Civil War.","It was fought from 1 to 3 July 1863, in Pennsylvania.","Union forces under George Meade defeated Robert E. Lee’s Confederate army.","Pickett’s Charge on the last day was a disastrous Confederate attack.","About 50,000 soldiers were killed, wounded, captured or missing.","Lincoln gave the Gettysburg Address there that November."]},{"n":["battle of midway","midway"],"s":["The Battle of Midway was a decisive US naval victory over Japan.","It was fought from 4 to 7 June 1942.","US codebreakers learned Japan’s plans in advance.","American aircraft sank four Japanese aircraft carriers.","It turned the tide of the Pacific War."]},{"n":["battle of the bulge"],"s":["The Battle of the Bulge was Germany’s last major offensive in the west.","It lasted from December 1944 to January 1945.","Germans attacked through the Ardennes forest in Belgium.","It created a “bulge” in the Allied line.","American troops held out at Bastogne.","It was the largest battle fought by the US Army in the war."]},{"n":["dunkirk","dunkirk evacuation"],"s":["The Dunkirk evacuation rescued Allied soldiers trapped in northern France.","It took place from 26 May to 4 June 1940.","About 338,000 British and French troops were rescued.","A flotilla of small civilian boats helped.","Churchill called it a “miracle of deliverance”.","It let Britain fight on."]},{"n":["el alamein"],"s":["The Battles of El Alamein were fought in Egypt during the Second World War.","The decisive second battle was in October and November 1942.","British forces under Montgomery defeated Rommel’s German and Italian army.","It ended the Axis threat to Egypt and the Suez Canal.","Churchill said, “Before Alamein we never had a victory. After Alamein we never had a defeat.”"]},{"n":["battle of bannockburn","bannockburn"],"s":["The Battle of Bannockburn was a decisive Scottish victory over England.","It was fought on 23 and 24 June 1314.","Robert the Bruce led the Scots.","They defeated Edward II’s larger army.","It secured Bruce’s position as king."]},{"n":["battle of lepanto","lepanto"],"s":["The Battle of Lepanto was a naval battle between a Catholic alliance and the Ottoman fleet.","It was fought on 7 October 1571, off Greece.","The Holy League won decisively.","It was one of the last great battles of oared galleys.","Miguel de Cervantes was wounded there."]},{"n":["siege of vienna"],"s":["The Ottoman Empire besieged Vienna twice.","The sieges took place in 1529 and 1683.","Both times the city held out.","In 1683 a relief army led by Polish king Jan Sobieski won a decisive victory.","It marked the high-water mark of Ottoman expansion in Europe."]},{"n":["battle of hastings date","stamford bridge"],"s":["The Battle of Stamford Bridge was fought weeks before Hastings.","It took place on 25 September 1066.","King Harold II defeated a Norwegian army under Harald Hardrada.","Hardrada was killed.","It is often seen as the end of the Viking Age."]},{"n":["battle of thermopylae date","xerxes"],"s":["Xerxes I was the Persian king who invaded Greece.","He ruled from 486 to 465 BC.","He bridged the Hellespont with boats.","He won at Thermopylae but lost at Salamis.","He also built much of the palace at Persepolis."]},{"n":["pearl harbor date","yamamoto"],"s":["Isoroku Yamamoto was the Japanese admiral who planned the attack on Pearl Harbor.","He lived from 1884 to 1943.","He had studied in the United States and feared a long war with it.","He also planned the Midway operation.","The US shot down his plane in 1943."]},{"n":["battle of austerlitz","austerlitz"],"s":["The Battle of Austerlitz was one of Napoleon’s greatest victories.","It was fought on 2 December 1805.","It is called the Battle of the Three Emperors.","Napoleon defeated Russia and Austria.","He lured the allies into attacking his weakened right flank."]},{"n":["battle of kursk","kursk"],"s":["The Battle of Kursk was the largest tank battle in history.","It was fought in July and August 1943, in the Soviet Union.","Germany attacked a Soviet salient.","The Soviets had prepared deep defences.","Germany never again launched a major offensive in the east."]},{"n":["battle of agincourt date","henry v"],"s":["Henry V was the English king who won at Agincourt.","He lived from 1386 to 1422.","He conquered much of northern France.","He married Catherine of Valois, daughter of the French king.","He died of illness at 35."]},{"n":["battle of yorktown","yorktown"],"s":["The Siege of Yorktown was the last major battle of the American Revolutionary War.","It ended on 19 October 1781.","American and French forces trapped a British army under Cornwallis.","A French fleet blocked British escape by sea.","The surrender led to peace talks."]},{"n":["battle of lexington","lexington and concord","shot heard round the world"],"s":["The Battles of Lexington and Concord began the American Revolutionary War.","They were fought on 19 April 1775.","British troops marched to seize colonial weapons.","Militiamen confronted them.","The first shot became known as “the shot heard round the world”."]},{"n":["pacific ocean"],"s":["The Pacific is the largest and deepest ocean on Earth.","Magellan named it in 1520, meaning “peaceful”.","It covers about a third of Earth’s surface.","It is larger than all the land on Earth combined.","The Mariana Trench, its deepest point, is about 11,000 metres deep.","The “Ring of Fire” of volcanoes and earthquakes surrounds it."]},{"n":["atlantic ocean"],"s":["The Atlantic is the second-largest ocean.","It began opening as Pangaea broke apart around 180 million years ago.","It separates the Americas from Europe and Africa.","It is still widening by a few centimetres each year.","The Gulf Stream carries warm water across it to Europe."]},{"n":["mariana trench","challenger deep"],"s":["The Mariana Trench is the deepest part of the world’s oceans.","Its deepest point, the Challenger Deep, was first reached by people in 1960.","It lies in the western Pacific.","It is about 11,000 metres deep.","Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh dived there in the bathyscaphe Trieste.","The film director James Cameron dived solo there in 2012."]},{"n":["amazon rainforest","amazon river","the amazon"],"s":["The Amazon is the world’s largest rainforest and one of its greatest rivers.","The forest has existed for tens of millions of years.","It covers much of South America, mostly in Brazil.","It is home to around 10 per cent of all known species.","The Amazon River carries more water than any other river.","Deforestation threatens large parts of it."]},{"n":["nile","river nile","nile river"],"s":["The Nile is one of the longest rivers in the world.","It has supported civilisation in Egypt for over 5,000 years.","It flows north about 6,650 kilometres into the Mediterranean.","Its two main branches are the White Nile and the Blue Nile.","Its yearly flood made Egyptian farming possible.","The Aswan High Dam, finished in 1970, now controls the flood."]},{"n":["sahara","sahara desert"],"s":["The Sahara is the largest hot desert in the world.","It became a desert around 5,000 years ago; before that it was much greener.","It covers much of North Africa.","It is almost as large as the United States.","Only part of it is sand dunes; much is rock and gravel.","Ancient rock art shows giraffes and hippos there."]},{"n":["antarctica continent","antarctic","south pole continent"],"s":["Antarctica is the coldest, windiest and driest continent.","It was first sighted in 1820.","It is covered by an ice sheet up to about 4.8 kilometres thick.","It holds about 70 per cent of the world’s fresh water.","The coldest natural temperature on Earth, −89.2 °C, was recorded there in 1983.","It has no permanent residents, only researchers.","The Antarctic Treaty of 1959 reserves it for peace and science."]},{"n":["pangaea","continental drift","plate tectonics"],"s":["Pangaea was a supercontinent that joined almost all of Earth’s land.","It existed about 335 to 175 million years ago.","Alfred Wegener proposed continental drift in 1912.","Plate tectonics later explained how continents move.","The plates move a few centimetres a year.","Their movement causes earthquakes, volcanoes and mountains."]},{"n":["continents","seven continents"],"s":["Continents are Earth’s main large landmasses.","They formed over hundreds of millions of years of plate movement.","They are usually counted as seven: Asia, Africa, North America, South America, Antarctica, Europe and Australia (Oceania).","Asia is the largest and most populous.","Some traditions count them differently, for example combining the Americas."]},{"n":["south america"],"s":["South America is a continent in the southern part of the Americas.","Spain and Portugal colonised most of it from the 16th century.","It has 12 independent countries.","It contains the Andes, the Amazon and the Atacama, one of the driest deserts on Earth.","Spanish and Portuguese are its main languages."]},{"n":["africa"],"s":["Africa is the second-largest continent.","It is the birthplace of humanity.","It has 54 countries.","It has about 1.4 billion people.","It is home to the Sahara and the Nile.","It has thousands of languages."]},{"n":["asia"],"s":["Asia is the largest and most populous continent.","Some of the oldest civilisations began there.","It has about 4.7 billion people, around 60 per cent of the world.","It contains Mount Everest.","It is home to China and India, the two most populous countries."]},{"n":["europe"],"s":["Europe is a continent in the western part of Eurasia.","Its history includes ancient Greece and Rome, the Renaissance and the Industrial Revolution.","It has around 44 countries.","The European Union unites many of them.","Its colonial empires once spanned the globe."]},{"n":["mount kilimanjaro","kilimanjaro"],"s":["Mount Kilimanjaro is the highest mountain in Africa.","It was first climbed in 1889.","It is in Tanzania.","It is about 5,895 metres tall.","It is a dormant volcano.","It has three volcanic cones: Kibo, Mawenzi and Shira.","Its glaciers are shrinking fast."]},{"n":["grand canyon"],"s":["The Grand Canyon is a huge gorge in Arizona.","The Colorado River carved it over about 5 to 6 million years.","It is about 446 kilometres long.","It is up to about 1.8 kilometres deep.","Its rocks show nearly two billion years of Earth’s history."]},{"n":["great barrier reef"],"s":["The Great Barrier Reef is the world’s largest coral reef system.","Its current form is around 8,000 years old.","It lies off north-east Australia.","It stretches over 2,300 kilometres.","It is home to thousands of species.","It is the largest living structure on Earth, visible from space.","Warming seas have caused mass coral bleaching."]},{"n":["mediterranean","mediterranean sea"],"s":["The Mediterranean is a sea surrounded by Europe, Africa and Asia.","It was the centre of the ancient Western world.","Romans called it Mare Nostrum, “our sea”.","It connects to the Atlantic through the Strait of Gibraltar.","It almost dried up completely about 5.5 million years ago."]},{"n":["volcanoes","volcano","krakatoa"],"s":["Volcanoes are openings in Earth’s crust where molten rock erupts.","The 1883 eruption of Krakatoa in Indonesia was one of the deadliest.","Krakatoa’s explosion was heard nearly 5,000 kilometres away.","The 1815 eruption of Tambora caused a “year without a summer” in 1816.","Most volcanoes lie along tectonic plate boundaries."]},{"n":["yellowstone"],"s":["Yellowstone was the world’s first national park.","It was established in 1872.","It is mostly in Wyoming, in the US.","It sits on top of a huge volcanic hotspot.","It has the geyser Old Faithful.","It is home to bison, wolves and bears."]},{"n":["world population","population of the world","how many people"],"s":["The world’s population is around 8 billion people.","It passed 8 billion in November 2022.","It was about 1 billion around 1800.","It reached 2 billion around 1927.","Growth sped up with better medicine and farming.","India and China are the most populous countries."]},{"n":["languages","most spoken language","english language"],"s":["There are about 7,000 languages spoken in the world.","English developed from Germanic dialects brought to Britain from the 5th century.","English and Mandarin Chinese have the most speakers overall.","Mandarin has the most native speakers.","English took many words from French after 1066 and from Latin.","Many languages are endangered."]},{"n":["calendar","gregorian calendar","julian calendar"],"s":["The Gregorian calendar is the calendar used in most of the world.","Pope Gregory XIII introduced it in 1582.","It replaced the Julian calendar, set up by Julius Caesar.","The Julian calendar drifted by about one day every 128 years.","Countries switching had to skip days; Britain skipped 11 days in 1752.","Russia only switched in 1918."]},{"n":["space exploration","mars rover","curiosity rover","perseverance"],"s":["Space exploration has sent robots across the solar system.","The first Mars landers touched down in the 1970s.","NASA’s Viking 1 landed on Mars in 1976.","Curiosity has explored Mars since 2012.","Perseverance landed in 2021, with a small helicopter, Ingenuity.","Ingenuity made the first powered flight on another planet."]},{"n":["james webb","james webb space telescope","jwst"],"s":["The James Webb Space Telescope is the most powerful space telescope ever built.","It launched on 25 December 2021.","It observes mainly in infrared light.","Its gold-coated mirror is 6.5 metres across.","It orbits the Sun about 1.5 million kilometres from Earth.","It can see some of the earliest galaxies."]},{"n":["climate change","global warming"],"s":["Climate change is the long-term warming of Earth, driven mainly by human activity.","Scientists warned about the greenhouse effect as early as the 19th century.","Burning fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide, which traps heat.","Earth has warmed by about 1.1 to 1.2 °C since pre-industrial times.","Effects include rising seas, heatwaves and melting ice.","Scientist Svante Arrhenius calculated the warming effect of carbon dioxide in 1896.","The Paris Agreement of 2015 aims to limit warming to well below 2 °C."]},{"n":["nuclear power"],"s":["Nuclear power generates electricity by splitting atoms.","The first nuclear power plant to supply a grid opened in the Soviet Union in 1954.","It produces very little carbon dioxide.","Its waste stays radioactive for a very long time.","Major accidents include Chernobyl in 1986 and Fukushima in 2011."]},{"n":["fukushima"],"s":["The Fukushima disaster was a nuclear accident in Japan.","It began on 11 March 2011.","A huge earthquake and tsunami knocked out the plant’s cooling systems.","Three reactors suffered meltdowns.","The tsunami itself killed nearly 20,000 people.","Over 100,000 people were evacuated."]},{"n":["olympics modern","modern olympics","pierre de coubertin"],"s":["The modern Olympic Games were revived by Pierre de Coubertin.","The first modern Games were held in Athens in 1896.","The Winter Olympics began in 1924.","The five rings represent the five inhabited continents.","The Paralympics began in 1960."]},{"n":["nato"],"s":["NATO is a military alliance of North American and European countries.","It was founded on 4 April 1949.","Members promise to defend each other if attacked (Article 5).","Article 5 has been invoked only once, after the September 11 attacks.","It has over 30 members."]},{"n":["google"],"s":["Google is an American technology company best known for its search engine.","It was founded in 1998 by Larry Page and Sergey Brin.","They started it as Stanford University students.","Its name comes from “googol”, the number 1 followed by 100 zeros.","It became part of Alphabet in 2015."]},{"n":["video games","first video game","pong"],"s":["Video games grew from experiments into a giant global industry.","Pong, released in 1972, was one of the first commercially successful arcade games.","Early experiments include Tennis for Two in 1958.","Home consoles spread in the 1970s and 1980s.","Super Mario Bros. (1985) is one of the best-selling games ever.","Today the industry is larger than film and music combined."]},{"n":["social media"],"s":["Social media are online platforms for sharing and connecting.","They grew rapidly in the 2000s.","Early sites included Friendster and MySpace.","Facebook launched in 2004, YouTube in 2005 and Twitter in 2006.","Billions of people now use them daily."]},{"n":["cars electric","electric cars"],"s":["Electric cars run on batteries rather than petrol.","Electric cars existed in the 1890s, before petrol cars became dominant.","They faded as cheap petrol cars spread.","Modern electric cars took off in the 2010s.","Many countries plan to phase out new petrol cars."]},{"n":["human genome project","genome"],"s":["The Human Genome Project mapped the entire human genetic code.","It ran from 1990 to 2003.","It was an international effort.","Humans have about 20,000 protein-coding genes.","It transformed medicine and biology."]},{"n":["dolly the sheep","cloning"],"s":["Dolly the sheep was the first mammal cloned from an adult cell.","She was born on 5 July 1996, in Scotland.","She was created at the Roslin Institute.","She was named after the singer Dolly Parton.","She lived until 2003."]},{"n":["large hadron collider","lhc","cern","higgs boson"],"s":["The Large Hadron Collider is the world’s largest particle accelerator.","It began operating in 2008, at CERN near Geneva.","It is a 27-kilometre ring underground.","In 2012 it found the Higgs boson.","The Higgs gives other particles mass.","Its magnets are cooled to −271 °C, colder than outer space.","The World Wide Web was also invented at CERN."]},{"n":["rasputin","grigori rasputin"],"s":["Grigori Rasputin was a Russian mystic who gained influence over the last tsar’s family.","He lived from 1869 to 1916.","He seemed to ease the bleeding of the tsar’s son, Alexei, who had haemophilia.","His influence scandalised Russian society.","He was murdered by nobles in 1916.","Legends say he survived poison and shooting before being drowned."]},{"n":["nicholas ii","tsar nicholas","romanovs","anastasia"],"s":["Nicholas II was the last Tsar of Russia.","He lived from 1868 to 1918 and reigned from 1894 to 1917.","He abdicated during the February Revolution of 1917.","He and his family were executed by Bolsheviks in July 1918.","Rumours that his daughter Anastasia survived were disproved by DNA tests."]},{"n":["ho chi minh"],"s":["Ho Chi Minh was the Vietnamese revolutionary leader of North Vietnam.","He lived from 1890 to 1969.","He declared Vietnam’s independence in 1945.","He led the fight against French colonial rule.","Saigon was renamed Ho Chi Minh City after the war."]},{"n":["pol pot","khmer rouge"],"s":["Pol Pot was the leader of the Khmer Rouge regime in Cambodia.","He lived from 1925 to 1998; the regime ruled from 1975 to 1979.","It forced people out of cities into labour camps.","Around 1.5 to 2 million people died.","Vietnam invaded and ended the regime in 1979."]},{"n":["idi amin"],"s":["Idi Amin was the military dictator of Uganda.","He ruled from 1971 to 1979.","He expelled Uganda’s Asian population in 1972.","His regime killed an estimated 100,000 to 500,000 people.","He was overthrown and died in exile in 2003."]},{"n":["kwame nkrumah"],"s":["Kwame Nkrumah led Ghana to independence.","He lived from 1909 to 1972.","Ghana became independent from Britain in 1957, one of the first sub-Saharan colonies to do so.","He was its first prime minister and president.","He championed Pan-Africanism."]},{"n":["haile selassie"],"s":["Haile Selassie was the last Emperor of Ethiopia.","He lived from 1892 to 1975 and ruled from 1930 to 1974.","He appealed to the League of Nations against Italy’s invasion in 1936.","He helped found the Organisation of African Unity.","He is revered in the Rastafari movement."]},{"n":["desmond tutu"],"s":["Desmond Tutu was a South African archbishop and anti-apartheid activist.","He lived from 1931 to 2021.","He won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1984.","He chaired the Truth and Reconciliation Commission.","He coined the phrase “Rainbow Nation”."]},{"n":["simon de montfort"],"s":["Simon de Montfort was an English noble who led a revolt against Henry III.","He lived from about 1208 to 1265.","In 1265 he called a parliament that included ordinary townsmen.","It is seen as an early step towards the House of Commons.","He was killed at the Battle of Evesham."]},{"n":["thomas more","utopia"],"s":["Sir Thomas More was an English statesman and writer.","He lived from 1478 to 1535.","He wrote Utopia, about an ideal island society.","The word “utopia” comes from his book.","He was executed for refusing to accept Henry VIII as head of the Church.","He was made a saint in 1935."]},{"n":["guy fawkes","gunpowder plot"],"s":["Guy Fawkes was one of the plotters in the Gunpowder Plot.","The plot was foiled on 5 November 1605.","Catholic conspirators planned to blow up the House of Lords and King James I.","Fawkes was caught guarding barrels of gunpowder beneath Parliament.","Bonfire Night is still celebrated in Britain on 5 November."]},{"n":["william the silent"],"s":["William the Silent led the Dutch revolt against Spain.","He lived from 1533 to 1584.","He is called the father of the Netherlands.","He was assassinated in 1584.","The Dutch national colour, orange, comes from his family, the House of Orange."]},{"n":["frederick the great"],"s":["Frederick the Great was King of Prussia.","He lived from 1712 to 1786 and reigned from 1740.","He made Prussia a major European power.","He was a talented flute player and composer.","He corresponded with Voltaire.","He encouraged potato farming."]},{"n":["maria theresa"],"s":["Maria Theresa was the only woman to rule the Habsburg lands in her own right.","She lived from 1717 to 1780 and ruled from 1740.","She reformed education and the army.","She had 16 children, including Marie Antoinette."]},{"n":["garibaldi hero","giuseppe garibaldi"],"s":["Giuseppe Garibaldi was an Italian general and hero of unification.","He lived from 1807 to 1882.","He led the “Expedition of the Thousand” to Sicily in 1860.","He also fought in South America.","He is called the “Hero of Two Worlds”."]},{"n":["karl benz"],"s":["Karl Benz was a German engineer who built the first practical automobile.","He lived from 1844 to 1929.","He patented his Motorwagen in 1886.","His company later became part of Mercedes-Benz.","His wife, Bertha, made the first long car trip to prove it worked."]},{"n":["rudolf diesel","diesel engine"],"s":["Rudolf Diesel was a German inventor of the diesel engine.","He lived from 1858 to 1913.","He patented his engine in the 1890s.","It was more efficient than steam engines.","He vanished from a ship crossing the English Channel in 1913."]},{"n":["samuel morse","morse code","telegraph"],"s":["Samuel Morse helped develop the electric telegraph and Morse code.","He lived from 1791 to 1872.","He sent the message “What hath God wrought” in 1844.","Morse code uses dots and dashes for letters.","SOS is · · · — — — · · ·.","He was also a painter."]},{"n":["george stephenson"],"s":["George Stephenson was an English engineer, the “Father of Railways”.","He lived from 1781 to 1848.","He built the Stockton and Darlington Railway in 1825.","With his son Robert he built the Rocket locomotive.","His track gauge became the world standard."]},{"n":["isambard kingdom brunel","brunel"],"s":["Isambard Kingdom Brunel was a British engineer.","He lived from 1806 to 1859.","He built the Great Western Railway.","He designed the Clifton Suspension Bridge.","His ship, the SS Great Britain, was the first iron, propeller-driven steamship to cross the Atlantic."]},{"n":["alexander von humboldt","humboldt"],"s":["Alexander von Humboldt was a German naturalist and explorer.","He lived from 1769 to 1859.","He explored Latin America from 1799 to 1804.","He saw nature as an interconnected whole.","More places and species are named after him than after almost anyone."]},{"n":["magna carta date","runnymede"],"s":["Runnymede is the meadow beside the River Thames where Magna Carta was sealed.","It happened in June 1215.","It lies near Windsor, west of London.","A memorial there was built by the American Bar Association in 1957.","A memorial to President Kennedy stands nearby."]},{"n":["irish famine","great famine","potato famine"],"s":["The Great Famine was a period of mass starvation in Ireland.","It lasted from 1845 to 1852.","A disease called potato blight destroyed the main food crop.","About a million people died.","Over a million emigrated.","Food was still exported from Ireland during the famine.","Ireland’s population has still not returned to its pre-famine level."]},{"n":["holodomor"],"s":["The Holodomor was a man-made famine in Soviet Ukraine.","It took place in 1932 and 1933.","Stalin’s collectivisation and grain seizures caused it.","Millions of Ukrainians died.","Its name means “death by hunger”.","Many countries recognise it as a genocide."]},{"n":["armenian genocide"],"s":["The Armenian genocide was the mass killing of Armenians by the Ottoman government.","It took place mainly in 1915 and 1916.","Around one million or more Armenians died.","Many died in death marches into the Syrian desert.","It is commemorated on 24 April."]},{"n":["apartheid south africa","apartheid laws"],"s":["Apartheid was a system of racial segregation in South Africa.","It was enforced from 1948 to the early 1990s.","People were classified by race.","Black South Africans were forced into separate areas and denied votes.","The Sharpeville massacre of 1960 drew world attention.","International sanctions and protests helped end it."]},{"n":["woodstock"],"s":["Woodstock was a famous music festival in New York State.","It took place in August 1969.","About 400,000 people attended.","Performers included Jimi Hendrix and Janis Joplin.","It became a symbol of the 1960s counterculture."]},{"n":["moon landing anniversary","first moonwalk"],"s":["The first moonwalk began on 20 July 1969 (21 July in UTC).","Neil Armstrong stepped out first, followed by Buzz Aldrin.","They collected about 22 kilograms of samples.","Their footprints are still there, as there is no wind on the Moon."]},{"n":["league of nations"],"s":["The League of Nations was an international organisation created after the First World War.","It was founded in 1920.","It aimed to prevent future wars.","The United States never joined.","It failed to stop aggression in the 1930s.","It was replaced by the United Nations in 1946."]},{"n":["prohibition"],"s":["Prohibition was a nationwide ban on alcohol in the United States.","It lasted from 1920 to 1933.","It led to illegal bars called speakeasies.","Gangsters like Al Capone grew rich smuggling alcohol.","It was repealed by the 21st Amendment."]},{"n":["al capone"],"s":["Al Capone was an American gangster in Chicago.","He lived from 1899 to 1947.","He grew rich during Prohibition.","He was linked to the St Valentine’s Day Massacre of 1929.","He was finally jailed for tax evasion.","He spent time in Alcatraz prison."]},{"n":["alcatraz"],"s":["Alcatraz is an island prison in San Francisco Bay.","It operated as a federal prison from 1934 to 1963.","It held notorious criminals like Al Capone.","In 1962 three men escaped and were never found.","It is now a museum."]},{"n":["jack the ripper"],"s":["Jack the Ripper was an unidentified serial killer in London.","The murders took place in 1888.","He killed at least five women in Whitechapel.","He was never caught.","Many suspects have been proposed."]},{"n":["roaring twenties","1920s"],"s":["The Roaring Twenties was a decade of prosperity and cultural change.","It refers to the 1920s, mainly in the US and Europe.","Jazz music became hugely popular.","Young women called flappers wore short dresses and bobbed hair.","It ended with the Wall Street Crash of 1929."]},{"n":["hippies","1960s","swinging sixties"],"s":["The 1960s was a decade of social upheaval and cultural change.","It ran from 1960 to 1969.","It saw the civil rights movement and protests against the Vietnam War.","The hippie counterculture rejected mainstream values.","London became a centre of fashion and music."]},{"n":["statue of zeus","colossus of rhodes"],"s":["The Colossus of Rhodes was a giant bronze statue of the sun god Helios.","It was completed around 280 BC.","It stood about 33 metres tall.","An earthquake toppled it in 226 BC.","It probably did not straddle the harbour, as often shown."]},{"n":["lighthouse of alexandria"],"s":["The Lighthouse of Alexandria was one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.","It was built in the 3rd century BC.","It may have been over 100 metres tall.","It stood on the island of Pharos.","Earthquakes destroyed it by the 14th century."]},{"n":["rosetta stone"],"s":["The Rosetta Stone is a stone slab that helped decipher Egyptian hieroglyphs.","It was carved in 196 BC and found in 1799.","It has the same text in hieroglyphs, Demotic and Greek.","French soldiers found it in Egypt.","Jean-François Champollion deciphered hieroglyphs in 1822.","It is in the British Museum."]},{"n":["dead sea scrolls"],"s":["The Dead Sea Scrolls are ancient Jewish manuscripts.","They were found from 1947 in caves near the Dead Sea.","They date from about the 3rd century BC to the 1st century AD.","They include the oldest known copies of Hebrew Bible texts.","A Bedouin shepherd found the first ones."]},{"n":["domesday book"],"s":["The Domesday Book is a great survey of England.","It was completed in 1086 for William the Conqueror.","It recorded land, people and animals.","It helped William assess taxes.","It is kept at the National Archives in London."]},{"n":["hadrian’s wall","hadrians wall"],"s":["Hadrian’s Wall is a Roman wall across northern England.","Building began in AD 122.","It was ordered by Emperor Hadrian.","It ran about 118 kilometres from coast to coast.","Forts and milecastles lined it.","Long stretches still stand."]},{"n":["cuneiform tablets","clay tablets"],"s":["Clay tablets were the main writing surface in ancient Mesopotamia.","They were used from about 3200 BC.","Scribes pressed wedge-shaped marks into wet clay.","Hundreds of thousands survive.","Some are school exercises and even complaints about bad copper."]},{"n":["hammurabi code examples"],"s":["Hammurabi’s Code set punishments based on social rank.","It dates from around 1754 BC.","A builder whose house collapsed and killed the owner could be executed.","It also set wages and prices."]},{"n":["trade unions","labour movement"],"s":["Trade unions are organisations of workers who bargain together.","They grew during the Industrial Revolution.","They campaigned for shorter hours and safer workplaces.","The eight-hour day was a major goal.","May Day honours workers in many countries."]},{"n":["women in science history"],"s":["Women made major scientific discoveries throughout history, often unrecognised.","Examples range from Hypatia in the 4th century to Marie Curie in the 20th.","Ada Lovelace wrote the first computer program.","Rosalind Franklin’s work revealed the shape of DNA.","Chien-Shiung Wu proved a key law of physics wrong in 1956.","Lise Meitner helped explain nuclear fission."]},{"n":["lise meitner"],"s":["Lise Meitner was an Austrian-Swedish physicist.","She lived from 1878 to 1968.","She helped discover nuclear fission.","She fled Nazi Germany in 1938.","Her colleague Otto Hahn alone received the Nobel Prize.","Element 109, meitnerium, is named after her."]},{"n":["chien-shiung wu","chien shiung wu"],"s":["Chien-Shiung Wu was a Chinese-American physicist.","She lived from 1912 to 1997.","Her 1956 experiment showed that nature does not always treat left and right the same.","She worked on the Manhattan Project.","She was called the “First Lady of Physics”."]},{"n":["sigmund freud","freud","psychoanalysis"],"s":["Sigmund Freud was an Austrian neurologist who founded psychoanalysis.","He lived from 1856 to 1939.","He believed unconscious desires shape behaviour.","He wrote The Interpretation of Dreams in 1899.","He described the id, ego and superego.","Many of his theories are now disputed, but his influence is huge.","He fled Nazi-occupied Vienna to London in 1938."]},{"n":["carl jung","jung"],"s":["Carl Jung was a Swiss psychiatrist who founded analytical psychology.","He lived from 1875 to 1961.","He was an early colleague of Freud before they split.","He described the “collective unconscious” and archetypes.","He introduced the ideas of introversion and extraversion."]},{"n":["ivan pavlov","pavlov"],"s":["Ivan Pavlov was a Russian physiologist famous for his experiments with dogs.","He lived from 1849 to 1936.","He showed dogs could learn to salivate at a signal linked to food.","This is called classical conditioning.","He won the Nobel Prize in 1904 for work on digestion."]},{"n":["john maynard keynes","keynes"],"s":["John Maynard Keynes was an influential British economist.","He lived from 1883 to 1946.","He argued governments should spend more during recessions.","His ideas shaped economic policy after the Great Depression.","He helped design the post-war Bretton Woods system."]},{"n":["mary shelley","frankenstein"],"s":["Mary Shelley was an English writer, author of Frankenstein.","She lived from 1797 to 1851; Frankenstein was published in 1818.","She began it at 18 during a ghost-story contest with Lord Byron.","It is often called the first science-fiction novel.","Her mother was the feminist writer Mary Wollstonecraft."]},{"n":["lord byron","byron"],"s":["Lord Byron was an English Romantic poet.","He lived from 1788 to 1824.","His works include Don Juan.","He was famous for scandal and was called “mad, bad and dangerous to know”.","He died while supporting Greek independence.","His daughter was Ada Lovelace."]},{"n":["h g wells","the time machine","war of the worlds"],"s":["H. G. Wells was an English writer and a pioneer of science fiction.","He lived from 1866 to 1946.","He wrote The Time Machine and The War of the Worlds.","A 1938 radio version of The War of the Worlds reportedly caused some panic.","He imagined tanks and atomic bombs before they existed."]},{"n":["jules verne"],"s":["Jules Verne was a French novelist and a father of science fiction.","He lived from 1828 to 1905.","He wrote Twenty Thousand Leagues Under the Sea and Around the World in Eighty Days.","He imagined submarines and moon journeys.","He is one of the most translated authors ever."]},{"n":["homer greek poet","sappho"],"s":["Sappho was an ancient Greek poet from the island of Lesbos.","She lived around 630 to 570 BC.","She wrote lyric poetry about love.","Only fragments of her work survive.","Plato reportedly called her “the tenth Muse”."]},{"n":["confucius sayings","analects"],"s":["The Analects is a collection of sayings of Confucius.","It was compiled by his followers after his death in 479 BC.","It covers virtue, learning and government.","A famous line: “Learning without thought is labour lost.”"]},{"n":["abacus"],"s":["The abacus is a counting tool using beads on rods.","Early forms were used in Mesopotamia; the bead abacus spread in China by the 2nd century BC.","It helped merchants calculate quickly.","It is still used to teach arithmetic in some countries."]},{"n":["zero","number zero","history of zero"],"s":["Zero as a number was developed in India.","The mathematician Brahmagupta set out rules for zero around AD 628.","The Babylonians and Maya used zero-like placeholders earlier.","It reached Europe through Arabic mathematicians.","It made modern mathematics possible."]},{"n":["money history","first coins","history of money"],"s":["Money developed to make trade easier.","The first coins were made in Lydia, in modern Turkey, around 600 BC.","Before coins, people bartered or used items like shells.","China introduced paper money around the 11th century.","Today most money exists digitally."]},{"n":["glass","history of glass"],"s":["Glass has been made by people for thousands of years.","The first glass objects appeared in Mesopotamia around 2500 BC.","Glassblowing was invented around the 1st century BC.","Venice became famous for glassmaking.","Glass lenses led to spectacles, telescopes and microscopes."]},{"n":["chocolate history","cacao"],"s":["Chocolate comes from the seeds of the cacao tree in the Americas.","Mesoamericans drank chocolate thousands of years ago.","The Aztecs used cacao beans as money.","Spain brought it to Europe in the 16th century.","Solid chocolate bars appeared in the 19th century."]},{"n":["tea history","history of tea"],"s":["Tea has been drunk in China for thousands of years.","Legend says Emperor Shennong discovered it around 2737 BC.","It spread to Japan and Korea.","It became hugely popular in Britain from the 17th century.","Tea taxes helped spark the Boston Tea Party."]},{"n":["coffee history","history of coffee"],"s":["Coffee originated in Ethiopia.","Coffee drinking spread in Yemen in the 15th century.","Coffee houses spread through the Ottoman Empire and Europe.","London coffee houses became centres of business and news.","Lloyd’s of London began in a coffee house."]},{"n":["nobel peace prize","peace prize"],"s":["The Nobel Peace Prize honours work for peace.","It was first awarded in 1901.","It is awarded in Oslo, Norway.","Winners include Martin Luther King Jr., Nelson Mandela and Malala Yousafzai.","Gandhi never won it."]},{"n":["red cross","henry dunant"],"s":["The Red Cross is a humanitarian movement that helps people in war and disaster.","It was founded in 1863.","Henry Dunant was inspired after seeing the wounded at the Battle of Solferino.","The Geneva Conventions protect wounded soldiers and civilians.","Dunant shared the first Nobel Peace Prize in 1901."]},{"n":["geneva conventions"],"s":["The Geneva Conventions are treaties setting rules for war.","The first was adopted in 1864; the main modern ones date from 1949.","They protect wounded soldiers, prisoners and civilians.","Nearly every country has signed them."]},{"n":["olympus mons mythology","underworld","hades"],"s":["Hades was the Greek god of the underworld.","His stories come from ancient Greek myth.","He was a brother of Zeus and Poseidon.","He ruled the dead with his wife, Persephone.","The three-headed dog Cerberus guarded his realm."]},{"n":["poseidon"],"s":["Poseidon was the Greek god of the sea, earthquakes and horses.","He was worshipped across ancient Greece.","He carried a three-pronged trident.","He was a brother of Zeus.","The Romans called him Neptune."]},{"n":["athena"],"s":["Athena was the Greek goddess of wisdom and war strategy.","She was the patron goddess of Athens.","She was born fully armed from Zeus’s head.","Her symbol was the owl.","The Parthenon was her temple."]},{"n":["apollo god"],"s":["Apollo was the Greek god of the sun, music, poetry and prophecy.","He was one of the twelve Olympians.","His oracle at Delphi was the most famous in Greece.","He was twin brother of Artemis.","NASA named its Moon program after him."]},{"n":["ulysses s grant","ulysses grant"],"s":["Ulysses S. Grant was the Union’s top general in the Civil War and the 18th US President.","He lived from 1822 to 1885 and was president from 1869 to 1877.","He accepted Lee’s surrender at Appomattox in 1865.","As president he fought the Ku Klux Klan.","He wrote acclaimed memoirs while dying of cancer.","He is on the US fifty-dollar bill."]},{"n":["robert e lee"],"s":["Robert E. Lee was the leading Confederate general in the American Civil War.","He lived from 1807 to 1870.","He commanded the Army of Northern Virginia.","He won battles like Chancellorsville but lost at Gettysburg.","He surrendered at Appomattox in April 1865.","Many statues of him have been removed in recent years."]},{"n":["james madison"],"s":["James Madison was the fourth US President, called the “Father of the Constitution”.","He lived from 1751 to 1836 and was president from 1809 to 1817.","He drafted much of the Constitution and the Bill of Rights.","He led the US in the War of 1812.","He was the shortest US president, at about 1.63 metres."]},{"n":["war of 1812"],"s":["The War of 1812 was fought between the United States and Britain.","It lasted from 1812 to 1815.","Causes included British interference with American shipping.","British troops burned Washington, including the White House, in 1814.","The US national anthem was written during a battle at Baltimore.","It ended in a stalemate."]},{"n":["white house"],"s":["The White House is the home and office of the US President.","It was first occupied in 1800.","John Adams was its first resident.","It was burned by the British in 1814 and rebuilt.","It has 132 rooms.","Theodore Roosevelt officially named it the White House in 1901."]},{"n":["star-spangled banner","us national anthem"],"s":["The Star-Spangled Banner is the US national anthem.","Francis Scott Key wrote the words in 1814.","He was inspired by the US flag still flying after the bombardment of Fort McHenry.","It became the official anthem in 1931."]},{"n":["london","history of london"],"s":["London is the capital of the United Kingdom.","The Romans founded it as Londinium around AD 47.","It was largely destroyed by fire in 1666.","It was bombed heavily in the Blitz of 1940 and 1941.","It was the world’s largest city in the 19th century.","It has hosted the Olympics three times: 1908, 1948 and 2012."]},{"n":["paris","history of paris"],"s":["Paris is the capital of France.","It began as a Celtic settlement of the Parisii tribe; the Romans called it Lutetia.","It was rebuilt with grand boulevards by Baron Haussmann in the 19th century.","It was occupied by Germany from 1940 to 1944.","It is famous for the Louvre, the world’s most visited museum."]},{"n":["new york","new york city","history of new york"],"s":["New York City is the largest city in the United States.","It was founded by the Dutch as New Amsterdam in 1624.","The English took it in 1664 and renamed it.","It was briefly the US capital.","Millions of immigrants arrived through Ellis Island.","It is a world centre of finance and culture."]},{"n":["jerusalem"],"s":["Jerusalem is one of the oldest cities in the world and holy to Jews, Christians and Muslims.","It has been inhabited for over 5,000 years.","King David made it his capital around 1000 BC.","The Western Wall is the holiest place where Jews can pray.","The Church of the Holy Sepulchre marks the traditional site of Jesus’s crucifixion.","The Dome of the Rock and al-Aqsa Mosque stand on the Temple Mount."]},{"n":["istanbul"],"s":["Istanbul is Turkey’s largest city, spanning Europe and Asia.","It was founded as Byzantium around 660 BC.","It was renamed Constantinople in 330.","The Ottomans captured it in 1453.","It was formally renamed Istanbul in 1930.","Hagia Sophia is its most famous building."]},{"n":["tokyo","edo"],"s":["Tokyo is the capital of Japan.","It was a small fishing village called Edo until the 17th century.","It became the shogun’s capital in 1603.","It was renamed Tokyo, “eastern capital”, in 1868.","It was devastated by an earthquake in 1923 and bombing in 1945.","Its metro area is the most populous in the world."]},{"n":["beijing"],"s":["Beijing is the capital of China.","It has been a capital for most of the last 800 years.","Kublai Khan made it his capital, called Khanbaliq.","It contains the Forbidden City and Tiananmen Square.","It hosted the Summer Olympics in 2008 and the Winter Olympics in 2022."]},{"n":["venice"],"s":["Venice is an Italian city built on more than 100 islands in a lagoon.","It was founded in the 5th century by people fleeing invaders.","It became a rich trading republic.","Its canals serve as streets.","It was ruled by elected leaders called doges for over 1,000 years.","Rising water and floods threaten it today."]},{"n":["troy archaeology","heinrich schliemann"],"s":["Heinrich Schliemann was a German businessman who excavated the site believed to be Troy.","He dug at Hisarlik, in Turkey, from 1871.","He found many layers of cities.","His methods damaged the site.","He also excavated Mycenae in Greece."]},{"n":["pompeii discovery","herculaneum"],"s":["Herculaneum was a Roman town buried by Vesuvius along with Pompeii.","It was buried in AD 79.","It was covered by deep volcanic mud and ash.","Even wooden furniture and food survived.","Charred scrolls found there are now being read using AI and X-rays."]},{"n":["easter rising"],"s":["The Easter Rising was an armed rebellion in Ireland against British rule.","It took place in Dublin in April 1916.","It was crushed within a week.","Fifteen leaders were executed.","The executions turned public opinion towards independence."]},{"n":["irish independence","michael collins irish"],"s":["Most of Ireland won independence from Britain after the Irish War of Independence.","The war lasted from 1919 to 1921.","Michael Collins was a key leader.","The Anglo-Irish Treaty created the Irish Free State in 1922.","A civil war followed over the treaty."]},{"n":["chinese new year","lunar new year"],"s":["Chinese New Year, or the Spring Festival, celebrates the start of the lunar year.","It has been celebrated for thousands of years.","Each year is linked to one of 12 animals of the zodiac.","Families gather for a reunion dinner.","Red decorations and fireworks are said to scare off bad luck."]},{"n":["christmas history"],"s":["Christmas celebrates the birth of Jesus.","It has been celebrated on 25 December since at least the 4th century.","Many traditions come from older winter festivals.","Christmas trees became popular in Britain through Queen Victoria’s German husband, Albert.","Santa Claus is partly based on Saint Nicholas."]},{"n":["halloween"],"s":["Halloween is a festival on 31 October.","It comes from the ancient Celtic festival of Samhain.","Celts believed the boundary with the dead was thin that night.","Irish and Scottish immigrants brought it to America.","Carved turnips came before pumpkins."]},{"n":["archimedes screw"],"s":["The Archimedes screw is a device for lifting water.","It is credited to Archimedes in the 3rd century BC, though it may be older.","It is a screw inside a tube, turned by hand or machine.","It is still used today for irrigation and in sewage plants.","Some modern versions generate electricity."]},{"n":["hagia sophia"],"s":["Hagia Sophia is a vast domed building in Istanbul.","It was completed in AD 537 under Emperor Justinian.","It was the world’s largest cathedral for nearly a thousand years.","It became a mosque in 1453.","It became a museum in 1934 and a mosque again in 2020.","Its dome seems to float on a ring of windows."]},{"n":["justinian"],"s":["Justinian I was a Byzantine emperor who tried to restore the Roman Empire.","He ruled from 527 to 565.","His generals reconquered Italy and North Africa.","He compiled Roman law into the Justinian Code.","He built Hagia Sophia.","His wife, Theodora, was a powerful empress."]},{"n":["theodora"],"s":["Theodora was a Byzantine empress and wife of Justinian.","She lived from about 497 to 548.","She came from humble origins as an actress.","During the Nika riots of 532 she reportedly persuaded Justinian not to flee.","She championed laws protecting women."]},{"n":["william shakespeare globe","globe theatre"],"s":["The Globe Theatre was the London playhouse where many of Shakespeare’s plays were performed.","It was built in 1599.","It burned down in 1613 when a cannon set the thatched roof alight.","It was rebuilt in 1614 and closed in 1642.","A reconstruction opened near the original site in 1997."]},{"n":["gladiators"],"s":["Gladiators were armed fighters who entertained audiences in ancient Rome.","Gladiator games began around 264 BC and ended in the 5th century AD.","Most were slaves, prisoners or criminals, though some volunteered.","They fought in different styles, with nets, tridents, swords and shields.","Not every fight ended in death.","Successful gladiators became celebrities."]},{"n":["roman legion","legionaries"],"s":["A Roman legion was the main unit of the Roman army.","Legions were used from the Republic through the Empire.","A legion had around 5,000 soldiers.","Soldiers carried a short sword (gladius) and a large shield (scutum).","They could form a “tortoise” formation with shields overlapping.","They built roads and forts as well as fighting."]},{"n":["knights","chivalry"],"s":["Knights were mounted warriors in medieval Europe.","They were prominent from about the 11th to the 15th century.","A boy trained as a page, then a squire, before being knighted.","Chivalry was a code of honour, courtesy and protecting the weak.","Tournaments and jousts let knights show their skill.","Gunpowder weapons made heavy armoured knights less important."]},{"n":["castles"],"s":["Castles were fortified homes built by medieval lords.","Most European castles were built between the 11th and 15th centuries.","Early ones were wooden motte-and-bailey castles.","Later ones had stone walls, towers and moats.","Cannons eventually made castles easy to breach.","Many were later turned into grand houses."]},{"n":["monasteries","monks"],"s":["Monasteries were communities of monks devoted to religious life.","Christian monasticism spread in Europe from the 6th century.","Saint Benedict wrote a famous rule for monks.","Monks copied books by hand, preserving ancient texts.","Monasteries ran schools, hospitals and farms.","Henry VIII dissolved England’s monasteries in the 1530s."]},{"n":["universities history","oldest university"],"s":["Universities developed in medieval Europe.","The University of Bologna, founded in 1088, is often called the oldest in continuous operation in the Western world.","Oxford was teaching by 1096.","The University of al-Qarawiyyin in Morocco was founded in 859.","Medieval students studied Latin, logic, law and theology."]},{"n":["knights templar","templars"],"s":["The Knights Templar were a Christian military order founded during the Crusades.","They were founded around 1119.","They protected pilgrims travelling to Jerusalem.","They became very rich and ran an early banking system.","The French king Philip IV had them arrested in 1307.","The order was dissolved in 1312, inspiring many legends."]},{"n":["inquisition","spanish inquisition"],"s":["The Spanish Inquisition was a tribunal to enforce Catholic orthodoxy in Spain.","It lasted from 1478 to 1834.","It targeted converted Jews and Muslims suspected of secretly keeping their faith.","It used torture and public trials called autos-da-fé.","Thousands were executed."]},{"n":["pilgrims","mayflower","thanksgiving"],"s":["The Pilgrims were English settlers who sailed to America on the Mayflower.","They landed at Plymouth, Massachusetts, in 1620.","They sought religious freedom.","About half died in the first winter.","The Wampanoag people helped them survive.","The harvest feast of 1621 is remembered as the first Thanksgiving."]},{"n":["jamestown"],"s":["Jamestown was the first permanent English settlement in North America.","It was founded in 1607, in Virginia.","Settlers struggled with disease and starvation.","Tobacco became its cash crop.","The first enslaved Africans in English North America arrived nearby in 1619."]},{"n":["emancipation proclamation"],"s":["The Emancipation Proclamation was an order by Abraham Lincoln to free enslaved people in Confederate states.","It took effect on 1 January 1863.","It applied only to areas in rebellion.","It allowed Black men to join the Union army.","Slavery was fully abolished by the 13th Amendment in 1865.","Juneteenth marks the day news of freedom reached Texas in 1865."]},{"n":["juneteenth"],"s":["Juneteenth commemorates the end of slavery in the United States.","It marks 19 June 1865.","That day, Union troops announced freedom to enslaved people in Galveston, Texas.","It became a US federal holiday in 2021."]},{"n":["ellis island"],"s":["Ellis Island was the main immigration station in New York.","It operated from 1892 to 1954.","About 12 million immigrants passed through it.","Inspectors checked arrivals for illness.","It is now a museum."]},{"n":["hoover dam"],"s":["The Hoover Dam is a giant concrete dam on the Colorado River.","It was built between 1931 and 1936.","It is on the border of Nevada and Arizona.","It created Lake Mead.","It generates hydroelectric power for millions."]},{"n":["empire state building"],"s":["The Empire State Building is a famous skyscraper in New York.","It was completed in 1931.","It was built in just over a year.","It was the world’s tallest building until 1970.","It is 381 metres tall to its roof, 443 metres with its antenna."]},{"n":["burj khalifa","tallest building"],"s":["The Burj Khalifa in Dubai is the tallest building in the world.","It opened in 2010.","It is about 828 metres tall.","It has over 160 storeys.","You can see it from nearly 100 kilometres away."]},{"n":["channel tunnel"],"s":["The Channel Tunnel links England and France under the sea.","It opened in 1994.","It is about 50 kilometres long.","Its undersea section is the longest in the world.","Trains carry passengers and cars through it."]},{"n":["apollo 13"],"s":["Apollo 13 was a Moon mission that had to be aborted after an explosion.","It launched on 11 April 1970.","An oxygen tank exploded on the way to the Moon.","Astronaut Jack Swigert reported, “Houston, we’ve had a problem.”","The crew used the lunar module as a lifeboat.","All three astronauts returned safely."]},{"n":["laika"],"s":["Laika was a Soviet dog, the first animal to orbit Earth.","She flew aboard Sputnik 2 on 3 November 1957.","She had been a stray in Moscow.","She died within hours from overheating.","Monuments to her stand in Russia."]},{"n":["neil armstrong biography"],"s":["Neil Armstrong was an American astronaut and the first person to walk on the Moon.","He lived from 1930 to 2012.","He was a naval aviator and test pilot.","He flew on Gemini 8 in 1966.","He was famously modest about his fame.","He later taught engineering."]},{"n":["tank man"],"s":["“Tank Man” is the unidentified man who stood in front of a column of tanks in Beijing.","It happened on 5 June 1989, the day after the Tiananmen crackdown.","He blocked the tanks for several minutes.","His identity and fate remain unknown.","The image is one of the most famous photographs ever."]},{"n":["watergate scandal"],"s":["Watergate was a political scandal that forced President Nixon to resign.","It began with a break-in at the Watergate building in June 1972.","Reporters Bob Woodward and Carl Bernstein investigated it.","Their secret source was nicknamed “Deep Throat”.","Tapes revealed Nixon tried to cover it up.","He resigned on 9 August 1974."]},{"n":["martin luther reformer"],"s":["Martin Luther was a German monk and theologian who started the Protestant Reformation.","He lived from 1483 to 1546.","He posted his Ninety-five Theses in Wittenberg in 1517, according to tradition on a church door.","He refused to recant at the Diet of Worms in 1521.","He married a former nun, Katharina von Bora."]},{"n":["john calvin","calvinism"],"s":["John Calvin was a French Protestant reformer.","He lived from 1509 to 1564.","He led the church in Geneva.","He taught predestination, the idea that God decides who will be saved.","His ideas spread to Scotland, the Netherlands and New England."]},{"n":["erasmus"],"s":["Erasmus was a Dutch Renaissance humanist scholar.","He lived from about 1466 to 1536.","He published a new Greek edition of the New Testament.","He criticised church corruption with satire.","The EU’s student exchange program is named after him."]},{"n":["magellan strait","cape of good hope","bartolomeu dias"],"s":["Bartolomeu Dias was the Portuguese explorer who first sailed around the Cape of Good Hope.","He rounded it in 1488.","He first named it the Cape of Storms.","King John II renamed it the Cape of Good Hope.","His voyage proved a sea route around Africa existed.","He later died in a storm near the same cape in 1500."]},{"n":["hernan cortes","cortes","cortés"],"s":["Hernán Cortés was the Spanish conquistador who conquered the Aztec Empire.","He lived from 1485 to 1547.","He landed in Mexico in 1519.","He allied with Indigenous enemies of the Aztecs, such as the Tlaxcalans.","His interpreter, Malintzin, was vital to his success.","Tenochtitlan fell in 1521."]},{"n":["francisco pizarro","pizarro"],"s":["Francisco Pizarro was the Spanish conquistador who conquered the Inca Empire.","He lived from about 1478 to 1541.","He captured the Inca emperor Atahualpa in 1532.","Atahualpa filled a room with gold as ransom but was executed anyway.","Pizarro founded Lima in 1535.","He was killed by rival Spaniards."]},{"n":["moctezuma","montezuma"],"s":["Moctezuma II was the Aztec emperor when the Spanish arrived.","He ruled from 1502 to 1520.","He welcomed Cortés into Tenochtitlan.","He was taken hostage by the Spanish.","He died in 1520 in disputed circumstances."]},{"n":["atahualpa"],"s":["Atahualpa was the last Inca emperor.","He lived from about 1502 to 1533.","He had just won a civil war against his brother when the Spanish arrived.","Pizarro captured him at Cajamarca in 1532.","He was executed in 1533."]},{"n":["leif erikson"],"s":["Leif Erikson was a Norse explorer who reached North America.","He arrived around the year 1000.","He was the son of Erik the Red, who founded settlements in Greenland.","He called the land Vinland.","A Norse site at L’Anse aux Meadows in Newfoundland confirms the visits."]},{"n":["erik the red"],"s":["Erik the Red was a Norse explorer who founded the first settlements in Greenland.","He lived from about 950 to 1003.","He was exiled from Iceland for killing several men.","He named Greenland to attract settlers.","His son Leif reached North America."]},{"n":["henry the navigator"],"s":["Prince Henry the Navigator sponsored Portuguese voyages of exploration.","He lived from 1394 to 1460.","He funded expeditions down the west coast of Africa.","Portuguese ships developed the caravel under his patronage.","He rarely sailed himself."]},{"n":["vasco nunez de balboa","balboa"],"s":["Vasco Núñez de Balboa was a Spanish explorer.","In 1513 he became the first European to see the Pacific from the Americas.","He crossed the Isthmus of Panama.","He claimed the ocean for Spain.","He was executed by a rival governor in 1519."]},{"n":["john cabot"],"s":["John Cabot was an Italian navigator who sailed for England.","He reached North America in 1497.","He probably landed in Newfoundland.","He was looking for a route to Asia.","He disappeared on a later voyage."]},{"n":["dr livingstone","david livingstone","stanley and livingstone"],"s":["David Livingstone was a Scottish missionary and explorer of Africa.","He lived from 1813 to 1873.","He was the first European to see Victoria Falls, in 1855.","He campaigned against the slave trade.","The journalist Henry Morton Stanley found him in 1871, supposedly saying, “Dr Livingstone, I presume?”"]},{"n":["robert falcon scott","scott of the antarctic"],"s":["Robert Falcon Scott was a British naval officer who led an expedition to the South Pole.","He reached the Pole on 17 January 1912.","He found that Amundsen had beaten him by five weeks.","He and his four companions died on the way back.","Lawrence Oates walked out into a blizzard, saying, “I am just going outside and may be some time.”"]},{"n":["matthew henson","north pole","robert peary"],"s":["Robert Peary and Matthew Henson led an expedition that claimed to reach the North Pole.","They claimed to reach it in April 1909.","Henson, an African American explorer, was vital to the expedition.","Some historians doubt they reached the exact Pole.","The first undisputed surface journey there came much later."]},{"n":["gilgamesh king","uruk"],"s":["Uruk was one of the first true cities in the world.","It flourished in southern Mesopotamia from about 4000 BC.","It may have had 40,000 to 80,000 people at its height.","Some of the earliest writing has been found there.","Its legendary king was Gilgamesh.","The name Iraq may derive from Uruk."]},{"n":["ur","royal tombs of ur"],"s":["Ur was an important Sumerian city in southern Mesopotamia.","It flourished in the 3rd millennium BC.","Its great ziggurat was built around 2100 BC.","Leonard Woolley excavated its royal tombs in the 1920s.","They held treasures like the Standard of Ur and a golden lyre."]},{"n":["catal huyuk","çatalhöyük","catalhoyuk"],"s":["Çatalhöyük was one of the world’s earliest large farming towns.","It was occupied from about 7100 to 5700 BC, in modern Turkey.","Houses were packed together with no streets.","People entered through holes in the roofs.","Walls were decorated with paintings of animals."]},{"n":["jericho"],"s":["Jericho is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world.","People have lived there for over 10,000 years.","It had a stone wall and tower by about 8000 BC.","It lies in the West Bank.","The biblical story says its walls fell to trumpets."]},{"n":["gobekli tepe"],"s":["Göbekli Tepe is an ancient stone monument in south-east Turkey.","It was built around 9500 BC, thousands of years before Stonehenge.","It has huge carved T-shaped pillars.","It was built by hunter-gatherers, surprising archaeologists.","It may be one of the world’s oldest temples."]},{"n":["terracotta warriors"],"s":["The Terracotta Warriors are life-sized clay soldiers buried with China’s first emperor.","They were made around 210 BC.","Each figure has a different face.","They were originally brightly painted.","Farmers found them in 1974 near Xi’an."]},{"n":["dunhuang","mogao caves"],"s":["The Mogao Caves near Dunhuang are a complex of Buddhist cave temples in China.","They were carved from the 4th to the 14th century.","They lie on the Silk Road.","A sealed library cave found in 1900 held tens of thousands of documents.","It included the Diamond Sutra, the oldest dated printed book, from 868."]},{"n":["diamond sutra"],"s":["The Diamond Sutra is a Buddhist text and the oldest known dated printed book.","This copy was printed in 868.","It was found in the Mogao Caves.","It is a scroll about five metres long.","It is kept in the British Library."]},{"n":["codex","codices","illuminated manuscripts"],"s":["Illuminated manuscripts are handwritten books decorated with gold, silver and colour.","They were made mostly in medieval Europe, from about 500 to 1500.","Monks often made them.","The Book of Kells, from around 800, is a famous example.","Printing made them rare."]},{"n":["book of kells"],"s":["The Book of Kells is a richly decorated manuscript of the Gospels.","It was made around AD 800.","It was probably created by monks on the island of Iona.","It is kept at Trinity College Dublin.","Its intricate decoration is world-famous."]},{"n":["wonders of the modern world","new seven wonders"],"s":["The New Seven Wonders of the World were chosen by a worldwide public vote.","The results were announced in 2007.","They are the Great Wall of China, Petra, Christ the Redeemer, Machu Picchu, Chichén Itzá, the Colosseum and the Taj Mahal.","The Great Pyramid was given honorary status.","Over 100 million votes were reportedly cast."]},{"n":["chichen itza"],"s":["Chichén Itzá is a large Maya city in Mexico’s Yucatán Peninsula.","It flourished from about AD 600 to 1200.","Its step pyramid, El Castillo, has 365 steps in total.","At the equinoxes, shadows look like a serpent sliding down the stairs.","It is one of the New Seven Wonders."]},{"n":["tikal"],"s":["Tikal was one of the largest cities of the Maya.","It flourished from about 200 to 900 AD, in modern Guatemala.","Its temples rise above the jungle canopy.","It may have had over 50,000 people.","It was abandoned in the 10th century."]}],"frame":{"intro_h":["Great question! Here’s a quick overview:","Happy to help! Here’s what I know:","Ooh, I love this one. So:","Sure thing! In short:","Good question, {player}! Here’s the gist:","Of course! Let me break it down:","Absolutely. Here are the key points:","I’ve read a fair bit about this! Briefly:","That’s a fascinating topic. Here goes:","Glad you asked! A quick summary:","Let me tell you about it!","Here’s a short answer:"],"intro_n":["Sure. Here’s the short version:","Okay, so:","Right. Quick summary:","I know a bit about that. Basically:","Here’s what I remember:","Short answer:","Alright, the gist is:","Let me think. Okay:","From what I’ve read:","Fine, history lesson incoming:","So, the basics:","Okay, here you go:"],"intro_v":["Fine. Since you clearly don’t know:","Pay attention, I’ll only say this once:","You don’t know this? Very well:","Knowledge is power. Here’s some, free of charge:","A history lesson. How quaint. Listen:","Hm. Even I know this one:","Very well. The facts:","Sit down. Here’s what matters:","Power remembers its history. So should you:","I’ll indulge you. Briefly:"],"more_h":["Want to know more? Just ask me to go on!","There’s more if you’re curious. Say “tell me more”.","Happy to dig deeper if you like!","I could keep going! Want more?","Anything specific you’d like to know about it?","Ask me “when”, “why” or “tell me more” for details!"],"more_n":["Want more? Say “go on”.","There’s more, if you care.","I can keep going if you want.","Ask if you want details.","That’s the short version, anyway."],"more_v":["Want more? Ask nicely.","There’s more. Earn it.","Say “go on” if your attention span allows.","I know more, of course.","Enough for now. Unless you insist."],"cont_h":["Sure! There’s more:","Of course! Here’s more:","Happy to! Going deeper:","Okay, more about it:","Here’s some more detail:"],"cont_n":["Okay, more:","Right, also:","Alright:","Fine, here’s more:","Also worth knowing:"],"cont_v":["Still listening? Good:","Very well, more:","Fine. Further:","You’re persistent. Here:","More, then:"],"end_h":["That’s about everything I know on that one! Ask me about something else?","I think I’ve told you all I know about it! What else are you curious about?","That’s the lot, I’m afraid. Want to try another topic?"],"end_n":["That’s all I’ve got on that.","Tapped out on that one. Ask something else.","That’s everything I know about it."],"end_v":["That’s all. Don’t ask again.","You’ve drained me on that subject. Something else.","Nothing more. Move on."],"unknown_h":["Hmm, I don’t have reliable information about {q}, and I’d rather not guess. Could you tell me more about it?","I’m honestly not sure about {q}. I don’t want to make something up! Is there something related I could help with?","That one’s outside what I know, I’m afraid. I don’t want to give you wrong facts about {q}.","I haven’t come across {q} before. If you tell me a bit about it, maybe I can help!"],"unknown_n":["No idea about {q}, honestly. I’d just be guessing.","Never heard of {q}. Not going to make stuff up.","Can’t help with {q}, sorry. Ask me about something else?","That’s beyond what I know. {q}? No clue."],"unknown_v":["{q}? I don’t know, and I won’t pretend to.","Ask someone who cares about {q}.","I deal in facts I’m sure of. {q} isn’t one of them.","Hm. {q} means nothing to me."],"when_lead":["As for when:","Timing-wise:","When? Well:","On the date:"],"why_lead":["Why does it matter? Well:","Its importance:","Why it’s remembered:","The big picture:"],"who_lead":["In short:","Simply put:","Basically:"],"topics_h":["I know quite a lot of history! Ask me about people like Napoleon, Cleopatra or Einstein, events like the Moon landing or the French Revolution, or places like the pyramids.","Try me on history: ancient Rome, the Renaissance, the World Wars, famous scientists, artists, explorers, inventions…"],"topics_n":["History, mostly. Ask me about a famous person, a war, an empire or an invention.","Try a name or an event. Caesar, Gandhi, the Titanic, whatever."],"topics_v":["History is the study of who won. Ask me about conquerors, empires, wars.","I know the stories of the powerful. Name one."]}};
